export const LIBRARY_BODIES_OPERATIONS: Record<string, string> = {
  "register-basics": "\n# Register Basics\n\nThis standard governs how a register session is opened, operated, and closed at the point of sale. It applies to cashiers, who open and close their own sessions and are accountable for the drawer during them, and to managers, who close a session left open by another cashier, approve cash movements that require a second person, and investigate variances. Every sale rung during a session carries that session's identifier, and the close-out figures are calculated from the tenders actually recorded in the session.\n\n## Register PIN\n\nEach staff member shall set a personal register PIN from their own staff profile before opening a register for the first time. A PIN is 4 to 8 digits, may be changed by its owner at any time, and is stored only in protected form; no staff member, including an administrator, can read another person's PIN back.\n\n1. A cashier with no PIN set cannot open a register. The register refuses the attempt and directs the cashier to set a PIN first.\n2. A cashier who enters an incorrect PIN cannot open the register. The register remains closed and nothing is partly opened.\n3. A cashier who has forgotten a PIN shall set a new one from their own profile. A forgotten PIN is replaced, not recovered.\n\n## Opening a shift\n\nOpening a shift is the point at which the cashier takes custody of the drawer. The cashier shall complete these steps in order:\n\n1. Sign in. The register resolves the sign-in to a staff profile. A person with no linked staff profile cannot use the register; a manager shall link the profile first.\n2. Select the physical register at which the cashier is standing, from the register list showing register number, name, store branch, and active status.\n3. Count the drawer. Count every bill and coin before ringing any sale. The count is the opening float, and it is the baseline against which the close-out is measured.\n4. Enter the float and the PIN. The register verifies the PIN first, then checks whether the register already has an open session.\n5. Begin selling only when the cashier's own session is open, stamped with the verified identity and the opening time.\n\nOnly one session may be open on a register at a time. If a previous session is still open, the register presents that session rather than opening a second one. The cashier shall not sell under another cashier's session. If the previous cashier has left, a manager shall close that session properly before the next cashier opens a new one.\n\nIf a cashier realizes during the shift that the opening float was never entered, the cashier shall tell a manager at once. The session is corrected with a documented cash movement; a silent correction at close is prohibited.\n\n## Cash movements during a shift\n\nCash may enter or leave the drawer outside a sale, for example a paid-out for emergency supplies. Every such movement shall be recorded at the time it occurs, with the amount, whether cash is entering or leaving, a reason in plain words, and the identity of the person recording it. A recorded movement adjusts the session's expected cash at once: cash in raises it, cash out lowers it.\n\nPaid-outs require a manager. Cash shall not be taken from the drawer for any purpose without a recorded movement. An unrecorded removal cannot be distinguished from a loss in any report the register produces.\n\n## Closing a shift\n\nThe cashier shall close the session in this order:\n\n1. Finish selling. Complete or clear any open cart. Do not close with a customer mid-tender.\n2. Run the session report first. The report states the opening float, every cash movement, every sale, the cash totals, and refunds, so the cashier knows what the drawer should hold before counting.\n3. Count the drawer in the same manner as at opening, and enter the counted total — the amount actually counted, not the amount the report expected.\n4. Read the variance. The register calculates variance as counted cash minus expected cash and records it on the session permanently.\n5. If the variance is over the threshold set by the store, call a manager before finishing the close.\n6. Sign out. A session left open after close causes the next cashier's sales to be recorded on the wrong session.\n\n## Controls\n\n- No shared register logins and no shared PINs. A cashier shall not share a PIN, use another person's PIN, or ask another person for theirs. Every action taken under a PIN is attributed to its owner in the audit trail.\n- A cashier who suspects a PIN is known to another person shall change it immediately.\n- A counted total shall never be adjusted to match the expected total. The variance is a measurement to be investigated, not a figure to be corrected at entry.\n- When variance exceeds the store threshold, the manager's investigation follows a fixed order: re-count the cash; review the shift's voids and refunds; check for sales rung to the wrong tender; review cash movements. Only then is the close accepted.\n- If the drawer does not open for change, the cashier shall trigger the release again from the register; the drawer release fires only on cash and check tenders. If it still does not open, the cashier shall call a manager and shall not leave cash unsecured on the counter while troubleshooting.\n\n## Records\n\nEach session creates a session record showing opening cash, expected cash, counted cash, variance, opening and closing times, and who opened and closed it. Each cash movement creates a movement record showing direction, amount, reason, and who recorded it. Session records, movement records, and the sales recorded under a session are kept in the point of sale system and are the records used for reconciliation and for the reports described in Reporting and Quick Access (§4.1.12).\n\n## Escalation and review\n\nManagers review variances over threshold, unresolved open sessions, and cash movements without an adequate reason. A register, PIN, or session fault that the store cannot resolve shall be reported to support@unsaidmemories.com. Legal process concerning a register record shall be directed to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> System faults at the register: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal process concerning a register record: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "offline-mode": "\n# Offline Mode\n\nThis standard governs conduct at the point of sale when the connection is lost. It applies to cashiers, who ring and hold carts during an outage, and to managers, who direct any exception and reconcile the drawer afterward. The governing fact is plain: the register does not sell offline. Every sale completion — the sale itself, every card path, every gift card tender, and every coupon validation — requires a connection. Cash cannot complete either, because the sale record, the accounting entry, the inventory movement, and the duplicate-submission protection are all recorded by the system at completion. Staff shall not invent a workflow the system does not have.\n\n## The offline indicator\n\nThe register always shows its connectivity state — quiet when online, an unmistakable banner when offline. The banner states the whole rule: the cart is saved locally; sales cannot complete until the connection returns; there is no offline queue. Staff shall trust it literally. While the banner is shown:\n\n- Items may still be rung into the cart. The cart is saved locally and survives a refresh.\n- No tender may be taken: card payments are impossible, cash cannot complete, gift card balances cannot be checked, and coupons cannot be validated.\n- Customer lookup for records not already shown will not resolve.\n\nThe cashier shall tell the customer plainly and early: the system is down, everything can be rung up, but payment cannot be taken until the connection returns.\n\n## Procedure during an outage\n\n1. Keep ringing. Build the cart normally — items, quantities, and discounts the cashier can state with confidence. The cart is saved locally.\n2. Do not take money. Not cash, not card, and not payment held against a promise to ring it later. An unrecorded cash tender is the most dangerous event at a register: the customer believes they paid, the drawer holds money with no sale attached, and reconciliation becomes a matter of memory. If the customer must leave, hold the cart and take their name and number — but not their money.\n3. Keep waiting carts separate by customer name. Do not start a second outage cart in confusion with the first.\n4. Watch the indicator, not hope. When the banner clears, the connection is back. Tender the waiting carts oldest first, verify each total before tendering — the cart holds ring-time prices, which may have changed while waiting — and confirm each sale completes normally before starting the next.\n5. Reconcile before close. Verify that outage carts tendered after reconnecting appear in the day's transaction log. Do not close a shift with carts still waiting; an untendered cart is not a sale, and the drawer shall hold no money for one.\n\n## What the register does not do\n\nThere is no queued-transaction store, so there is no first-in-first-out synchronization when the connection returns; there is nothing queued to synchronize. There is no stored card capture for later authorization, so there is no scenario in which a card declines after the customer has left. Any person who states that the register queues offline sales is describing a different system. Providing a true offline mode is a building decision for the Company, not a cashier decision; until it exists, the procedure above is the whole of offline operation. Staff shall not improvise a paper-based substitute system without a manager's explicit direction.\n\n## The interrupted card authorization\n\nA customer whose card was mid-authorization when the connection dropped may have a pending charge recorded by the payment provider without a completed sale. When the connection returns, staff shall not re-tender blindly. Check the pending-charge state first — still waiting, or completed — as described in Troubleshooting (§4.1.13). This is the one outage scenario in which money may have moved without a sale.\n\n## Controls\n\n- No money shall be taken while the banner is shown, for any tender type.\n- If cash was handed over as the connection dropped, the cashier shall hand it back, explain, and hold the cart. The sale shall not be completed.\n- If the browser closes or crashes during an outage, reopen the register page; the locally saved cart should restore. If it does not, the ring-up is lost and shall be rung again, which is why staff shall keep each customer's physical goods grouped separately during an outage.\n- If the connection flaps — up, down, up — each tender attempt needs a live connection at the moment of completion. A sale that appears to complete during a flap shall be verified in the transaction log before the customer leaves.\n\n## Records\n\nCarts rung during an outage are saved locally on the register only; they create no sale, payment, or inventory record until tendered after reconnecting. Sales completed after reconnecting create the normal sale, payment, and receipt records described in Payment (§4.1.6) and Receipt (§4.1.7), and appear in the day's transaction log under Reporting and Quick Access (§4.1.12).\n\n## Escalation and review\n\nManagers review outage carts, any money offered during an outage, and any interrupted card authorization before the shift closes. A connection or register fault that the store cannot resolve shall be reported to support@unsaidmemories.com. Legal process concerning a sale record shall be directed to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> System faults during an outage: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal process concerning a sale record: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "hardware-integration": "\n# Hardware Integration\n\nThis standard governs the devices where the register meets the physical sale: the card reader, barcode scanner, receipt printer, cash drawer, customer-facing display, and scale. It applies to cashiers, who check device health, perform first-line repairs, and sell around a failed device, and to managers, who replace failing hardware and report faults the store cannot fix. A device failure during a rush is a customer-service event first; staff shall keep the sale moving with the workarounds stated here and repair the device between customers.\n\n## Card reader\n\nThe reader takes tap, chip, and swipe payments and hands the register a tender recorded with its authorization code. A healthy reader shows its ready state — steady light, and \"ready\" on the register's reader status — before the first card of the day. Pairing is the usual failure: Bluetooth readers drop pairing and USB cables get kicked loose. Re-pairing or reseating takes under a minute and resolves the large majority of \"reader not found\" errors. Staff shall keep wireless units charged — they fail mid-shift with no warning but a dimming light — and keep the chip slot clean; a dirty slot misreads good cards and produces a decline that is really a read failure. While the reader is down, the store keeps selling: cash, gift card, store credit, payment link, and manager-approved manual card entry all bypass the reader, as described in Payment (§4.1.6). Staff shall know where the backup reader is before it is needed.\n\n## Barcode scanner\n\nThe scanner turns a barcode into an item lookup — one beep, one product, at its catalog price. The two failure modes are a dead scanner (no beep, no light; check power and cable first, then swap the unit) and a live scanner that finds nothing (it beeps, but no product appears; the barcode is not on the item record — fall back to name search and report the missing barcode to a manager, as described in Product Lookup (§4.1.2)). Scanners read through plastic wrap and light smudges but not cracked housings or heavy frost; wipe and retry before declaring a barcode bad. A scanner that double-adds is reading too sensitively; the cashier shall check the cart quantity after every scan during a rush.\n\n## Receipt printer\n\nThe printer turns the completed sale into paper: header, lines, totals, tenders, authorization codes, footer, and cut. Its health is visible — paper loaded, no jam light, test print clean. Staff shall change the roll before it runs out mid-receipt. Faded printing that a new roll does not fix means the thermal head is failing, and its receipts may be unreadable by return time; flag it for replacement. A jammed printer does not stop the sale, because the transaction is already recorded: clear the jam, reload, and reprint from the sale record under Receipt (§4.1.7). Staff shall never re-ring a sale because the receipt failed.\n\n## Cash drawer\n\nThe drawer opens for a cash sale's change, for a manager-approved no-sale opening, and for nothing else. The opening signal is electrical and travels with the receipt output, which is why the drawer opens on cash and check tenders and stays shut on card tenders. If the drawer fails to open on a cash sale, trigger the release again from the register; if it still does not open, call a manager rather than leaving cash unsecured on the counter. Every opening is recorded as a cash movement with a reason, under Register Basics (§4.1.1). At close, counted cash reconciles against expected cash: opening float, plus cash kept from sales, minus cash refunds and paid-outs. A drawer that will not lock at close is a stay-until-it-is-fixed situation.\n\n## Customer-facing display\n\nThe second screen faces the customer: each item as added, the running total, and at tender time the amount due, with tip options where applicable. Its purpose is trust — the customer watches the sale being built and can catch a duplicated item or wrong variant before payment. If the display is dark or frozen, the sale may continue; the cashier shall read the total aloud instead, and the display shall be fixed before the next rush. Staff shall never show the customer the full register screen as a substitute; it exposes other customers' data, discount controls, and functions the customer shall not see. Customer data handling is governed by the Privacy Policy (§2.1.2).\n\n## Scale\n\nWhere the store sells weighed goods, the scale feeds the quantity directly: the goods are placed, the weight appears, and the price is computed from the per-unit price. Staff shall zero the scale with nothing on it before weighing, and re-zero it if it drifts; a scale reading 0.02 while empty adds that amount to every weighing. If the register's weight plainly disagrees with the scale's display, the connection has dropped: re-zero and re-weigh before overriding manually. Scales are legal-for-trade devices in many places; the certification sticker shall be current. An expired certification is not a technicality; it is the difference between a lawful sale and a fine.\n\n## Controls\n\n- If the drawer opens on every tender including cards, the opening signal is firing incorrectly. This is a system fault; report it and do not wedge the drawer.\n- If the printer prints the sale but the drawer does not open on cash, reprinting will not fix it, because the same output is produced again. The sale is sound; the drawer needs its manual release through a manager, and the fault shall be reported.\n- If the scanner reads but the register adds the wrong product, two items share a barcode. Staff shall not correct it by deleting and re-adding, which returns the same wrong product. Ring the item by SKU search and report the collision.\n\n## Records\n\nDevice events are recorded through the records they serve: card tenders with authorization codes on payment records, scans as sale lines, receipts as receipt records, and drawer openings as cash movements. Faults reported by the store shall identify the device, the register, and the sale affected, where one exists.\n\n## Escalation and review\n\nManagers review repeated device failures and arrange replacement hardware. A hardware or connection fault that the store cannot resolve shall be reported to support@unsaidmemories.com. Legal process concerning a sale or a scale certification matter shall be directed to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Hardware faults the store cannot resolve: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal process concerning a sale record: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "reporting-and-quick-access": "\n# Reporting and Quick Access\n\nThis standard governs the working reports at the point of sale: today's sales, the transaction log, the session report, voids and refunds, and the end-of-day summary. It applies to cashiers and shift leads, who read these reports during the day and reconcile the drawer against the session report, and to managers, who sign the end-of-day summary and correct records the reports expose as wrong. These are the views that answer \"how are we doing\" and \"what went wrong\" while there is still time to act. Every figure traces to a specific sale, payment, or refund record; nothing is estimated.\n\n## Today's sales\n\nThis view shows every completed sale today: number, time, total, tender breakdown, and receipt number. Use it to answer the mid-shift question of whether the store is on track, and to surface problems early. A sale count that looks low for the foot traffic usually means sales are being rung under the wrong source. A revenue figure that looks wrong usually traces to one large sale; find it, open it, and check for a misapplied discount or void. The tender totals — cash against card — are calculated separately from the sale totals so that a sale paid with two tenders is counted once, not twice. The tender split on this report may be relied on for that reason.\n\n## Transaction log\n\nThe log is the complete, unfiltered list of everything the registers did today — sales, voids, refunds, no-sale drawer openings, and manager overrides — newest first, each row carrying its tenders in readable form, such as \"Cash + Visa.\" Where today's sales is the scoreboard, the log is the play-by-play. Use it when something needs explaining: the drawer is short, a customer disputes a charge, a discount looks unauthorized. Each entry links to its full record. The log is append-only; entries are never edited or deleted, only corrected by new entries. If a transaction that is known to have happened cannot be found, check the filters first — wrong register, wrong date, voids hidden — before concluding it is missing.\n\n## Session report — the close-out tool\n\nThis report is what the drawer is reconciled against. For the open session it shows the session itself (opening float, expected cash, who opened it, and when), every cash movement in order, the sale count and total, the cash-sales total, and the refunds total. The cashier shall run it before counting the drawer, not after; the report states what the count should find, and counting first anchors the cashier to a number they then defend. Expected cash is calculated, not estimated: opening float, plus the net cash each sale kept (cash tendered minus change given back), minus cash refunds, plus or minus recorded cash movements. The close-out procedure itself is stated in Register Basics (§4.1.1).\n\nWhen the report and the count disagree, investigate in this fixed order:\n\n1. Re-count the cash. Most variances end here.\n2. Review the shift's voids and refunds.\n3. Check for sales rung to the wrong tender. A cash sale recorded as card is the classic case: the drawer holds the cash and the report does not expect it.\n4. Review cash movements for missing reasons or missing entries.\n5. Only then escalate to a manager.\n\n## Voids and refunds\n\nThis view shows the money that went backward: voided sales with timestamps and reasons, and refunds with amounts, reasons, and methods, separated by method because cash refunds remove money from the drawer with no card trail and deserve extra attention. Staff shall watch for pattern, not volume: a spike in voids on one shift, refunds clustering around one product (a possible defective batch), and void reasons that say nothing, such as \"other\" or blank. Every void and refund shall carry a reason that makes sense in six months, as required by Post-Transaction (§4.1.8). If the cash-refund total for a shift looks high, reconcile it against the physical cash before the shift closes, not after.\n\n## End of day\n\nThe day's final accounting is built from the session reports: total sales, tender splits, discounts given, tax collected, refunds and voids, and expected cash per drawer. It is generated from the day's records, not typed by hand, which is why the records must be right. The manager shall sign the summary when it balances. The signature states that the day's money is accounted for.\n\n## Controls\n\n- If the session report shows sales the cashier did not ring, the cashier is looking at another session on the same register: the previous shift's session was never closed and has been inherited. Stop, get a manager, close it properly, and open a new session. Staff shall not keep selling into another cashier's session.\n- If the tender split does not match the drawer, a sale was rung to the wrong tender type. Find it in the transaction log, where tenders are shown per row, and have a manager correct the record. Staff shall not \"fix\" it with an offsetting cash movement.\n- The refunds view sums completed refunds linked to the session's sales. A refund on a sale from a previous session appears in its own session's report, not the current one.\n\n## Records\n\nThe reports are generated from the session, sale, payment, refund, void, and cash movement records kept in the point of sale system. The signed end-of-day summary is the store's record that the day's money was reconciled.\n\n## Escalation and review\n\nManagers review variances, void and refund patterns, and unsigned or unbalanced end-of-day summaries. A reporting fault that the store cannot resolve shall be reported to support@unsaidmemories.com. Legal process concerning a sale record shall be directed to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> System faults in register reporting: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal process concerning a sale record: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "pos-troubleshooting": "\n# POS Troubleshooting\n\nThis standard governs how register failures are handled at the point of sale. It applies to cashiers, who work each failure in the order stated, and to managers, who take over payments whose status cannot be verified and faults the store cannot fix. The rule for every failure is the same: protect the sale record first, fix the problem second. A sale recorded correctly can survive any hardware failure; a sale never recorded properly cannot be reconstructed from memory.\n\n## The two-minute rule\n\nIf any failure is not resolved in two minutes with a line forming, stop troubleshooting and tender around the problem: another tender type, another register, or a held cart. The line is the priority; the broken item is fixed between customers. The workarounds in this standard — manual card entry, payment link, cash — exist so a failure is never the customer's problem for longer than it has to be.\n\n## Card reader not connecting\n\nThe register reports the reader not found, or the reader's light is dark. Work the chain from the device inward:\n\n1. Check power. Wireless units fail silently; check the charge light.\n2. Check pairing. Bluetooth readers drop pairing; USB cables get kicked loose. Re-pair or reseat, then check the register's reader status.\n3. If the register sees the reader but cards still fail, clean the chip slot. A dirty slot misreads good cards and produces declines that look like customer problems.\n4. If nothing works, switch tenders, not registers: cash, gift card, store credit, payment link, and manager-approved manual card entry all complete the sale without the reader. Record the dead reader for repair and take the backup reader.\n\n## Receipt printer offline\n\nThe sale is complete; only the paper is missing. Check paper, check for a jam — open the cover and remove the torn fragment, because the small piece left behind causes the next jam — and check power and cable. While the printer is down, offer email or text receipts from the sale record under Receipt (§4.1.7). Never re-ring a sale because the receipt did not print; reprint from the completed sale. Re-ringing creates a second sale, a second charge, and a reconciliation problem. Faded print that a fresh roll does not fix means the thermal head is failing; flag it for replacement.\n\n## Transaction stuck — the screen froze mid-sale\n\nDetermine what actually happened before touching anything. The register protects against double submission of the same attempt: one attempt carries one submission key, and completed results are remembered for 24 hours. That protection covers the same submission completing twice, such as a double click. It does not cover a new tender attempt after a freeze, which carries a new key. Therefore:\n\n1. Check the payment's status first. A payment with a succeeded status and an authorization code is a completed charge even if the screen is frozen; restart the register application and the completed sale will be present.\n2. If the payment did not complete — no authorization code, status uncertain — do not assume it failed and re-tender. Check whether the customer's bank shows a pending charge; if so, treat it as paid and reconcile rather than charging twice.\n3. When in doubt, call a manager before tendering a second time. A double charge is fixable by refund, but it is always the register operator's fault in the customer's eyes.\n\n## Card payment stuck in \"waiting\"\n\nFor keyed, link, and on-file card payments, the pending charge moves through waiting for payment, completing, and completed, with failed, expired, and cancelled as end states. If a payment shows as waiting, give it time: the provider's confirmation and the register's own confirmation can arrive in either order, and the single-completion control means exactly one of them finishes it. If it settles to failed, read the recorded error — decline reason or amount mismatch — and act on what it says. If it sits waiting past its expiry, it is expired; the money was never confirmed, and re-tendering is safe. Staff shall never tender a second card payment while the first is still waiting or completing.\n\n## Card charging unavailable\n\nWhen the register states that card charging is unavailable, this is not a retry situation; there is no key to find. Switch tenders — cash, check, gift card, or a manual card record still work — and report it. Manual card entry, with manager approval, is the bridge: the card was charged on a separate terminal and the tender is recorded, as described in Payment (§4.1.6).\n\n## Amount mismatch on a card payment\n\nIf the provider confirms a different amount than the pending charge, the payment is deliberately not recorded against the sale: the money moved with the provider, but the books refuse to attach a mismatched amount to the sale. Do not re-tender and do not void blindly. Call a manager: the provider payment must be located and either matched or refunded through the provider, and the sale completed against the correct amount.\n\n## Offline banner will not clear\n\nThe register never queues sales, so there is no synchronization to wait for; \"waiting for the return of queued sales\" is not a state that resolves itself. Confirm the connection is genuinely back by the indicator. If the banner stays up, the register still has no connection and no sale can complete until it does, under Offline Mode (§4.1.10). Do not close the shift with customers waiting on held carts; an untendered cart is not a sale. If the indicator flaps, verify each tendered sale in the transaction log before the customer leaves.\n\n## Manager override rejected\n\nA rejected override is almost never a system error; it is the system working as designed. The usual causes are that the account's role does not carry override authority for the action, the staff record is inactive, or the PIN was mistyped. It does not mean \"try another manager's credentials until one works\"; the audit log records every attempt. If the override is genuinely needed and no credential is accepted, the action waits. An override that cannot be obtained means the action needs more authority than is present, which is what overrides are for.\n\n## Looking up online orders\n\nWhen a customer references a website order at the register, for a return or a pickup, use the sale and order search in the administrative screens. Do not type the order identifier into other search boxes. Online orders store their identifiers and money amounts in a different format from register sales, so hand-typed lookups can fail in confusing ways. If a lookup fails, re-check what was typed, then use the administrative search rather than guessing at the format.\n\n## Controls\n\n- No second tender shall be attempted until the first payment's status has been verified.\n- No sale shall be re-rung to solve a printer, reader, or screen failure.\n- Rejected override attempts shall not be followed by attempts with other managers' credentials.\n\n## Records\n\nEach failure that touches money leaves its record in the ordinary course: payment status, processor transaction identifier, and authorization code on the payment record; pending-charge state and recorded errors on the pending charge; void, refund, and override entries in the transaction log. Troubleshooting shall not delete or alter these records; corrections are made by new entries.\n\n## Escalation and review\n\nManagers review unverified payments, amount mismatches, and repeated device failures. A fault that the store cannot resolve shall be reported to support@unsaidmemories.com, identifying the register, the device or function affected, and the sale involved. Legal process concerning a sale or payment record shall be directed to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> System faults the store cannot resolve: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal process concerning a sale or payment record: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "product-lookup": "\n# Product Lookup\n\nThis standard governs how staff find and ring items at the point of sale. It applies to cashiers, who search for and add items, and to managers, who correct missing prices and catalog flags. The register searches the same catalog the business operates from; it shows only items flagged for register sale, priced from the retail price list. The item type determines what happens when the sale completes, so the cashier shall confirm the type before tendering.\n\n## The three item types\n\n- **Product** — a physical good. Its price comes from the retail price list, and completing the sale reduces inventory.\n- **Service** — a groom, bath, or add-on, shown with an SVC- code. Its price comes from the service catalog's default price. Completing the sale reduces no inventory.\n- **Subscription** — a plan, such as a Bath Club tier or the Shipping Club. Completing the sale creates a subscription record for the customer; it is an enrollment in recurring billing, not a one-time sale. The cashier shall tell the customer, before tendering, that the purchase starts a recurring membership, and the customer shall be attached to the sale as described in Cart (§4.1.3).\n\n## Search by name\n\n1. Type what the customer asks for, even approximately. Search covers names, brands, and descriptions together.\n2. When several matches appear, read the full product name and variant detail before selecting. Similar names across brands are the most common source of price complaints at pickup.\n3. Confirm the price shown is the retail list price and is usable before adding the item.\n\n## Search by SKU\n\nUse SKU search when the customer has a receipt, a website order, or a shelf label. One SKU identifies one product at one price and removes ambiguity between similar names. If a SKU search returns nothing, check for mistyped characters first — 0 for O and 1 for I are the usual errors. If it still returns nothing, the item is likely inactive or not flagged for register sale; discontinued items remain in the records but cannot be sold. A manager shall confirm the item's status.\n\n## Barcode scanner\n\nThe scanner reads the barcode recorded for the SKU and behaves as a SKU search. If the scanner beeps and no product appears, the barcode was likely never loaded onto the SKU record. The cashier shall fall back to name search, add the item, and report the missing barcode to a manager. If the scanner double-adds — one beep, two lines — the cashier shall check the cart quantity after every scan during a rush.\n\n## Category tabs\n\nThe tabs present the live category structure — Dog, Cat, Fish, Bird, Reptile, and Small Animal, plus Services and Subscriptions sections. Tabs are for browsing when a customer wants to compare, not for finding a known item. Because the tabs follow the live category structure, a reorganized category appears reorganized at the register; when a customer states a product was previously under a different category, search by name.\n\n## Stock figures\n\nThe stock figure beside a product is the main warehouse on-hand quantity. It is information, not a block: a sale that exceeds available stock still completes, and the system flags the shortfall for the daily report. A cashier who sells past the figure shown shall tell the customer honestly about fulfillment timing rather than promising stock the shelf does not hold.\n\n## Controls\n\n- An item showing $0.00 shall not be sold. For a product, $0.00 means the retail price list has no price for it; for a service, it means the service catalog has no default price. The cashier shall stop, park the sale, and have a manager correct the price before tendering. Completing the sale would write a zero-dollar line into the books.\n- An item visible on the website but not at the register is not flagged for register sale, or is inactive. Only a manager may change the flag; the cashier shall not work around it.\n- If a subscription was rung as a one-time product, the cashier shall void the line before payment and re-add the subscription item. After payment, the correction is a refund and a new sale, handled under Post-Transaction (§4.1.8).\n\n## Records\n\nEach item added creates a sale line carrying its description, quantity, and unit price, as described in Cart (§4.1.3). Sales past available stock create a stock alert for the daily report. Missing prices and missing barcodes reported to a manager are corrected in the catalog, not at the register.\n\n## Escalation and review\n\nManagers review zero-price items, missing barcodes, and stock alerts, and correct the catalog entry that caused them. A lookup or catalog fault that the store cannot resolve shall be reported to support@unsaidmemories.com. Legal process concerning a sale record shall be directed to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> System faults in product lookup: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal process concerning a sale record: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "cart": "\n# Cart\n\nThis standard governs how the cart is built and handled at the point of sale, from the first line added until payment completes. It applies to cashiers, who build the cart, attach the customer, and apply discounts, and to managers, who review overrides and discounts. Nothing in the cart is final until payment completes, but the cart determines what the books will record: the receipt reads these lines, a refund references them, the accounting entry posts from them, and inventory is reduced from them. Staff shall treat the cart as the record being written, not as scratch work.\n\n## Adding items and lines\n\nEach product rung creates one line, numbered in order, carrying the description, quantity, and unit price exactly as the catalog holds them. Adding the same product again increases the quantity on the existing line and recomputes its totals; it does not create a second line. A refund is later processed against that line's quantity. Each line also carries the item's cost, which the accounting entry uses for cost of goods. If a price looks wrong, the cashier shall check whether the catalog price changed or the wrong variant was selected before using an override, which is governed by Keypad (§4.1.4).\n\nThe three line kinds behave differently at completion:\n\n- A **product** line reduces inventory at the main warehouse.\n- A **service** line reduces nothing, but may carry an appointment link.\n- A **subscription** line creates a live subscription record at completion — plan, customer, pet, billing interval, and next billing date. The cashier shall state before tendering that the line starts a membership, and the line requires a customer attached.\n\n## Attaching the customer\n\nThe cashier shall attach the customer before payment, not after. Attaching links the sale to the customer's record for purchase history, loyalty, and gift card ownership. Three checks happen at tender time and are skipped silently if no customer is attached: charging a card on file, using a gift card or store credit belonging to the customer, and enrolling a subscription.\n\nIf the customer is new, create the record with at least a name and phone number, following Customers (§4.2.2). If the customer declines, ring the sale with no customer attached. Staff shall never attach one customer's sale to another customer's record; doing so corrupts two histories and places loyalty points in the wrong account with no clean correction.\n\n## Checking out a booking and taking deposits\n\nWhen a sale is linked to a grooming appointment, completing the sale also checks the customer out of the booking: the appointment is marked completed, its paid and outstanding amounts settle, any held booking deposit is applied and the customer-deposits liability is relieved, and the groomer's commission accrues. The cashier shall confirm the appointment shown is the correct one before tendering; completing the wrong appointment marks the wrong visit complete and accrues commission to the wrong groomer.\n\nA deposit collected without a booking — a special-order prepay or a hold on a large item — is recorded as a deposit: numbered, marked held, linked to the customer, with the sale noted in its notes. A deposit is the customer's money held by the store, not revenue; the books carry it as a liability until it is applied or refunded. The cashier shall tell the customer plainly what the deposit covers and what happens if the customer does not return.\n\n## Line discounts and coupons\n\nA discount on one line is stored on that line as its own discount figure, separate from the unit price; the original price remains visible. A coupon code is validated at tender time — active, in date, uses remaining, promotion window, and minimum subtotal on eligible lines. An invalid coupon does not produce a smaller discount; it stops the sale entirely, with the reason stated, as described in Order Summary (§4.1.5). Staff shall never enter a manual discount to match a rejected coupon. If no coupon is entered, the register checks for automatic promotions and applies the single best one.\n\n## Order notes\n\nNotes record what the receipt and the next shift need to know, such as a pickup arrangement or an approved substitution. The cashier shall write notes as if the reader has no other context. Staff shall not enter anything in a note they would not show the customer.\n\n## Holding, clearing, and voiding before payment\n\nHolding parks the entire cart — lines, discounts, customer, and notes — so another customer can be served. A held cart is not a sale: no payment is taken and no inventory is committed. Held carts have no permanent storage. Staff shall retrieve and complete held carts promptly, shall not hold a cart and leave for the day, and shall read the customer name before confirming a retrieved cart, every time.\n\nClearing empties the cart with no record; it is for a false start before payment. Once payment is taken, clearing is no longer available. A paid sale is corrected only through the post-transaction flow — void or refund — under Post-Transaction (§4.1.8), so the audit trail stays intact.\n\n## Controls\n\n- Price overrides require a reason, as governed by Keypad (§4.1.4). An override without a reason cannot be distinguished from an error in the audit trail.\n- A subscription line without a customer attached shall not be tendered; the sale will fail or create a membership belonging to no one.\n- A held cart left from a previous shift has stale prices and its customer may be gone. Staff shall clear it rather than tendering an abandoned cart.\n\n## Records\n\nEach completed sale creates sale lines carrying line number, item, service where applicable, description, quantity, unit price, gross amount, discount amount, taxable amount, tax amount, line total, and cost. Subscription lines create subscription records; appointment-linked sales update the appointment and deposit records; standalone deposits create numbered held deposit records. These records are kept in the point of sale system.\n\n## Escalation and review\n\nManagers review overrides without reasons, manual discounts entered in place of coupons, and appointment-linked sales completed against the wrong appointment. A cart fault that the store cannot resolve shall be reported to support@unsaidmemories.com. Legal process concerning a sale record shall be directed to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> System faults in the cart: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal process concerning a sale record: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "keypad": "\n# Keypad\n\nThis standard governs use of the register keypad: entering quantities, overriding a price, obtaining a manager override, and opening the drawer without a sale. It applies to cashiers, who enter quantities and request overrides, and to managers, who authorize overrides with their own register PIN. Every keypad change to a quantity or a price is recorded on the sale line permanently, so each change shall be made deliberately and for a stated reason.\n\n## Entering quantity\n\nAfter selecting a product, the cashier enters the number on the keypad and confirms; the line's quantity updates and its totals are recomputed from the unit price. The keypad always acts on the currently highlighted line, so the cashier shall look at the highlight before typing. If the quantity is entered and the line total does not change, the entry was confirmed with no product selected.\n\nFor weighed goods, the keypad takes the weight from the scale where one is configured. Where no scale is configured, the cashier shall enter the quantity the scale shows and double-check the unit; pounds and ounces shall not be confused.\n\n## Price override\n\nA price override changes the unit price on a line. The register records the new price permanently on the line record; the original catalog price remains recoverable from the item record. Overrides exist for real situations only: a shelf tag that disagrees with the system, a manager-approved price match, or a damaged package sold at a markdown.\n\n1. Confirm the catalog price is actually wrong or the exception actually applies before overriding.\n2. Enter the override with a reason.\n3. If the catalog price is wrong, report it so the catalog is corrected. Overriding at every sale leaves the next cashier facing the same wrong price.\n\n## Manager override and the PIN\n\nSome actions require authority a cashier does not hold: large discounts, price overrides above the store threshold, voids after payment, and no-sale drawer openings. When the register asks for a manager override, the manager shall enter their own register PIN themselves. The action is recorded under the manager's name alongside the cashier's, and the register then returns to the cashier. The PIN standard is stated in Register Basics (§4.1.1): each PIN is personal, 4 to 8 digits, and stored only in protected form, so a forgotten PIN is replaced, not recovered.\n\nThe override is an attestation that the exception is legitimate, not a formality. If no manager is on the floor, the action waits.\n\n## Clear and confirm\n\nThe clear key erases an entry before it is committed — a half-entered quantity, a wrong price, a miskeyed PIN — and changes nothing in the sale. Confirm commits the entry to the line. The cashier shall build this habit: type, read the display, then confirm. The display echoes exactly what the register understood, and a single keystroke can change 6 to 60. If a wrong entry has already been confirmed, the cashier shall correct it with the proper correction — adjust the quantity, or redo the override — rather than stacking another entry on top, so the line's history reads cleanly.\n\n## Opening the drawer without a sale\n\nOpening the drawer without a sale — to make change, to count, or for a paid-out — is a manager action. It is recorded as a cash movement with a reason, under Register Basics (§4.1.1). A cashier who needs the drawer open and is not a manager shall get a manager. Every drawer opening is on the record.\n\n## Controls\n\n- No shared PINs and no borrowed credentials. A cashier shall not borrow a manager's PIN for any override, and a manager shall not give a PIN to a cashier to type. A PIN used by someone else no longer identifies the person who acted.\n- A price shall never be overridden downward to make change come out even or to resolve a short drawer.\n- An override authorizes the action as presented. If the cart changes materially after an override — new lines, a different tender — the cashier shall obtain the override again.\n- If a manager override is rejected, the usual causes are that the account's role does not carry override authority for the action, the staff record is inactive, or the PIN was mistyped. Staff shall not try another manager's PIN until one works. Every attempt is recorded in the audit log, and shopping for credentials is itself subject to discipline.\n- A cashier who overrode a price and later learns the catalog was correct shall tell a manager. The correction is a documented adjustment, not a quiet re-edit, because the original entry is already part of the sale's history.\n\n## Records\n\nQuantity and price changes are recorded on the sale lines they affect. Manager overrides are recorded with the manager's identity alongside the cashier's. No-sale drawer openings are recorded as cash movements with a reason. These records are kept in the point of sale system and reviewed under Reporting and Quick Access (§4.1.12).\n\n## Escalation and review\n\nManagers review overrides, rejected override attempts, and no-sale openings as part of shift review. A keypad or override fault that the store cannot resolve shall be reported to support@unsaidmemories.com. Legal process concerning a sale record shall be directed to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> System faults in the keypad or overrides: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal process concerning a sale record: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "order-summary": "\n# Order Summary\n\nThis standard governs how the order summary is read and checked at the point of sale: subtotal, discounts, taxes, tip, and grand total. It applies to cashiers, who shall verify the summary before tendering, and to managers, who investigate totals that cannot be explained from the cart. Every figure in the summary is stored on the sale record permanently. The summary is what accounting reads, what the end-of-day report sums, and what a refund references. When a customer questions a total, the cashier shall walk the summary from top to bottom with the customer; the arithmetic is all visible.\n\n## Subtotal\n\nThe subtotal is the sum of the lines before anything is taken off: quantity times unit price, added up, with no discounts removed. It answers what the goods are worth. Line discounts are summed separately, and anything above the line-discount sum is the order-level discount. The subtotal never includes tax, tip, or fees. If the subtotal looks wrong, the error is in the cart — a wrong quantity, a duplicated line, or the wrong variant. Fix the lines and the subtotal corrects itself.\n\n## Discounts\n\nThe discount total is everything taken off: line markdowns, any sale-level discount, and the coupon or automatic promotion. Each coupon or promotion discount is recorded as its own discount record linked to the promotion and coupon; a manual discount is recorded as a plain register discount. That distinction is how a manager later tells a promotion from a hand-entered markdown. Discounts apply before tax is computed, so a discount on a taxable item reduces the taxable amount.\n\nTwo coupon rules apply at the register:\n\n- A coupon that fails validation — expired, not yet valid, usage limit reached, minimum not met, or no promotion attached — stops the entire sale, with the reason stated. It never completes with a silent smaller discount.\n- Buy-one-get-one coupons are not redeemable at the register.\n\nIf no coupon is entered, the register applies the single best active automatic promotion on its own. If the discount total is zero but a promotion was expected, the usual causes are an expired coupon or a minimum subtotal the cart did not meet.\n\n## Taxes\n\nThe tax total is computed from the taxable amount of each line after discounts. The rates are the store's own tax rules, never a general constant. A rule marked goods-only applies to product lines; every other automatic rule applies to the whole taxable base. The sale writes one tax record per jurisdiction — jurisdiction, rate, taxable amount, and tax amount — adjusted so the records sum exactly to the collected tax, which is what allows a tax return to be filed from the records.\n\nA sale with no tax on a cart full of taxable goods usually means the items were rung as non-taxable service lines by mistake. A tax-exempt customer shall have the exemption verified before the sale completes; removing tax after payment requires a refund and a corrected new sale. When in doubt, charge the tax and let a manager adjust. Under-collected tax is the store's liability.\n\n## Tip\n\nTips are recorded on the payment, not the sale, because a tip belongs to the specific tender it was added to. The register offers tip choices at card tender time. Cash tips handed directly to staff are not recorded by the register and shall not be entered as sale tips. For card-on-file and pending-charge completions, the tip is computed as the sale's tip minus any tips already recorded on prior tenders, so a split tender does not double-count a tip. A tip entered in error may be corrected before the payment is finalized; after finalization, correction requires a manager, because the authorized card amount changes. Tips are never taxed and never discounted.\n\n## The accounting entry behind the summary\n\nEvery completed sale posts a balanced accounting entry. The debits record the payment, cost of goods, and discounts; the credits record revenue at the gross subtotal, sales tax payable, and inventory relieved. If the entry does not balance exactly, the entire sale stops: no partial sale and no unbalanced books. Staff will not see this in normal operation; it is the control that makes the summary trustworthy.\n\n## Grand total\n\nThe grand total is the amount the customer pays: subtotal, minus discounts, plus tax. It is the figure the payment screen starts from, the figure printed largest on the receipt, and the figure a refund references. After payment, the register records what was actually paid and, for cash, the change given back. The cashier shall read the total aloud before tendering, every time; it is the last point at which an error is cheap to correct.\n\n## The pre-tender check\n\nBefore accepting the first tender, the cashier shall run this check:\n\n1. Read the item count against the goods on the counter. Duplicated lines and wrong variants are found here.\n2. Read the customer name at the top. The wrong attached customer misattributes loyalty, gift cards, and subscriptions.\n3. Read the discount lines. Every markdown shall have a reason the cashier can state aloud.\n4. Read the tax. Zero tax on taxable goods is a mis-rung line, not a bargain.\n5. Read the grand total aloud to the customer. The customer's agreement is the last cheap checkpoint.\n\n## Controls\n\n- Staff shall never enter a manual discount to match a rejected coupon. Doing so creates an unaccounted markdown the promotion report cannot explain, and the coupon's usage count remains uncounted.\n- If a coupon is rejected and the customer objects, the cashier shall read the exact reason shown, offer an automatic promotion if one applied, or complete the sale without the coupon.\n- If the tax total changed between a quote and tender, a line was edited or a discount changed the taxable base. The cashier shall re-walk the summary; the tax follows the lines.\n- If a sale is reported as failed because its accounting entry did not balance, the money did not move and the cart is intact. The cashier shall call a manager; this is a system problem, not a cashier problem.\n\n## Records\n\nThe sale record keeps the subtotal, the discount records (each linked to its promotion or coupon, or marked as a register discount), one tax record per jurisdiction, the tip on the payment record, the amount paid, and any change due. These records are kept in the point of sale system.\n\n## Escalation and review\n\nManagers review manual discounts, rejected coupons disputed by customers, and tax figures that cannot be explained from the lines. A summary or accounting-entry fault that the store cannot resolve shall be reported to support@unsaidmemories.com. Legal process concerning a sale record shall be directed to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> System faults in the order summary: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal process concerning a sale record: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "payment": "\n# Payment\n\nThis standard governs how payment is taken at the point of sale: card, cash, gift card, store credit, check, and split payment. It applies to cashiers, who take each tender, and to managers, who approve manual card records and resolve payments whose status cannot be verified. Until a tender is accepted, everything in the sale is editable. The moment a tender is accepted, the sale becomes a financial record: a payment with its own number, linked to the sale, stamped with who took it and how.\n\n## Card — the four paths\n\nA card payment at the register may complete in four ways. The cashier shall choose the path that fits the situation:\n\n1. **Keyed** — the card is typed into the register. The sale completes when the register confirms the charge.\n2. **Link** — the customer pays on a payment page. The register shows a link the cashier can display, copy, or email to the address on file. Use this path when the card needs the customer's phone approval, or for a phone order.\n3. **On file** — the customer's saved card is charged. This path requires the customer attached to the sale; with no customer attached, the register refuses. If the bank requires authentication, the register reports it rather than failing silently, and the cashier shall use the link path so the customer can approve on their phone. If the card declines, the register gives the decline reason; offer another path rather than retrying the same card repeatedly.\n4. **Manual** — recorded only. The card was charged elsewhere, such as a phone terminal, and the cashier records the tender. Manager approval is required, and the entry shall be made exactly, because the register trusts it completely.\n\n## What happens when a card is charged\n\nThe keyed, link, and on-file paths begin the same way: the register creates a pending charge — a record of everything needed to finish the sale: amount, customer, and the full cart. When the payment provider confirms the money, the sale completes through the same sale-completion path as a counter-paid sale; there is one completion path, and every card payment uses it. Completion claims the pending charge so that exactly one completion — the register's confirmation or the provider's notification — finishes it. If it is already completed, the second stands down and nothing happens twice.\n\nIf the confirmed amount does not match the pending amount, completion is refused and the payment is not recorded against a sale; the mismatch is surfaced, not covered over. If the sale fails after the card was charged, the pending charge returns to waiting, with the error recorded, so a retry or the provider's notification can still complete the recording. Confirmed money shall never be left silently unrecorded.\n\n## When card charging is unavailable\n\nIf no payment provider key is configured for the register, card charging is unavailable and the register says so plainly: cash, check, gift card, or a manual card record still work. The register does not pretend to charge a card and does not hold a card charge for later. Staff shall know the fallback tenders before this message appears.\n\n## Saved cards\n\nCards on file are held by the payment provider against the customer's provider record, reached from the customer record. No card numbers are stored in the store's own records. Saved cards are listed from the provider showing brand, last four digits, and expiry, and a new card is saved to the provider record when the customer chooses to save it during a keyed or link payment. Handling of customer payment data is also governed by the Privacy Policy (§2.1.2).\n\n## Cash\n\nCash is the only tender for which the register calculates change: amount tendered minus total equals change due, recorded on the sale. The cashier shall count the cash before it enters the drawer, enter the amount tendered as counted — not as the total — and let the register state the change. The cashier shall announce the change while handing it over and count it back into the customer's hand. Large bills receive a counterfeit check under store policy. If wrong change is discovered after the customer leaves, the cashier shall not adjust the sale; the cashier shall report it to a manager at close. Cash sales update the session's expected cash automatically, by cash tendered minus change given back.\n\n## Gift card — the hard rules\n\nA gift card tender draws down the card's stored balance. The register enforces three rules without exception, and breaching any one stops the entire sale, with a full rollback rather than a partial completion:\n\n1. The card must exist. A mistyped number is \"not found,\" not a zero balance.\n2. The card must be redeemable — active or partially redeemed. An expired, voided, or never-activated card cannot be tendered.\n3. The balance must cover the tendered amount. The register will not reduce an over-tender to the balance on its own; tendering $50 against a $30 balance does not complete the sale.\n\nEvery redemption writes a ledger entry with the balance before and after, so the card's history can be audited to the cent. When the balance reaches zero, the card is marked redeemed. The cashier shall check the balance before tendering, and shall tender gift cards before cash, because a gift card cannot make change.\n\n## Store credit and check\n\nStore credit works like a gift card but belongs to a customer account rather than a card number. The customer shall be attached first; the register shows the available balance and applies it on confirmation. Partial use is normal; the remainder stays for next time.\n\nA check is recorded with its check number as the reference. The drawer release fires for checks as for cash. Store policy governs acceptance — identification, limits, and no third-party checks. A check is a promise, not a guarantee; the cashier shall know the store's rule on large checks before the customer writes one.\n\n## Split payment\n\nEach tender becomes its own payment against the same sale, and the paid total is the sum. The register limits card charges to the remaining balance; cash may overpay and generate change. Tender in this order: gift cards and store credit first, because they cannot make change; then cards; then cash. If a split goes wrong midway — first tender accepted, second declined — the cashier shall not void the whole sale. The accepted tender stands; void or refund it individually and re-tender the remainder.\n\n## Card-decline recovery\n\n1. Do not retry the same card immediately. Repeated rapid declines can cause the customer's bank to lock the card.\n2. Read the message. An authentication-required result on a card-on-file charge means: send a payment link and let the customer approve on their phone.\n3. Offer the next path — another card keyed, the link, or cash — framed as routine.\n4. If the register shows the payment as succeeded with an authorization code and the customer says they were not charged, trust the register. A succeeded payment with an authorization code is a completed charge; the customer's banking application may not show it yet.\n5. Never re-tender a card payment whose status has not been verified. An uncertain payment shall be checked — payment status and pending-charge state — before a second attempt.\n\n## Reader status\n\nThe card reader's status light is the first diagnostic. A steady ready light means paired and listening; no light means power or pairing; a blinking fault light means attention is needed. If the register reports the reader not found while the light looks correct, re-pair a Bluetooth reader or reseat a USB reader. While the reader is down, cash, gift card, store credit, and manager-approved manual card entry still work.\n\n## Controls\n\n- A card purchase shall never be refunded as cash; refunds follow Post-Transaction (§4.1.8) and the Returns and Refunds Policy (§2.1.19).\n- Manual card records require manager approval.\n- Staff shall not re-tender a payment whose status is waiting, completing, or otherwise unverified.\n\n## Records\n\nEach tender creates a payment record with its own payment number, linked to the sale, showing method, amount, tip where applicable, and who took it. Card payments create pending-charge records that move through waiting, completing, and completed, with failed, expired, and cancelled as end states. Gift card redemptions create ledger entries with balances before and after. These records are kept in the point of sale system.\n\n## Escalation and review\n\nManagers review manual card records, amount mismatches, and payments that cannot be verified. A payment fault that the store cannot resolve shall be reported to support@unsaidmemories.com. Legal process concerning a payment record shall be directed to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> System faults in payment: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal process concerning a payment record: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "receipt": "\n# Receipt\n\nThis standard governs receipts at the point of sale: what a receipt contains, the print, email, and text options, the customer's right to decline, and reprints. It applies to cashiers, who shall offer the receipt choice on every sale, and to managers, who correct misdirected receipts and replace failing printer hardware. The receipt is the sale's proof of what was bought, what was paid, and how, and it is the document every return, dispute, and warranty question starts from.\n\n## What the register generates\n\nWhen a sale completes, the system creates a stored receipt record — receipt number, sale link, and delivery method — and printer output for the thermal printer. For cash or check tenders, the printer output ends with the drawer-release command, which is why the drawer opens on a cash sale and stays shut on a card sale. The printed receipt carries the store block, the receipt and sale numbers, the date, every line as quantity and description with its line total, subtotal, discounts, tax, the total, each tender with its amount, and change. Card payments show the authorization code and the card brand with last four digits — never the full card number.\n\n## Print receipt\n\nPrint is the default and the option that needs no contact details and no connectivity. If the printer jams mid-receipt, the cashier shall reprint from the completed sale record; the cashier shall never re-ring the sale. Reprinting produces the same receipt, not a second sale. Staff shall keep the printer loaded; a register with no paper cannot prove a sale until the roll is changed. If print quality fades and a fresh roll does not fix it, the thermal head is failing and shall be flagged for replacement before it produces receipts a customer cannot read at return time.\n\n## Email receipt\n\nAn emailed receipt goes to the address on the customer's record. If the customer is attached to the sale, the address is already present; if not, the cashier shall ask for it and confirm the spelling before sending. A receipt sent to the wrong address is a privacy incident, not only a typo, because it discloses another person's purchase; handling of customer contact data is governed by the Privacy Policy (§2.1.2). The email contains the same detail as the printout, plus links for returns and support. Email receipts are transactional: they are sent because the customer bought something, regardless of marketing opt-in status. If the customer says the receipt never arrived, check the address on the record first, then resend; spam filters catch more receipts than the mail system loses.\n\n## Text receipt\n\nA text receipt goes to the phone number on the customer's record and is the briefest form: store name, sale number, total, and a link to the full receipt. The cashier shall confirm the number verbally rather than relying on an old record. Like email, it is transactional — a customer who opted out of marketing still receives receipts — but a customer who asked for no contact at all shall be offered print instead.\n\n## No receipt\n\nA customer may decline all three forms. The sale is still fully recorded; the register does not need the customer's permission to keep its own books. The cashier shall warn the customer once, in plain terms, that without a receipt a return requires looking up the sale by date, amount, or card, which takes minutes instead of seconds and is not always possible for a cash sale with no customer attached. Staff shall always offer the choice; \"no receipt\" shall be the customer's choice, not the cashier's shortcut during a rush.\n\n## Reprint\n\nAny completed sale can produce its receipt again, because the sale record is permanent and the receipt always renders from it:\n\n1. Find the sale — by its sale number (shown on the customer's bank statement), by date and amount, or by the card used.\n2. Open it and choose reprint or resend. This does not alter the sale: no new payment, no new totals, no duplicate revenue.\n3. If the receipt must go somewhere new, such as a different email than the one on file, update the customer record first, then send. An address typed only into the send dialog is not stored, and the next reprint would go to the old address again.\n\n## Controls\n\n- A sale shall never be re-rung because a receipt failed. Reprint from the sale record.\n- If a receipt is emailed to the wrong address, the cashier shall correct the customer record immediately and resend. If the receipt contained another customer's details because the wrong customer was attached, the cashier shall report it as a privacy incident to a manager at once.\n- A request for a receipt for a sale from weeks ago is routine. The record is permanent; find it and reprint it.\n\n## Records\n\nEach sale creates a receipt record with its receipt number, the linked sale, and the delivery method chosen. The sale, line, and payment records the receipt renders from are kept in the point of sale system permanently.\n\n## Escalation and review\n\nManagers review misdirected receipts and failing printer hardware. A receipt fault that the store cannot resolve shall be reported to support@unsaidmemories.com. Legal process concerning a receipt or a privacy incident shall be directed to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> System faults with receipts: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal process or a privacy incident concerning a receipt: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "post-transaction": "\n# Post-Transaction\n\nThis standard governs what happens after payment at the point of sale: starting the next sale cleanly, processing refunds and returns at the register, and voiding a paid sale. It applies to cashiers, who start each new sale and process refunds within their authority, and to managers, who approve refunds above the store threshold and reconcile refunds against closed sessions. Refunds at the register are carried out under the Returns and Refunds Policy (§2.1.19), which governs the customer's rights; this standard governs the register procedure.\n\n## Starting a new sale\n\nStarting a new sale closes the loop on the last one: the completed sale keeps its number, lines, payments, and receipt choice, and the register returns to an empty cart with no customer attached. The cashier shall make the reset deliberate, in this order: receipt offered, sale closed, new sale started, then greet the next customer. Ringing the next customer's items onto the previous customer's sale — usually because no new sale was started and the previous customer is still attached — is the most common register error in a busy store and misattributes loyalty points. If the register shows a cart with items the cashier did not add, the cashier shall stop. If the cart is unpaid, clear it; if it is paid, call a manager before touching it.\n\n## Refund and return at the register\n\nA refund reverses money for goods coming back. It is a financial event in its own right, never an edit to the original sale. The original sale is history; it is never updated and never deleted. The cashier shall process a refund in this order:\n\n1. **Find the original sale first** — by receipt number, date and amount, or the card used. The refund links to the sale and, where applicable, to the original payment. A refund without a linked sale is not permitted.\n2. **State the reason** in plain words plus a reason code — \"defective, seam split on first wash,\" not \"return.\" The reason drives defect reporting; a miscoded reason corrupts those figures silently.\n3. **Choose lines or the full sale.** For a partial return, name the lines and quantities being returned and whether each goes back to stock. For a full refund, the register builds the refund lines from the original sale automatically; every line comes back into stock, recorded line by line.\n4. **Choose the refund method.** The method shall match how the customer paid: cash back for cash, card reversal for card. The register enforces this by default. Store credit is the alternative the customer may choose; it is placed on the customer's account for next time.\n5. **Obtain manager approval where required.** Above the store threshold, the refund waits for a manager's review and approval before money moves. A refused refund is recorded as well; the refusal is also an auditable answer.\n6. **Complete the refund.** The system writes the refund record, marked completed; a reversal sale with negative totals, with subtotal, discount, and tax scaled by the refunded share so reports that sum reversal entries stay correct; restock movements for flagged lines; and the reversal accounting entry, debiting revenue and crediting cash or the bank account. A cash refund reduces the session's expected cash at once.\n\n## Void and refund — after payment\n\nIf a customer pays and immediately says they do not want the purchase, the cashier shall void the sale while it is still the current transaction rather than running a refund. A void stamps the sale with the void time and reason and reverses its payments in one step. Void is for a sale that should not have happened; refund is for a sale that happened and is now being unwound. The distinction matters in the reports: voids show the sale was a mistake, refunds show the customer returned goods.\n\n## Controls\n\n- Cumulative refunds shall never exceed the original sale total. The system enforces this cap and refuses the excess, stating the amount already refunded. A refusal usually means a previous refund on the same sale was forgotten; look it up before disputing the register.\n- A card reversal goes back to the same card, always. Staff shall refuse, politely, a request for cash back on a card purchase, and offer the card reversal or store credit. Cash back on a card sale is a fraud risk, and the register does not do it by default for that reason.\n- A damaged line shall be marked as not going back to stock, with its disposition noted. Damaged goods return to the shelf in the system only if the restock flag is set incorrectly; the inventory count is only as honest as the restock flags.\n- A refund on a sale from a closed session still processes, because the money movement is real, but the drawer adjustment applies only while the original session is open. A cash refund against a closed session requires a manager's cash movement to reconcile the current drawer.\n\n## Records\n\nEach refund creates a refund record with its own number and completed status, a reversal sale with negative totals, refund lines with restock flags, restock movements for flagged lines, a reversal accounting entry, and, where approval was required, the manager's approval identity and time. Voids stamp the sale with void time and reason. These records are kept in the point of sale system.\n\n## Escalation and review\n\nManagers review refunds above threshold, refused refunds, and refunds against closed sessions. Disputes about whether a return is permitted are governed by the Returns and Refunds Policy (§2.1.19). A refund fault that the store cannot resolve shall be reported to support@unsaidmemories.com. Legal process concerning a refund shall be directed to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> System faults in refunds or voids: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal process concerning a refund: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "modals-and-overlays": "\n# Modals and Overlays\n\nThis standard governs the register prompts that interrupt a sale for a decision the register cannot make alone: discount and coupon, customer search, tip selection, age verification, manager override, refund reason, held orders, and receipt preview. It applies to cashiers, who answer each prompt, and to managers, who provide overrides. Each prompt appears at the moment the information is needed, and each answer writes something permanent. Staff shall not dismiss a prompt carelessly; dismissing one incorrectly is how sales end up wrong in ways that take a manager to fix.\n\n## Discount and coupon\n\nThis prompt appears when the sale is discounted or a coupon code is entered. It asks the kind (percent or fixed), the amount, and why; the reason becomes the discount's reason on the record, separating a legitimate promotion from an unexplained markdown in the reports. Coupons are validated first, strictly: the code must exist, be active, be within its dates, have remaining uses, have a promotion attached, and meet the minimum subtotal against the eligible lines. A rejected coupon names the failed rule; the cashier shall read the message instead of overriding around it. An invalid coupon stops the entire sale until the coupon situation is resolved. Staff shall never enter a manual discount to match an expired coupon. If no coupon is entered, the register applies the single best automatic promotion on its own; the cashier shall check the discount lines so they can be explained to the customer. Discount rules are stated in full in Order Summary (§4.1.5).\n\n## Customer search\n\nThis prompt attaches the customer to the sale, searching customer records by name, phone, and email, with partial matches allowed. Phone number is usually the fastest key. When several records match, the cashier shall check the details — address, pet names, last visit — before choosing. Choosing the wrong person merges two people's histories and loyalty balances, and separating them again requires a support request. If no record exists, create one with at least a name and phone number. A sale with no customer attached cannot earn loyalty, cannot receive a digital receipt, cannot charge a card on file, and cannot enroll a subscription. If the customer declines, the cashier shall say so plainly at the time, so the next person who sees the sale understands why no customer is attached. Customer records are governed by Customers (§4.2.2), and customer data handling by the Privacy Policy (§2.1.2).\n\n## Tip selection\n\nFor card payments where tipping applies — grooming services most often — the register presents tip choices after the total is shown: preset percentages, preset amounts, custom entry, and no tip. The customer chooses, not the cashier. Staff shall never select a tip on the customer's behalf, shall never suggest an amount beyond showing the screen, and shall never present the tip screen again after a decline. The register records the choice once, stored on the payment itself, so a split tender keeps each tip with its own card. On card-on-file completions the tip is computed net of tips already recorded on prior tenders, so it cannot double-count. A tip entered by mistake before finalization may be changed; after finalization, correction requires a manager, because the authorized card amount changes.\n\n## Age verification\n\nCertain products require the customer to be of legal age, and the register stops the sale until age is verified. When the prompt appears, the cashier shall:\n\n1. Check a valid photo identification for the buyer.\n2. Enter the birth date exactly as shown; the register calculates the age against today's date.\n3. Complete the sale only if verification passes. A failed verification removes the restricted item; the rest of the cart proceeds.\n\nStaff shall not estimate an age, shall not take the customer's word, and shall not accept a companion's identification in place of the buyer's. An expired identification is not valid identification; treat it as no identification.\n\n## Manager override and PIN\n\nWhen the register needs authority beyond the cashier's, this prompt asks for a manager's own credentials — the manager's register PIN, entered by the manager, never handed to the cashier to type. The action is recorded under the manager's identity alongside the cashier's. The PIN standard is stated in Register Basics (§4.1.1) and Keypad (§4.1.4). If no manager is present, the prompt waits and the sale waits with it. Staff shall not shop for credentials; every rejected attempt is in the audit log.\n\n## Refund reason\n\nEvery refund requires a reason: plain words plus a reason code the reports use. The prompt offers the common reasons — defective, wrong item, changed mind, damaged in store — and a free-text field for the rest. The cashier shall write the reason as if it will be read in six months during an audit: \"defective — seam split on first wash\" is useful; \"return\" is not. The reason code drives defect rates by product and damage reporting. If the real reason is disputed, write it factually anyway; the record is not the place for diplomacy. The refund procedure is stated in Post-Transaction (§4.1.8), and refund rights are governed by the Returns and Refunds Policy (§2.1.19).\n\n## Held orders\n\nThis prompt lists the carts parked on this register — when each was held, what is in it, and which customer it belongs to. Retrieving a cart restores its lines, discounts, customer, and notes intact. The cashier shall read the customer name before confirming, because tendering someone else's sale is the mistake this prompt exists to prevent. Held carts have no permanent storage; a hold list that has been sitting for hours shall be treated as abandoned and cleared rather than left to linger into the next shift.\n\n## Receipt preview\n\nBefore the receipt prints or sends, this prompt shows exactly what the customer will receive: every line, every discount, the tax, the total, and the tenders. It is the last chance to catch an error while it is still free to fix. The cashier shall read it as the customer will — top to bottom, item count against the bags on the counter. The two errors this preview exists to catch are the duplicated line and the wrong customer attached.\n\n## Controls\n\n- No prompt answer shall be entered on another person's behalf: not a tip, not an override PIN, not an age verification.\n- A rejected coupon, a failed age verification, and a rejected override shall each be handled as the relevant section states, never worked around with a manual discount, an estimate, or borrowed credentials.\n\n## Records\n\nEach answered prompt writes its record with the sale: the discount and its reason, coupon validation results, the attached customer, the tip on the payment, override attempts and approvals with the manager's identity, refund reasons and reason codes, and held cart contents while held. These records are kept in the point of sale system.\n\n## Escalation and review\n\nManagers review discount reasons, override attempts, and refund reasons as part of shift review. A prompt fault that the store cannot resolve shall be reported to support@unsaidmemories.com. Legal process concerning a sale or an age-verification record shall be directed to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> System faults in register prompts: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal process concerning a sale record: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "crm-dashboard": "\n# CRM Dashboard\n\nThis standard governs the daily use of the customer record system's dashboard: the overview figures, the Needs Attention feed, and the booking funnel. It applies to front desk staff, who work the dashboard each morning, and to managers, who review what the feed surfaces. Everything on the dashboard is computed from live customer, appointment, and payment records. There is no stored summary that can go stale, and no figure that is not traceable to a real record.\n\n## The overview figures\n\nThe top of the customers page shows overview tiles, computed in the browser from the customer directory rows described in Customers (§4.2.2):\n\n- **Total** — every customer row in the directory.\n- **Active** — rows where the customer is not marked inactive.\n- **New (30d)** — customers created within the last 30 days.\n- **Returning** — customers with 2 or more appointments on record.\n- **At risk** — customers whose lifecycle stage is at risk, under Customer Lifecycle (§4.2.6).\n- **Follow-up** — customers at risk, or flagged follow-up due.\n- **Upcoming** — customers with a next appointment: a future appointment that is not cancelled, completed, or no-show.\n- **Outstanding** — the sum of outstanding balances across the customer base.\n\nBecause the tiles are derived from the same rows as the list below them, they always agree. If the at-risk tile shows 6, filtering the list to at risk shows exactly those 6. When a tile surprises the reader, the rows behind it are one scroll away; click into the list.\n\n## Needs Attention — the four signals\n\nThe Needs Attention feed is the closest thing the system has to a to-do list. Every row is derived from a real signal on a real appointment. There is no separate issue list, nothing to mark done, and nothing to go stale: a row appears when its signal fires and disappears when the underlying condition clears. The window is appointments from 30 days ago forward, excluding cancelled and no-show appointments, capped at 100 rows. The four signals, in priority order:\n\n1. **Unpaid balance (High)** — an appointment that is completed, or whose start time has passed, with a balance remaining: total minus amount paid is greater than zero. The action is direct: take payment at the counter or send a payment reminder. Staff reconciling by hand shall do the same subtraction; the stored outstanding amount on the appointment is not the figure to trust.\n2. **Unsigned document (Medium)** — the customer has a document awaiting signature. Send the document for signature or collect it at check-in.\n3. **Vaccination expiring (Medium; High if expired)** — a pet on the appointment has a vaccination record expiring within 30 days, or already expired. Request the updated record before the visit; this signal prevents a customer being turned away at the door.\n4. **No-show history (Medium)** — the customer has a prior no-show appointment. Confirm this appointment and collect the deposit before the visit.\n\nAn honestly empty list means nothing is firing. Staff shall still check the feed every morning, because the unpaid-balance signal in particular is money sitting uncollected.\n\n## The booking funnel\n\nThe funnel records the path from first visit to booked appointment, one record per event:\n\n- **Website visit** — the storefront page-view record.\n- **Account created** — a new salon customer record from booking signup.\n- **Intake started** — the first step of the booking process, which is the intake itself: name, contact, dog, coat, grooming.\n- **Intake completed** — the booking process reaches its review step.\n- **Booking started** — the begin-booking event.\n- **Booking completed** — a booking actually completes: deposit or full payment confirmed, or a booking created directly and confirmed.\n\nFunnel records are written so that a failed funnel write never breaks a booking, a payment, or a signup; if the write fails, the booking still goes through and the funnel simply misses a record. Intake stages are recorded once per booking session, not once per page refresh, so drop-off between intake started and intake completed shows real abandonment, not noise. A large gap between booking started and booking completed usually means deposit friction and should be compared against the abandoned-booking leads described in Rebooking and Automation (§4.2.7).\n\n## When the dashboard looks wrong\n\n- **Tiles show but the list is empty, or the reverse.** Both read the same rows, so this is a filter problem, not a data problem. Clear every filter on the list and look again; a forgotten lifecycle or tag filter is the usual cause.\n- **\"Database unavailable\" or a service error on every screen.** The customer record system cannot reach its database. This is an infrastructure problem, not a data problem: no rows are lost and everything returns when connectivity does. Staff shall not re-enter anything; wait for the connection, and escalate under Troubleshooting (§4.2.11).\n- **Lifetime value shows $0 for everyone.** That is honest, not broken. Lifetime value is the sum of succeeded payments carrying the customer's identifier, and most payments in the ledger do not carry one, as explained in Customers (§4.2.2). Staff shall never compare current figures against older figures produced before the ledger-based calculation, and shall never correct the figure by hand.\n- **A customer known to exist is not in the directory.** Search by email first, exactly; the system matches customers on email, and name variants are two different rows until merged. If email search finds nothing, the record may exist only in the older booking-side records and never carried over, because the carry-over needs an email address. The correction procedure is in Data Management (§4.2.9).\n\n## The morning routine\n\nFront desk staff shall complete this routine in order, every morning:\n\n1. Open the Needs Attention feed. Work it top to bottom: unpaid balances first, unsigned documents second, expiring vaccinations third, no-show history fourth.\n2. Glance at the tiles — total, new in 30 days, at risk, upcoming — to calibrate the day. Anything that moved a lot overnight deserves a question before the doors open.\n3. Scan today's appointments. For each: customer confirmed, service type confirmed, payment method confirmed, deposit down, and any pet handling cautions on the dogs.\n4. Check the waitlist if the book has gaps, and offer an open slot to the top of the queue before it goes to waste, under Appointments (§4.2.4).\n5. Clear yesterday's filters. A stale filter is how customers appear to disappear.\n\n## Controls\n\n- Dashboard figures shall not be edited, adjusted, or explained away by hand. Where a figure looks wrong, staff shall trace it to the underlying records.\n- Customer data shown on the dashboard shall be used only for the customer-service purpose it is displayed for, under the Privacy Policy (§2.1.2). Dashboard figures and lists shall not be exported without authorization, under Data Management (§4.2.9).\n\n## Records\n\nThe dashboard creates no records of its own; it displays the customer, appointment, pet, vaccination, document, and payment records kept in the customer record system, and the funnel records each booking event creates.\n\n## Escalation and review\n\nManagers review the Needs Attention feed daily for unpaid balances left unworked. A dashboard or database fault that the store cannot resolve shall be reported to support@unsaidmemories.com. Legal process concerning a customer record shall be directed to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> System faults in the customer record system: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal process concerning a customer record: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "global-search": "\n# Global Search\n\nThis standard governs global search in the administrative system: what it searches, what an empty result means, and the search-driven workflows the desk runs daily. It applies to front desk staff and managers, who use one search box across every record type without needing to know which part of the system owns the record. Search results display customer and payment data that shall be used only for the service purpose for which it was found, under the Privacy Policy (§2.1.2), and shall not be exported without authorization.\n\n## What it searches\n\nGlobal search covers seven record types, each against its real records and real fields:\n\n| Type | Searches |\n|---|---|\n| Customers | First name, last name, email, phone |\n| Pets | Name, breed |\n| Appointments | Appointment number, internal notes, customer notes |\n| Invoices | Invoice number, external identifier |\n| Payments | Payment number, processor transaction identifier, external reference |\n| Messages | Subject, body, from and to address |\n| Documents | Name |\n\nSingle-type search returns up to 50 rows, newest first. Searching across all types runs every type at once, 5 rows each, and totals them. An empty query returns empty; it does not dump the records.\n\nStaff shall read the field lists carefully, because they define search's blind spots:\n\n- **Customers: no address, no notes.** Searching a street name finds nothing even if the address is on the record. Search the name, email, or phone.\n- **Pets: name and breed only.** A description such as \"the anxious poodle\" will not match; the pet's name, or \"poodle,\" will.\n- **Appointments: numbers and notes only.** Appointments cannot be searched here by customer name or pet name; use the appointments list for that, under Appointments (§4.2.4). But the appointment number is searchable, which is why the desk reads it back to phone customers: it gives the customer a key that works in this box.\n- **Payments: numbers and processor identifiers.** Search the payment number from the receipt, or the processor payment identifier for a disputed charge.\n- **Messages: the actual communications log**, not a separate archive.\n\n## What an empty result means\n\nThe search fields are verified against the records as they actually exist, so an empty result is now meaningful: the record genuinely is not there, or is not under that spelling. It is not a broken query. The standing lesson for the desk is broader: distrust silent emptiness. If a search that should hit returns nothing, say so plainly instead of assuming the customer does not exist. \"Not found\" and \"does not exist\" differ by the spelling, the type filter, and which location's records are being searched; check all three before telling a customer they are not in the system.\n\n## Working the search\n\n- **Start broad, then narrow.** Search across all types when the record type is unknown — \"a customer called about a charge\" could be a payment, an invoice, or the customer. Switch to the single type once the kind of record is known.\n- **Phone numbers: digits only, no formatting.** Matching is by contained text; a formatted query with dashes can miss a differently formatted stored value. Strip the query to digits.\n- **Names: try last name first.** First names collide across customers and pets; the type filter separates them, but last name is still the sharper key.\n- **Appointment numbers: exact.** They are the primary key of the conversation; read them back, write them down, search them exactly as given.\n\n## The command palette\n\nThe command palette is the keyboard-facing side of the same system — the quick-action dispatcher — and it also fires the appointment actions described in Appointment Detail (§4.2.5). If the palette stops offering an action it used to offer, that is a change to the staff member's permissions, not a data problem. Check the staff member's permissions before anything else.\n\n## Search-driven workflows\n\nThree searches the desk runs constantly, and exactly how to run them:\n\n**\"Has this person been here before?\"** Search customers by last name. If nothing, try the phone digits. If still nothing, they are new — but search across all types once before creating a record, in case the name appears on a pet or an appointment note under a different spelling. Creating the record is governed by Customers (§4.2.2) and the search-first discipline in Data Management (§4.2.9).\n\n**\"Where's the money?\"** For a customer disputing a charge, search payments by the payment number from their receipt. With no receipt, a name will not work in invoice search, because invoices search by number and external identifier; go to the profile's invoices view instead. Search is for when the desk has the number; the profile is for when the desk has the person.\n\n**\"Which appointment was that?\"** Search appointments by the appointment number, exactly as given. This is why the desk reads the number back at booking, under Appointments (§4.2.4): it is the one key that works here without fail.\n\n## Controls\n\n- Staff shall verify spelling, type filter, and record set before telling a customer no record exists.\n- Search shall not be used to browse customer, message, or payment data unrelated to the task in hand. Every search is for a stated service purpose.\n- An action missing from the command palette shall be treated as a permissions question first, not worked around.\n\n## Records\n\nGlobal search creates no records; it reads the customer, pet, appointment, invoice, payment, message, and document records kept in the customer record system and the accounting records. Actions fired from the command palette create the records described in Appointment Detail (§4.2.5).\n\n## Escalation and review\n\nManagers review searches reported as wrongly empty and permission changes affecting the command palette. A search fault that the store cannot resolve shall be reported to support@unsaidmemories.com. Legal process concerning a record found through search shall be directed to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> System faults in global search: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal process concerning a customer record: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "crm-troubleshooting": "\n# CRM Troubleshooting\n\nThis standard governs how failures in the customer record system are diagnosed and corrected. It applies to front desk staff, who work each failure in the order stated, and to managers, who take over infrastructure faults and code faults. Every entry states what the failure looks like, why it happens, and the fix in the order to try it. Staff shall work top to bottom and shall not skip to a clever explanation before ruling out the ordinary one. The standing rule is: assume the data is telling the truth about a process failure before assuming the data is corrupt. Empty means empty, $0 means the ledger proves $0, and a stuck status means the conversion missed. Nine times out of ten, the fix is in the process — obtain the email, set the status, configure the rule — not in the records. The tenth time, it is a system fault, and this standard names where it lies.\n\n## Database unavailable on every screen\n\n**Looks like:** the dashboard, directory, and appointments all fail at once with a database-unavailable message.\n\n**Why:** every screen in the customer record system connects to the database through a shared connection service that requires its configured connection string. If that configuration is missing or the database is unreachable, every screen fails. The audit write is protected separately, so a logging failure cannot cascade — but nothing can be read without the connection.\n\n**Fix:** this is infrastructure, not data. No rows are lost; everything returns when connectivity does. Staff shall not re-enter anything, shall not \"fix\" records, and shall not restart the register in a panic. Confirm the scope of the outage — one person, or everyone — wait for the connection, verify the figures came back, and escalate to support@unsaidmemories.com if it does not return.\n\n## Customer is not in the system\n\n**Looks like:** staff know the customer booked, but search finds nothing. Try, in order:\n\n1. **Search the email exactly.** The system matches customers on email; name variants are two rows. Email first, phone second, last name third, under Global Search (§4.2.10).\n2. **Check for a forgotten filter.** A lifecycle, tag, or location filter left on from the last task hides rows silently. Clear all filters and search again. This is the single most common cause.\n3. **The booking had no email.** The carry-over from the booking records requires an email, because the appointment record requires a customer. A booking without one produces no customer record at all; the record lives only in the older booking-side records. Obtain the email, add it to the record, and the carry-over brings it across, under Customers (§4.2.2).\n4. **Record-set mismatch.** Every query is scoped to the location's records. A record created under a different set — franchise against main — is invisible, not missing.\n\n## Appointment stuck at scheduled\n\n**Looks like:** the dog is on the table but the appointment says scheduled.\n\n**Why:** scheduled is the carry-over fallback, returned for anything the status conversion does not recognize. The booking side writes forms such as \"Completed,\" \"CONFIRMED,\" and \"Checked In\"; the conversion standardizes case and spacing before mapping. A past fault of exactly this kind — a spaced status falling through to scheduled — was corrected by that standardization. If it appears on a current appointment, the conversion has regressed or a new booking-side status has appeared that the conversion does not know.\n\n**Fix:** set the status by hand with the in-service action, under Appointment Detail (§4.2.5). Then flag it. A recurring stuck-at-scheduled pattern means the conversion needs the new value added; that is a system fix, not a data fix, and shall be reported to support@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Booking error: appointment created, linking failed\n\n**Looks like:** the booking appears to work, then an error naming the appointment identifier states that linking its pet or service failed.\n\n**Why:** the appointment row was created, but the pet or service link rows failed — usually a wrong service identifier, or a service record belonging to a different record set.\n\n**Fix:** do not create the appointment again. That mints a second appointment number and leaves two rows for one dog. Fix the link: verify the service exists for this record set, verify the pet record, and re-attach. The error message names the appointment identifier; use it. The full rule is in Appointments (§4.2.4).\n\n## Lifetime value $0 and outstanding $0 everywhere\n\n**Looks like:** every customer shows $0 lifetime value; every balance shows $0.\n\n**Why:** lifetime value is the sum of payments that succeeded and carry that customer's identifier, and most payments do not carry one. The stored lifetime value on the customer record itself is $0 for every row; the directory ignores it and uses the ledger. Outstanding balance is genuinely $0 across the board at present.\n\n**Fix:** none. This is honest, not broken. Staff shall not hand-edit the figures; they would diverge from the ledger and the next directory load would disagree with the edit. Fix the linkage going forward — payments recorded against the customer — and the figures will fill in truthfully, under Data Management (§4.2.9).\n\n## No-show fee did not fire, or fired twice\n\n**Looks like:** an appointment was marked no-show and no invoice appeared — or the customer claims double billing.\n\n**Why:** the fee machinery applies once per appointment; the invoice carries a record tag, and re-firing the transition finds it and charges nothing twice. A \"double bill\" is almost always two separate no-show appointments, not one billed twice. A fee that did not fire usually means the location's booking rule is not configured — no stored rule, no fee; a settings form may show starting values, but only saved rules are applied — or the appointment was marked no-show, un-marked, and re-marked in a way that confused the history.\n\n**Fix:** check the fee invoices for the appointment's identifier before anything else. If the invoice exists, the fee fired; show it to the customer. If it does not exist and the rule is configured, the transition needs re-firing. Get the status right the first time; un-marking to \"fix\" things creates the confusion. Fee rules are stated in Appointments (§4.2.4).\n\n## Commission looks short\n\n**Looks like:** a groomer's pay does not reflect the day's work. Check, in order:\n\n1. Was the appointment actually completed — the status, not just done in real life? Accrual fires on the status transition.\n2. Does the groomer's staff record carry a commission rate? No rate means no accrual and no invented default. The gap is reported, not hidden.\n3. Is the pay type commission-bearing — commission, salary plus commission, or hourly plus commission? Pure hourly or salary never accrues, by design.\n4. Was the groomer recorded on the service line, or did it fall back to the appointment's assigned groomer? The performer is the line's groomer first.\n\nAccrual happens once per appointment, service, and staff member, so completing twice never double-pays, and re-completing to \"fix\" a short commission does nothing. Find which of the four checks failed and fix that. The accrual rules are stated in Appointment Detail (§4.2.5).\n\n## A staff member's button does nothing\n\n**Looks like:** actions, or the command palette, offer nothing or fail silently for one person.\n\n**Why:** every route in the customer record system is gated by administrative permission, and the finer actions are gated by module permission — customers or appointments, read or write. A silent failure is usually a permission, not a fault.\n\n**Fix:** check the staff member's module permissions before touching data or code. The rebooking action, the waitlist, and the actions function all check the gate first.\n\n## Search returns nothing, but should not\n\n**Looks like:** global search finds nothing for a record staff know exists.\n\n**Why:** the search fields are verified, so empty now means genuinely not found, or not under that spelling. But check the type: appointment search covers numbers and notes, not customer or pet names; customer search covers name, email, and phone, not address. Searching outside the covered fields is the usual miss.\n\n**Fix:** switch to the right type, strip phone queries to digits, and try the last name. For an appointment, search the appointment number exactly as given. Field coverage is stated in Global Search (§4.2.10).\n\n## Controls\n\n- Staff shall not re-enter, re-create, or hand-edit records to solve a failure described in this standard where the stated fix is to wait, to correct a link, or to fix a process step.\n- A fault identified as a system or code fault shall be reported, not worked around with parallel records or spreadsheets, under Data Management (§4.2.9).\n\n## Records\n\nTroubleshooting shall not delete or alter customer, appointment, payment, commission, or fee records except by the corrective actions stated here, each of which creates its own history and audit entries under Appointment Detail (§4.2.5).\n\n## Escalation and review\n\nManagers review recurring failures for a pattern pointing to a system fault. Faults the store cannot resolve — database unavailability that does not clear, a regressed status conversion, search failures against verified fields — shall be reported to support@unsaidmemories.com, identifying the screen, the record involved, and what was tried. Legal process concerning a customer, appointment, or fee record shall be directed to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> System faults in the customer record system: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal process concerning a customer record: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "customers": "\n# Customers\n\nThis standard governs the customer directory in the customer record system: how customers are found, added, and edited, and how the directory's figures are to be read. It applies to front desk staff, who search, add, and edit customer records daily, and to managers, who merge duplicates and review data quality. The directory holds one row per real person. Every write path finds or creates a customer by email address, so the same person booking twice lands on the same row instead of minting a duplicate. The desk's duty is to protect that rule: search first, create second, merge when the rule breaks anyway. Customer data is handled under the Privacy Policy (§2.1.2): it is used for the single purpose of serving that customer, and it shall not be exported without authorization.\n\n## The directory\n\nEach directory row is enriched with figures drawn from the customer's real records:\n\n- **Pets** — the pets linked to the customer.\n- **Lifetime value** — the sum of succeeded or captured payments carrying this customer's identifier. Most payments do not carry a customer identifier, so most customers honestly show $0. The directory uses the payment ledger, not a stored lifetime value figure. Where the ledger proves no value, the value is $0; staff shall not correct it by hand.\n- **Balance** — the customer's outstanding balance.\n- **Last visit** — the latest start time among completed appointments, falling back to the stored last-visit date.\n- **Next appointment** — the earliest future appointment that is not cancelled, completed, or no-show, with its service names.\n- **Assigned staff** — the assigned staff member or preferred groomer, otherwise the groomer on the most recent appointment. Where no real assignment exists, the field stays empty.\n\nSearch covers first name, last name, email, and phone, by partial match. The list shows 200 rows by default, newest first. For a base of thousands, staff shall search rather than scroll. Missing data shows as empty. Staff shall never assume a missing value; ask the customer.\n\n## Adding a customer\n\nStaff shall search before creating, every time: email first, exact; then phone; then last name. The thirty seconds this takes is cheaper than any merge.\n\nWhen the Add Customer action is used, the system finds or creates by email:\n\n- **Email is the identity.** Two records with the same email, case-insensitive, are the same person to the system. Entering an email that already exists returns the existing row; the system refuses to duplicate.\n- **No email, no matching.** A customer added without an email cannot be matched later. Staff shall always obtain the email. A phone number alone does not match records.\n- **New rows start as new customers** in the lifecycle, under Customer Lifecycle (§4.2.6). Abandoned-booking leads, created automatically by the system, start as new leads instead, under Rebooking and Automation (§4.2.7).\n- **Lead source shall be set** at creation, from the lead source list: Website, Google, Referral, Social Media, Phone, Walk-In, Campaign, Other. It is how marketing attribution works later, and it cannot be reconstructed after the fact.\n\n## Editing a customer\n\nOpen the record, change the field, and the change is saved. Three fields require more care than the screen suggests:\n\n- **Email.** This is the matching key and the destination for receipts, reminders, and marketing. Changing it re-points all of those. Confirm the new address verbally with the customer; a typo silently diverts the customer's reminders to a stranger.\n- **Marketing opt-ins, do-not-call, and do-not-contact flags.** These are hard stops, not suggestions. Every sending path checks the record's flags before sending, and lead capture does not override them. If a customer says \"stop texting me,\" staff shall set the flag while the customer is present and tell the customer it is done.\n- **Phone.** Booking information may fill an empty phone field, but it never overwrites a number staff entered. The desk's entry prevails.\n\n## The two record sets, plainly\n\nThe business keeps an older booking-side record set — customers, dogs, and bookings written by the website booking process and older flows — and the customer record system itself, which every administrative screen reads. A bridge carries each booking into the customer record system: one booking becomes one appointment, one customer (found or created by email), and one pet (matched by the booking's dog record first, then by customer and pet name). The customer record keeps a link back to the booking-side record, as do the pet and the appointment, which carries a human-readable appointment number.\n\nTwo consequences follow for the desk:\n\n1. **A booking without an email never reaches the customer record system.** The appointment record requires a customer, and no email means no customer row, with no exceptions. If a walk-in books without giving an email and cannot later be found, that is why. Obtain the email at booking; it is the condition of admission to the whole system.\n2. **Statuses are converted in transit.** The booking side writes forms such as \"Completed,\" \"CONFIRMED,\" and \"Checked In\"; the customer record system stores completed, confirmed, and in service. If an appointment appears stuck at scheduled when it should be in service, this conversion is the first place to look, under Troubleshooting (§4.2.11).\n\n## Controls\n\n- One person, one row. Staff shall not create a second row for a person who already has one; duplicates are merged under Data Management (§4.2.9).\n- Customer data shall be used only for serving that customer. No export of the directory, in whole or in part, is permitted without authorization.\n- An empty record set means the connection for it is not built, never that it is unused. Staff shall not work around an empty record set with a spreadsheet or a parallel process; flag it so the connection gets built. Spreadsheets become duplicate factories.\n\n## Records\n\nEach customer created or edited creates or updates a customer record carrying identity, contact details, lifecycle stage, lead source, opt-in and do-not-contact flags, assigned staff, and outstanding balance, with pets, appointments, and payments linked from their own records. These records are kept in the customer record system.\n\n## Escalation and review\n\nManagers review duplicates, records missing email addresses, and opt-in flags that were not honored. A directory fault that the store cannot resolve shall be reported to support@unsaidmemories.com. Legal process concerning a customer record, including a request under the Privacy Policy (§2.1.2), shall be directed to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> System faults in the customer directory: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal process concerning a customer record: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "customer-profile": "\n# Customer Profile\n\nThis standard governs the customer profile: the single screen holding everything the business knows about one person — identity and contact details, pets and handling information, appointments and money, notes, flags, and tags, and the rebooking recommendation. It applies to front desk staff and groomers, who read and update the profile at check-in and checkout, and to managers, who review profile accuracy. Staff shall learn the profile in the order it is used at the desk: identity first, then the pets, then the money, then the history. Customer data on the profile is handled under the Privacy Policy (§2.1.2), for the single purpose of serving that customer.\n\n## Identity and contact\n\nThe header shows the name, the lifecycle stage marker, and contact details. The fields the desk touches most:\n\n- **Email** — the matching key, under Customers (§4.2.2). Changing it re-points receipts and reminders; confirm the new address verbally.\n- **Phone and mobile** — the profile shows the phone number, falling back to the mobile number, then empty. If both are empty, the record cannot receive text reminders; fixing that is the first task at check-in, not after a no-show.\n- **Address** — first line, city, state, and postal code. Used for the household view and for anything mailed.\n- **Preferred location** — may be empty on every current record. Until locations are assigned, a directory location filter truthfully matches nothing; staff shall not report that as a fault.\n- **Preferred or assigned groomer** — the preferred groomer or assigned staff member, falling back to the groomer on the most recent appointment. Where it is empty, it is empty; the system does not invent one.\n\n## Pets\n\nPets reach the profile through the ownership link to the customer, marked with the primary pet where one is set. Each pet carries name, species, breed, sex, and color. Two rules bite:\n\n- **Sex is a fixed vocabulary: male, female, or unknown, in lowercase.** Booking information is converted into that vocabulary when it arrives. If a pet's sex looks wrong, it arrived wrong from the booking; fix it on the pet, and the fix will hold, because the matching process does not overwrite an existing pet's fields blindly.\n- **Matching is by the booking's dog record first, then by customer and pet name.** Name matching trims spaces and ignores case, so spelling variants of one name are the same pet. But a new dog given a departed dog's name is also the same row to the matcher. When a customer gets a new dog with a reused name, staff shall record the distinction in a note, because the registry cannot tell them apart.\n\nThe handling profile sits alongside the pet: temperament, the handling flags (nails, face, feet, ears, dryer, clippers), and handling notes. Caution designations — bite history, severe anxiety, seizure disorder, flat-faced breed risk, senior or fragile — surface at booking so the appointment can go only to a groomer whose specialties include special handling. If intake reveals something the profile does not know, staff shall update the profile the same day. A profile that contradicts the dog in front of the groomer is a safety problem, not a data problem.\n\nVaccination records have their place in the system, but that record set is currently empty because its entry path is not built. Until it is built, vaccination proof stays where the salon keeps it today, and the Needs Attention feed cannot fire the expiring-vaccination signal described in CRM Dashboard (§4.2.1). Under the standing rule in Customers (§4.2.2), an empty record set is flagged, not worked around.\n\n## Appointments and money\n\nThe appointments view for this customer shows each appointment with its number, start time, status, total, amount paid, and deposit, with pet, service, groomer, and location names included; the underlying appointment record carries none of those names itself.\n\nThe payments view shows payments recorded against this customer. Only payments carrying the customer's identifier appear. A payment taken at the register against an appointment without the customer linked will not show here. If a customer insists they paid and the view is empty, check the appointment's amount paid and the payment number before disputing the customer; the money is usually recorded on the appointment, not the profile.\n\nFee invoices — the accounts receivable side, for cancellation and no-show fees — also appear on the profile. These are real open invoices, never silent card charges: when an appointment is marked no-show, or cancelled inside the cutoff window, the fee becomes an invoice owed by the customer, with any held deposit applied against it first. The customer can see it, and the desk collects against it. The desk's duty is to tell the customer the invoice exists and take payment against it. Fee mechanics are stated in Appointments (§4.2.4) and Appointment Detail (§4.2.5).\n\n## Notes, flags, and tags\n\nNotes on the profile use a fixed type vocabulary — internal, customer visible, appointment, pet handling, and system — and are searched by their text. Staff shall pin the note the next person needs; pinned notes stay in place. System notes are written by the system itself, for example the abandoned-booking capture, and staff shall not edit them; they are the audit trail of what the system did and why. Notes are governed in full by Notes (§4.2.8).\n\nTags are assigned to customers and pets from the tag list — including VIP, First Visit, Nervous, and Special Handling — and are the closest thing the system has to segments; there are no prebuilt segment sets. A \"Nervous\" tag on the pet, together with a caution handling profile, is how the next groomer knows before the dog is on the table.\n\n## Rebooking recommendation\n\nThe grooming history view carries a \"Recommend Next Visit\" action. It records a recommended date, in strict year-month-day format — any other format is refused — with an optional pet and a reason, and creates a rebooking recommendation with status open. A staff member whose action does nothing shall have their permissions checked first; the action requires the customers write permission. The manual recommendation and the automatic side of rebooking — the needs-rebooking list and interval calculation — are governed by Rebooking and Automation (§4.2.7). Use the recommendation when the groomer says \"see me in six weeks\"; use the list when working a win-back list.\n\n## Controls\n\n- Staff shall verify identity and contact details verbally with the customer before changing email or phone on the profile.\n- Handling cautions shall be updated the same day they are learned. A caution recorded only in conversation protects no one.\n- Profile data shall not be exported without authorization, under Data Management (§4.2.9).\n\n## Records\n\nThe profile displays and updates the customer record, the linked pet and handling records, appointments, payments, fee invoices, notes, and tags, all kept in the customer record system. A rebooking recommendation creates its own record with status open.\n\n## Escalation and review\n\nManagers review profiles with conflicting pet records, missing contact details, and unupdated handling cautions. A profile fault that the store cannot resolve shall be reported to support@unsaidmemories.com. Legal process concerning a customer record shall be directed to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> System faults in the customer profile: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal process concerning a customer record: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "appointments": "\n# Appointments\n\nThis standard governs appointments in the customer record system: the status vocabulary, the list and enriched view, booking at the desk, the Needs Attention workflow, the waitlist, and the money rules for deposits and fees. It applies to front desk staff, who book and move appointments, and to managers, who configure the booking rules that set deposits and fees. Every appointment the business owns is in this registry, whether it came from the website booking process, the front desk, or another system entry. If an appointment exists in the older booking records but not in this registry, check the email; no email means no registry row, with no exceptions, under Customers (§4.2.2).\n\n## The status vocabulary\n\nAppointment status is a fixed vocabulary. Staff shall think of the values as a pipeline, not as labels:\n\n- **Precheck** — the booking arrived but is not confirmed yet.\n- **Deposit paid** — money is down and the slot is held.\n- **Scheduled** — on the books. This is also the fallback for anything the booking carry-over does not recognize, so a scheduled appointment with a same-day start time deserves a glance.\n- **Assigned** — a groomer is attached.\n- **Waiting check-in** — the customer is expected; the dog is not on the table yet.\n- **Confirmed** — the customer confirmed.\n- **Checked in** — the dog is in the building.\n- **In service** — on the table, work happening.\n- **Hold** — paused, not cancelled. Use it when the customer calls mid-day to push by an hour; it keeps the appointment on the board without losing its history.\n- **Completed** — done. This is a money status: completing an appointment fires commission accrual for the groomer, under Appointment Detail (§4.2.5).\n- **No-show** — the customer did not come. This is also a money status: marking no-show fires the no-show fee machinery, producing a real invoice, never a silent charge.\n- **Cancelled** — cancelled. Inside the cutoff window, this fires the cancellation fee the same way.\n- **Rescheduled** — moved. The history records the transition; the new appointment carries on.\n\nEvery transition writes a status history entry — from, to, reason, and the originating booking reference — and an audit entry. Nothing about an appointment's past is editable; the history is append-only by design, because when a customer disputes a no-show fee, the history is the evidence.\n\n## The list and the enriched view\n\nThe plain appointment list returns the appointment records themselves, which carry no names. Pet, service, groomer, and location names live on the linked records. The list views staff actually use present the enriched form: customer name, email, and phone; pet names and breed; service names; groomer name; location name; the money figures (total, amount paid, deposit amount, outstanding amount, subtotal, tax total); and the communication timestamps for pickup notification, payment processed, and thank-you messages — the communications audit trail.\n\nTwo warnings apply:\n\n- **The stored outstanding amount is not trusted.** Records can show zero there while amount paid is also zero. The Needs Attention feed computes total minus amount paid itself for exactly this reason. Staff reconciling shall do the subtraction rather than reading the stored figure.\n- **Service type confirmed and payment method confirmed** are the confirmation flags. An appointment can be confirmed with an unconfirmed service type — the slot was confirmed but not what will be done — and that state is worth a call before the dog arrives.\n\n## Booking an appointment at the desk\n\nWhen an appointment is created, the pet and service selections are not part of the appointment record itself. The system creates the appointment row, then writes the link rows: one pet link, and one service link per service, with the price taken from the service's default price at that moment. The price snapshot matters: if the service price changes next week, this appointment keeps the price it was booked at.\n\nIf the linking fails, the system reports the failure plainly and names the appointment. The appointment exists without its links. Staff shall not create it again; fix the link — usually a wrong service identifier or a service record belonging to another location's records — and re-attach. Creating it again mints a duplicate appointment number and leaves two records for one dog.\n\n## Needs Attention — the morning triage\n\nThe same four signals as the dashboard, in CRM Dashboard (§4.2.1), read here as a workflow:\n\n1. **Unpaid balance** — take payment or send the reminder. Do this first; it is revenue.\n2. **Unsigned document** — collect at check-in or send for signature now.\n3. **Vaccination expiring** — request the record before the visit. An expired vaccination at the door is a turned-away customer and a lost slot.\n4. **No-show history** — confirm the appointment and collect the deposit before the visit.\n\nStaff shall work the feed top to bottom every morning. Because the feed is derived from the underlying conditions, clearing a condition clears the row; there is no separate \"mark done\" step and no stale rows to clean up.\n\n## The waitlist\n\nThe waitlist is a real queue, not a notepad. Each entry carries the customer, pet, service, location, preferred groomer, a priority, the requested time window, and an expiry. Only entries in waiting or offered state are shown; booked means converted, cancelled means removed. When a slot opens, staff shall work the waitlist by priority and requested window, and convert the entry rather than booking fresh. Conversion keeps the history — who waited, how long, and what they wanted — which is how the business learns whether the waitlist is working.\n\n## Deposits, fees, and the money rules\n\nDeposits and cancellation and no-show fees are set by each location's booking rules, stored as its configuration and read at the time the fee is applied. The discipline is strict:\n\n- **No stored rule, no fee.** An unset or zero rule means no fee is charged. A settings screen may display starting values in its form, but only what the location actually saved is applied. Staff shall never promise a customer a fee that is not configured, and never assume one exists.\n- **Fees become invoices, never silent charges.** A no-show or inside-cutoff cancellation creates a real open fee invoice owed by the customer and visible on the customer's profile. It is never a silent card charge. Staff shall tell the customer the invoice exists and collect against it.\n- **Held deposits apply first.** A deposit already held is applied against the fee before anything else; the deposit is marked applied and the invoice records it in its notes. A held deposit larger than the fee stays held — deposits are not split — and the invoice says so; the desk applies the remainder manually.\n- **One fee per appointment.** Re-applying a no-show transition finds the existing fee invoice by its record tag and charges nothing twice. Marking no-show twice does not double-bill, but un-marking and re-marking to \"fix\" something can confuse the history, so staff shall get the status right the first time.\n\nA service created on first touch starts with a deposit requirement and a $25 default deposit. That is the starting value, not the policy; the location's booking rules decide what is actually charged.\n\n## Booking at the desk, start to finish\n\nFor a phone or walk-in booking, staff shall follow this procedure in order. Every step exists because skipping it creates a specific later problem:\n\n1. **Search first.** Email, then phone, then last name. If the customer exists, open the profile; do not create a new record.\n2. **Confirm the pets.** Which dog or dogs, and is the registry pet the right one? Check the handling profile for cautions before promising a groomer or a slot; a special-handling dog booked to a groomer without that specialty is a problem created at the desk.\n3. **Pick the service and add-ons.** Service lines are priced from the service's default price, recorded at booking. Quote the total from the appointment, not from memory; the recorded price is the price the customer pays.\n4. **Pick the slot.** Check the groomer's day, not only the customer's preference. Stored appointment times are in the salon's timezone, America/Chicago; the wall-clock time the customer hears is Chicago time.\n5. **Take the deposit if the location's rules require one.** Deposit paid is its own status; use it. A booking with money down no-shows less.\n6. **Read back the appointment number.** It is searchable in global search and is the key the customer uses if they call back.\n7. **Confirm the contact details.** Email is the matching key and reminder destination; phone is for text reminders. Fix errors now; a typo diverts every future reminder.\n8. **Set the confirmation flags' starting state honestly.** Service type and payment method start unconfirmed unless verified on the spot. An honest unconfirmed flag is better than a false confirmed one; the morning triage works from these flags.\n\nIf the customer is new: create the customer record (email is mandatory), link or create the pet, and set the lead source.\n\n## Controls\n\n- Staff shall not re-create an appointment after a linking failure, and shall not mark no-show or cancel an appointment without being certain, because both actions move money.\n- No fee shall be promised or charged unless the location's booking rule for it is actually stored.\n- Appointment history shall not be edited. Corrections are made by new entries.\n\n## Records\n\nEach appointment creates an appointment record with its number, status, times, and money figures; pet and service link records with snapshotted prices; a status history entry and an audit entry for every transition; and, where a fee applies, a fee invoice with any held deposit applied. Waitlist entries are kept with their requested windows and outcomes. These records are kept in the customer record system.\n\n## Escalation and review\n\nManagers review unlinked appointments, disputed fees, and booking rules before they are relied on. A booking fault that the store cannot resolve shall be reported to support@unsaidmemories.com. Legal process concerning an appointment or fee record shall be directed to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> System faults in appointments: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal process concerning an appointment record: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "appointment-detail": "\n# Appointment Detail\n\nThis standard governs the actions that may be taken on a single appointment from its detail view: the twelve status actions, the operational actions, and the day-of flow from arrival to departure. It applies to front desk staff, who run the actions, and to groomers and managers, whose commissions and fees those actions trigger. Everything done to an appointment goes through the actions function, and that function is strict: every action is a real record write, with a status history entry and an audit entry, or it does not happen. The status vocabulary itself is stated in Appointments (§4.2.4).\n\n## The status actions\n\nEach status action sets the appointment's status to its target:\n\n| Action | Sets status to |\n|---|---|\n| Confirm | Confirmed |\n| Precheck | Precheck |\n| Deposit paid | Deposit paid |\n| Scheduled | Scheduled |\n| Assigned | Assigned |\n| Waiting check-in | Waiting check-in |\n| Check in | Checked in |\n| In service | In service |\n| Hold | Hold |\n| Complete | Completed |\n| No-show | No-show |\n| Cancel | Cancelled |\n\n## The operational actions\n\n- **Reschedule** — requires the new start time. The old slot is captured before the change so the reschedule email shows old to new. Sends the email.\n- **Duplicate** — copies the appointment. Use it for the \"same thing next time\" booking, not for fixing a mistake on this one.\n- **Send reminder** — sends the reminder email now, outside the automated schedule.\n- **Follow up** — records a follow-up against the appointment.\n- **Add to waitlist** — moves the customer to the waitlist, under Appointments (§4.2.4).\n- **Take payment** — records a payment: it requires a positive amount, writes a payment record with a generated payment number, and updates the appointment's amount paid. If no payment method is configured, it fails with a plain error rather than taking money into a void.\n- **Confirm service type and confirm payment method** — set the confirmation flags. These are milestones, not statuses: they do not move the pipeline; they record that a person verified something.\n- **Pickup notification, payment processed, and thank you** — stamp their respective sent-at timestamps and trigger the send. The enriched appointment view reads these as the communications audit trail: if a customer says nobody told them the dog was ready, this is where to check.\n- **Add service and set service** — attach service lines, priced from the service's default price under the same snapshot rule as booking.\n\nEvery action performs three writes: the appointment update, a status history entry (from-status, to-status, reason), and an audit entry. Missing the appointment identifier produces a refusal; a missing appointment produces a not-found response. Nothing is silent.\n\n## The two actions that move money\n\n**Complete.** Completing an appointment with a status change fires commission accrual. One earned entry is created per performed service, at the performing staff member's profile commission rate. The performer is the groomer recorded on the service line, falling back to the appointment's assigned groomer. The rules that protect payroll:\n\n- Only commission-bearing pay types accrue: commission, salary plus commission, and hourly plus commission. Pure hourly or salary staff never accrue, by design.\n- No rate on the profile means no accrual and no invented default. The gap is reported back — no groomer assigned, or no commission rate — so it stays visible instead of silently underpaying someone.\n- Accrual happens once per appointment, service, and staff member. Completing the same appointment twice never double-pays, and re-carrying an already-completed booking does not re-enter it.\n\nIf a groomer says their commission looks short, check three things in order: was the appointment actually completed — the status, not just done in real life; does their staff record carry a commission rate; and is their pay type commission-bearing.\n\n**No-show and cancel.** These fire the booking-fee machinery. Inside the location's cutoff window, the fee becomes a real open invoice owed by the customer — never a silent card charge — with any held deposit applied first, under the rules in Appointments (§4.2.4). The fee invoice carries a record tag; re-firing the transition finds it and charges nothing twice. Marking no-show is therefore a financial action, not only a calendar action. Staff shall be sure before tapping it, and if it is tapped by mistake, fix it promptly; the invoice it creates is visible to the customer.\n\n## Check-in, in-service, and the day-of flow\n\nThe day runs on the status pipeline: waiting check-in, checked in, in service, completed. At check-in, staff shall verify the service type and payment method flags; confirming them here is what keeps a \"slot confirmed but service not confirmed\" appointment from becoming a mid-grooming surprise. Staff shall collect anything the Needs Attention feed flagged — unsigned documents, expiring vaccinations — while the customer is present; it is the cheapest moment to do it.\n\nHold is the pressure valve: customer running an hour late, dog needs a break mid-groom, dryer queue backed up. It pauses the appointment without cancelling it; the history keeps the full story and the appointment stays on the board. Staff shall not use cancelled for a pause; cancelled fires the fee machinery.\n\n## The day-of runbook\n\nFor a single appointment, from arrival to departure, staff shall follow these steps. The status the appointment should show at each step is given in parentheses:\n\n1. **Before arrival** (confirmed, waiting check-in). Verify the three points: service type confirmed, payment method confirmed, deposit down. Pull the Needs Attention signals for this appointment; unsigned documents and expiring vaccinations are collected now, not mid-groom. Read the pet handling notes. If there is a caution, confirm the assigned groomer has special handling in their specialties.\n2. **Arrival** — set checked in. Confirm the dog matches the registry pet, allowing for the reused-name problem described in Customer Profile (§4.2.3). Collect anything outstanding from step 1. Note late arrivals on the appointment — \"running 15 minutes late, called ahead\" protects the groomer's schedule and the record.\n3. **Start of work** — set in service. If the customer is waiting nearby, this is when customer-visible notes receive their pickup-time estimate. The pickup notification later stamps its sent-at time; that is the communications audit trail.\n4. **During** — set hold if paused. Record notes on the grooming record for anything the next groomer needs: behavior, coat condition, what was done differently.\n5. **Finish** — set completed. This fires commission accrual, which is why the status shall be set even if everyone knows the work is done. An appointment left in service overnight accrues nothing and sits on tomorrow's board. At completion, the groomer's \"recommend next visit\" goes in before the customer leaves, under Rebooking and Automation (§4.2.7).\n6. **Payment** — take payment for the balance. The payment writes a payment record and updates amount paid. If the customer disputes, the payment number is the key. The payment processed action stamps its timestamp.\n7. **After** — the thank-you action sends the follow-up. Its sent-at stamp closes the communications trail.\n\nEvery step writes its own evidence — status history, audit entry, timestamps, notes. When a customer disputes anything — the fee, the cut, the pickup time — the evidence is already there.\n\n## When an action fails\n\n- **A refusal stating that the appointment identifier and new start time are required** (or similar) — the request was malformed. The screen should have prevented it; if it appears, something is wrong with the form, not the appointment.\n- **\"Appointment not found\"** — the identifier does not exist for this location's records. Record scoping is strict: an appointment belonging to another location's records, or a mistyped identifier, reads as not found, not as a permission error.\n- **A booking error after creation** — the appointment was created but the pet or service links failed, under Appointments (§4.2.4). Fix the link; do not re-create.\n- **\"No payment method available\" on take payment** — configure a payment method in the financial settings first. The function refuses to record a payment it cannot tag with a method.\n- **Audit failure** — the audit write is protected so that its failure does not fail the action. The appointment still updates; the audit gap is recorded on the system side. This is non-fatal by design.\n\n## Controls\n\n- No-show and cancel shall be used only when certain, because both move money.\n- Commission shall never be adjusted by hand to compensate for a missing rate or an incomplete status. Fix the status, the rate, or the pay type instead.\n- Hold shall be used for a pause; cancelled shall not.\n\n## Records\n\nEach action creates an appointment update, a status history entry, and an audit entry. Completion creates commission entries; no-show and inside-cutoff cancellation create fee invoices; take payment creates a payment record with its payment number and updates amount paid; notification actions stamp their sent-at times. These records are kept in the customer record system.\n\n## Escalation and review\n\nManagers review failed actions, disputed fees, and missing commissions. A fault that the store cannot resolve shall be reported to support@unsaidmemories.com. Legal process concerning an appointment, fee, or commission record shall be directed to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> System faults in appointment actions: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal process concerning an appointment record: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "customer-lifecycle": "\n# Customer Lifecycle\n\nThis standard governs the customer lifecycle in the customer record system: the stages, the health assessment behind them, how stages are recalculated, and how tags and lead sources are used alongside them. It applies to front desk staff, who read stages and set them by hand where the facts require, and to managers, who run recalculation and review its results before applying them. Lifecycle is the system's answer to where a customer stands with the business. The stages are not marketing labels; they are the working vocabulary for win-back, VIP treatment, and knowing who needs a call.\n\n## The stages\n\nThe stage is suggested from the customer's real behavior — visits, appointments, and expected cycle — as follows:\n\n- **New lead** — zero visits, zero appointments. The person exists — a signup, an abandoned booking, an inquiry — but has not come in. Abandoned-booking captures land here, under Rebooking and Automation (§4.2.7).\n- **New customer** — one visit. They are in the door; they have not formed the habit yet. This is the stage where the second booking matters most.\n- **Active** — visited within their expected cycle. The expected cycle comes from their visit history: a customer on a 6-week grooming cycle who came 5 weeks ago is active; at 8 weeks they are drifting.\n- **Returning** — comes back, but outside the expected cycle. Loyal, just irregular.\n- **Needs attention** — health has slipped to needs attention, but the customer is not far past cycle — under 1.5 times the expected gap. The nudge stage.\n- **At risk** — health is at risk: past 1.5 times the expected cycle, with signals stacking — missed documents, expiring vaccinations, unpaid balances, the same signals as the Needs Attention feed in CRM Dashboard (§4.2.1).\n- **Lapsed** — was needs attention, now past 1.5 times the cycle. Gone quiet, not gone.\n- **Lost** — was at risk, now past 3 times the expected cycle. Treat as win-back or release.\n- **VIP** — lifetime value of $1,000 or more **and** 8 visits or more. Both conditions are required. A $1,200 one-time buyer is not VIP; an 8-visit $900 regular is not either. The bar is deliberately high.\n\nHealth itself is healthy, needs attention, or at risk, computed with reasons. The scoring returns the reasons list, and a reason such as \"documents current\" shows that the health assessment looks at document state, not only visit recency.\n\n## Recalculating stages\n\nStages go stale. A new customer from March who now comes every 5 weeks is active, but nothing updates the field until recalculation runs. The recalculation scores every customer in the location's records and applies each suggestion to the lifecycle stage. It has two modes:\n\n- **Report-only run** — scores and reports without writing. The response lists every proposed change as old to new. Staff shall run this first, always. It shows exactly what a live run would do, customer by customer.\n- **Live run** — applies the changes. A suggestion of \"keep current stage\" is never written, so a live run touches only rows the scoring actually disagrees with.\n\nRun recalculation after any bulk change — an import, a merge cleanup, a period when bookings were not carried over — and on a cadence the manager sets; weekly is reasonable for an active salon. Staff shall not run it mid-day and act on the at-risk list in the same breath. Let the report sit, check the big movers by hand — a VIP dropping to lapsed deserves a human look before the system declares it — then apply.\n\nA stage set by scoring is a suggestion made durable, not a verdict. If staff know something the figures do not — the customer's dog has died, they are traveling for two months, they have moved — set the stage by hand. The scoring will leave a hand-set stage alone on the next run unless the figures disagree again. The scoring suggests; the desk decides.\n\n## Tags — the real segmentation\n\nThere are no prebuilt segment sets in the system. Segmentation is tags: tags assigned to customers, and tags assigned to pets. The tag list includes VIP, Breeder, Puppy, First Visit, Rescue Partner, Staff, Senior, Referral Partner, Special Handling, Nervous, and High Maintenance.\n\nTags and stages do different jobs. Stages are computed and temporary — they change as behavior changes. Tags are asserted and stay in place — a person, or an import, put them there, and they stay until a person removes them. \"Nervous\" on a pet is a tag because no scoring should ever remove it; at risk on the customer is a stage because it must track reality. Use them together: the win-back list is \"stage is at risk, no upcoming appointment,\" a filter on computed state; the handling list is \"tag is Special Handling,\" a filter on asserted state. The directory supports both. Staff shall clear filters when finished, because a forgotten filter is the leading cause of a customer appearing not to be in the system.\n\n## Lead sources\n\nLead sources are: Website, Google, Referral, Social Media, Phone, Walk-In, Campaign, Other. The source is set at creation; the abandoned-booking capture uses Website. This is attribution infrastructure, and it works only if the desk sets it at the first contact. \"How did you hear about us\" cannot be reconstructed six months later, and marketing spending decisions are made from these records. Ask the question, pick the source, move on.\n\n## The funnel view of lifecycle\n\nThe booking funnel in CRM Dashboard (§4.2.1) is the top of the lifecycle: website visit, account created, intake started, intake completed, booking started, booking completed. A lead that never gets past intake started is a different problem — process friction, or price surprise at the service step — from a lead that completes intake but never books, which points to deposit friction or slot availability. Staff shall read the funnel before blaming follow-up for a thin new-lead pipeline; the gap is usually in the flow, not the follow-up.\n\n## Controls\n\n- A live recalculation shall never be run before its report-only run has been reviewed.\n- A stage shall not be set by hand to flatter the figures. Hand-set stages record a fact the figures cannot see, and the reason shall be noted on the customer record under Notes (§4.2.8).\n- Tags that record a safety fact, such as Nervous or Special Handling, shall be removed only by a person who has verified the fact no longer applies.\n\n## Records\n\nEach customer record carries its lifecycle stage and the health assessment behind it. A recalculation run produces a report listing every change, old to new, before any change is applied. Tag assignments and lead source are kept on the customer and pet records in the customer record system.\n\n## Escalation and review\n\nManagers review recalculation reports, hand-set stages, and tag removals. A lifecycle fault that the store cannot resolve shall be reported to support@unsaidmemories.com. Legal process concerning a customer record shall be directed to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> System faults in lifecycle scoring: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal process concerning a customer record: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "rebooking-and-automation": "\n# Rebooking and Automation\n\nThis standard governs rebooking in the customer record system: the groomer's recommendation at checkout, the needs-rebooking worklist, and the capture of abandoned bookings as leads. It applies to groomers, who make the recommendation at the chair, to front desk staff, who enter it before the customer leaves and work the needs-rebooking list, and to managers, who review follow-up practice. The system provides both sides: the human side, the groomer's recommendation, and the system side — who is overdue, who abandoned, and who to contact. Customer contact under this standard shall respect the opt-in and do-not-contact flags on the record, under the Privacy Policy (§2.1.2).\n\n## Recommend Next Visit — the human side\n\nAt checkout, the groomer says \"see me in six weeks.\" That recommendation shall be recorded so it survives the conversation, using the Recommend Next Visit action on the customer's grooming history. The action records the pet, a recommended date in strict year-month-day format — any other format is refused — and a reason, and creates a rebooking recommendation with status open.\n\nStaff shall build the habit at the chair, not at the desk. The groomer knows the coat, the interval, and the customer; \"six weeks\" from the groomer carries weight a system reminder does not. The desk's duty is to ensure the recommendation is entered before the customer leaves, because a recommendation that lives only in conversation is gone by the parking lot.\n\nThe service record backs this up: each service carries a default rebooking interval in days. When the groomer does not specify, the default is the starting point — but the groomer's judgment prevails over it. A matted dog does not get the standard interval for its breed's usual groom.\n\n## The needs-rebooking list — the system side\n\nThe needs-rebooking list is the win-back worklist: one row per customer, showing last visit, next appointment, average visit frequency in days, and lifecycle stage. Read it as: people whose average frequency says they should have been back by now, and who have no next appointment. This is the list to work when the book has gaps; every call is to someone the data says is overdue, not a cold call.\n\nPair it with the lifecycle stages in Customer Lifecycle (§4.2.6): at risk with no next appointment is the urgent list; lapsed is the warm list; lost is the \"win-back offer or release\" list. Pair it also with open rebooking recommendations: a customer with an open recommendation whose date has passed calls for a specific, personal follow-up — naming the pet, the service, and the date it was due — which converts better than any general \"we miss you\" message.\n\n## Abandoned bookings — the leads that almost were\n\nNot every booking completes. When a booking is abandoned with the deposit paid, it is resolved through the deposit machinery — fee application, refund, or customer credit — under the accounting standards. When it is abandoned with the deposit not paid, there is no money to resolve, but there is a person: name, email, pet, and the service and slot they wanted. The abandoned-booking capture moves that person into the customer record system instead of letting the lead die with the session.\n\nThe capture rules are strict, because they protect against the two classic failures of lead capture — duplicates and consent violations:\n\n- **One record per email, without exception.** A second abandonment by the same person enriches the existing record; it never creates another. The system already carries duplicates from before this rule; the capture does not add to them. Duplicate handling is governed by Data Management (§4.2.9).\n- **The existing record is enriched, never downgraded.** The abandonment context is recorded as a system note naming the originating booking — what service was wanted, for which pet, on what date and time, and that the record's communication preferences shall be honored. The lifecycle stage is never moved backward, and communication preferences are never overridden. The note is written once per booking; a check on the booking reference prevents duplicates.\n- **New leads start at new lead**, with lead source Website where it can be determined. Every sending path checks the record's opt-in flags before sending; capture never opts anyone in.\n\nThe follow-up track for these leads is deliberately soft and occasional; the note on the record says so. One abandoned booking is not consent for a repeated campaign. A single well-timed \"still interested in that slot?\" is the standard, and the opt-in flags govern absolutely.\n\n## What automation means today\n\nThe scope shall be stated honestly, because the word is easily oversold. The system has the detection side — the needs-rebooking list, funnel events, abandoned-lead capture, and lifecycle scoring — and the sending channel, through communications and email, including the appointment reminder sends. It does not yet have a campaign administration side: no campaign membership records, no send scheduling, and no automation run history. Today's automation is therefore this: the system surfaces who to contact and why, and a person — or a simple reminder job — sends the message.\n\nThat means the rebooking habit is still a human habit, and this standard is written accordingly. Staff shall work the needs-rebooking list weekly, enter the groomer's recommendation at every checkout, and treat an abandoned booking as a lead, not a loss. When campaign administration is built, the habits are what will make it effective.\n\n## Controls\n\n- No customer shall be contacted under this standard in breach of the opt-in, do-not-call, or do-not-contact flags on their record.\n- A rebooking recommendation shall be entered before the customer leaves, or not presented later as if it had been made at checkout.\n- An abandoned-booking lead shall receive soft, occasional follow-up only. Repeated automated nudges to such a lead are prohibited.\n\n## Records\n\nEach recommendation creates a rebooking recommendation record with status open, the pet, the recommended date, and the reason. Abandoned-booking capture creates or enriches a customer record at new lead stage and writes a system note naming the originating booking. The needs-rebooking list is derived from visit and appointment records and creates no record of its own. These records are kept in the customer record system.\n\n## Escalation and review\n\nManagers review open recommendations whose dates have passed, abandoned-lead follow-up, and any contact made against a record's flags. A rebooking fault that the store cannot resolve shall be reported to support@unsaidmemories.com. Legal process concerning a customer record or a contact-preference complaint shall be directed to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> System faults in rebooking: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal process concerning a customer record: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "notes": "\n# Notes\n\nThis standard governs notes in the customer record system: the five note types, where a note is attached, pinning, and editing. It applies to front desk staff and groomers, who write the notes the next person will act on, and to managers, who review note quality. The records remember what happened; notes remember what it meant. A note is the difference between \"customer has a poodle\" and handling information that allows the dog to be groomed safely. Notes are business records. Staff shall write every note as if the next reader is themselves on a bad day, and shall handle note content under the Privacy Policy (§2.1.2).\n\n## The five types\n\nNote type is a fixed vocabulary — five values, no more:\n\n- **Internal** — staff eyes only. The candid record: billing disputes in progress, warnings, context a customer shall not see. Never shown to the customer.\n- **Customer visible** — the customer can see this. Pickup instructions, care notes, and observations such as a lump found that the customer should have checked by their veterinarian. Write it knowing the customer will read it: professional, specific, and with no shorthand they could misread.\n- **Appointment** — tied to one appointment. Day-of context: \"running 15 minutes late, called ahead,\" \"wants the summer cut this time.\" Useful at that appointment and noise everywhere else, which is why it is scoped to it.\n- **Pet handling** — tied to the pet. Handling and safety: temperament, triggers, and what works. This is the type that prevents bites. Anything that affects how a person touches the animal goes here, and goes here the day it is learned.\n- **System** — written by the system, not by staff. The abandoned-booking capture writes these, naming the originating booking. Staff shall not edit system notes; they are the audit trail of what the system did and why. If a system note is unclear, read its reference; it names the originating booking.\n\nPicking the wrong type is the most common notes mistake, and it has real consequences. An internal note about a billing dispute marked customer visible is a privacy incident. A handling warning recorded as an internal note on the appointment, instead of a pet handling note on the pet, will not surface at the next booking. When in doubt, answer two questions: who needs to see this, and when? The answers give the type.\n\n## Pinning\n\nA note may be pinned or unpinned with a single change. Pinned notes stay at the top. Staff shall pin the note the next person needs before they know they need it: the bite history, the \"do not book with\" constraint, the standing instruction. Unpin a note when it is no longer the most important thing; a profile with eleven pinned notes has no pinned notes. Review pins when the underlying fact changes, not on a schedule.\n\n## Editing\n\nNote text may be corrected. The rule is: fix facts, do not rewrite history. Correcting a pet's stated age is a fix. Rewriting a note recording that a customer disputed the wait into a note saying the customer was satisfied, after the fact, falsifies the record. If the situation resolved, add a new note saying so. The old note is what happened; the new note is what happened next.\n\n## Where notes live\n\nA note may be attached to a customer, a pet, an appointment, or a grooming record. That scoping decides where the note surfaces:\n\n- Attach it to the **pet** when it is about the animal — pet handling notes especially. Pet-scoped notes survive across bookings and surface at every future appointment.\n- Attach it to the **appointment** when it is about that visit. \"Running late\" belongs to that day, not to the pet forever.\n- Attach it to the **customer** when it is about the person: billing, communication preferences, \"prefers text over calls.\"\n- The **grooming record** attachment is for post-visit notes — behavior observed during the groom, coat condition, what was done differently.\n\nNotes are also written automatically: appointment actions record customer notes, and the booking carry-over brings customer notes from the booking onto the appointment. If a note appears that staff did not write, check its type: system means the system wrote it, and the text usually says why.\n\n## The notes that matter most\n\nIf staff write nothing else, they shall write these:\n\n1. **Handling warnings** (pet handling, pinned). Bite history, triggers, what calms the animal. This is safety equipment made of text.\n2. **Standing grooming instructions** (pet handling or customer visible). Blade numbers, cut preferences, exclusions. Whether the third groom matches the first is the difference between a regular who stays and a regular who leaves.\n3. **Billing context** (internal). Disputed charges, promised callbacks, manager-approved discounts. The next person to touch the money needs the story.\n4. **Communication preferences** (internal, on the customer). \"Text, do not call.\" \"Email only on weekdays.\" Sending paths honor these only if they are written down.\n\nA profile with no notes is a stranger the system has met before. Staff shall not let that happen on their watch.\n\n## Controls\n\n- An internal note shall never be marked customer visible. If a note's content could embarrass the business or disclose another matter if read by the customer, it is internal, or it shall not be written.\n- A handling warning shall be recorded the day it is learned, on the pet, and pinned where the next groomer needs it first.\n- System notes shall not be edited by staff.\n- Notes shall record facts. Speculation about a customer, and personal remarks unrelated to service, shall not be entered; notes are business records and may be produced in response to legal process.\n\n## Records\n\nEach note is kept in the customer record system with its type, text, pin state, and its attachment to a customer, pet, appointment, or grooming record. Edited notes and system notes are kept as part of the same record set.\n\n## Escalation and review\n\nManagers review pinned notes, handling warnings, and any note disclosed to a customer in error. A misdirected customer-visible note shall be reported to a manager at once. A notes fault that the store cannot resolve shall be reported to support@unsaidmemories.com. Legal process concerning a note or customer record shall be directed to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> System faults in notes: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal process concerning a customer record: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "data-management": "\n# Data Management\n\nThis standard governs data quality in the customer record system: the one-person-one-row rule, how duplicates arise, the merge procedure, the search-first discipline, periodic review, and how the directory's figures are to be read. It applies to front desk staff, who prevent duplicates daily and carry out merges, and to managers, who approve doubtful merges and review data quality monthly. The system enforces the rule on every write path — find or create by exact email, so the same person booking twice lands on the same customer row. The desk's duty is everything the system cannot enforce: catching the duplicates that slip in through doors the matching cannot see, and merging them without losing history. Customer data is handled under the Privacy Policy (§2.1.2), for the single purpose of serving the customer; no export is permitted without authorization.\n\n## How duplicates get in\n\nThe matching is exact-email. Everything else is a door:\n\n- **No email at creation.** A walk-in booked without an email cannot be matched later. When they book online next month with an email, that is a second row. This is the most common door, and the fix is at intake, not in the records afterward.\n- **Spelling variants.** Two spellings of one name are the same human and different text; the matching cannot join them.\n- **Nickname against legal name.** The booking says one form, the register says another.\n- **The older-records split.** A record created in the older booking-side customers set and one created directly in the customer record system for the same person, where the carry-over never linked them — usually, again, because of the email.\n\nNone of these is anyone's fault; they are the ordinary disorder of a busy desk. Creating one is not the failure. Leaving it is.\n\n## The merge\n\nA merged record is marked with a pointer to the record it was merged into. Staff shall carry out a merge in this order:\n\n1. **Decide the survivor.** Usually the row with the most history — appointments, payments, notes. Open both profiles side by side and compare before touching anything.\n2. **Re-point the linked records.** The duplicate's pets, appointments, notes, and payments move to the survivor. This is the step that matters: a merge that moves the customer row but orphans the pets has made things worse.\n3. **Stamp the pointer.** The retired row is marked as merged into the survivor. Nothing is truly deleted; the audit trail stays intact.\n4. **Reconcile conflicts by hand.** Two phone numbers, two addresses, different spellings of the name — the system cannot decide these. Staff can. Call the customer if necessary; a wrong guess propagates everywhere the record goes.\n5. **Verify afterward.** Open the survivor and check: pets present, appointment history complete, notes intact, lifetime value sensible. Then search for the duplicate's name and confirm only one row answers.\n\nMerging is corrective work. When in doubt, do not merge; ask a manager. An unmerged duplicate is messy; a wrong merge is destructive.\n\n## The search-first discipline\n\nPrevention beats correction. Before creating any customer, staff shall search:\n\n1. The **email** first — exact match is the matching key.\n2. Then the **phone** — the most reliable key when the email is unknown.\n3. Then the **last name** — catches the spelling variants.\n\nThirty seconds, every time, with no exceptions for \"I'm sure they're new.\" Certainty is how duplicates are born.\n\n## Periodic review\n\nOnce a month — more often for a high-volume desk — staff shall sort the directory by last name and scan for near-matches: the pairs the daily discipline missed, such as the same phone with different names, the same address with different emails, and the reused-pet-name problem at the customer level, described in Customer Profile (§4.2.3). Merge promptly. Every day a duplicate lives, its history splits further across the two rows and the merge gets harder.\n\n## What the figures can and cannot tell you\n\nThe directory's aggregates are honest but limited, and data management means knowing the limits:\n\n- **Lifetime value $0** usually means the ledger has no payments tagged with that customer's identifier, not that they have never paid. Staff shall not \"fix\" it by hand; fix the payment linkage going forward.\n- **Outstanding balance $0 across the board** is the current truth, not a fault. When balances begin flowing — fee invoices, payment plans — this figure becomes the desk's money list; keep it clean by collecting against invoices promptly.\n- **A customer missing from the customer record system but present in the older records** is almost always the no-email case, under Customers (§4.2.2). Add the email to the older record and let the carry-over bring it across; do not create a fresh row by hand and end up with two.\n\n## Controls\n\n- No customer record shall be exported, in whole or in part, without authorization. Exports, where authorized, are for the single purpose stated in the authorization.\n- Staff shall not keep customer data in spreadsheets or other parallel systems. Under the standing rule, an empty record set means its connection is not built, never that it is unused. The vaccination record set has its structure but no rows; the entry path is not built yet. That is a gap to flag, not permission to track vaccinations in a spreadsheet. Spreadsheets become parallel systems, and parallel systems become the duplicate factory. If the record set exists, the data goes in it; if the entry path does not exist yet, say so plainly so it gets built.\n- A doubtful merge shall be referred to a manager rather than guessed.\n\n## Records\n\nA merge is recorded by the merged-into pointer on the retired row, with the survivor's re-pointed pet, appointment, note, and payment links. Search-first checks, periodic reviews, and authorized exports shall be carried out as duties under this standard; the customer records themselves are kept in the customer record system.\n\n## Escalation and review\n\nManagers review the monthly duplicate scan, doubtful merges, and any request to export customer data. A data fault that the store cannot resolve shall be reported to support@unsaidmemories.com. Legal process concerning customer data, including requests under the Privacy Policy (§2.1.2), shall be directed to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> System faults in customer data: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal process concerning customer data: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "accounting-dashboard": "\n# Accounting Dashboard\n\nThis standard governs the Accounting Dashboard, the read-only summary the Accounting Operator uses to start each working day in the accounting system of UnSaid Memories, LLC. The dashboard moves no money and edits no record. It answers one question — what needs attention right now — across receivables, cash, payables, payouts, and exceptions, and every figure on it leads to the underlying record where the work is done. The Accounting Manager reviews the dashboard weekly and owns the month-end close that the dashboard supports.\n\n## The five blocks\n\n1. **Outstanding receivables.** Every unpaid invoice, aged by due date: current, 30 days late, and older. The aging is the substance of the block; a receivables total without aging states nothing useful. One large invoice long past due is a collections problem; many small current invoices are ordinary business.\n2. **Cash position.** Money that has arrived, stated separately from money that is only promised. A payment recorded as succeeded is cash. A payment in pending is not cash, and the two are never combined into one figure.\n3. **Payables due.** What the Company owes, to whom, and when it is due. A vendor payment is never first discovered to be late from the vendor.\n4. **Payouts in flight.** What is scheduled to leave and what has arrived. Seller payouts settle on a 7–14 day rolling delay; a payout whose state has not moved past its scheduled date is investigated at the payment processor, not in the books, because the books record only what happened. Seller payout obligations are governed by Payments (§4.3.4).\n5. **Exceptions.** Failed payments, unapplied deposits, unreconciled bank items, and refunds awaiting approval. The other four blocks are information; the exceptions block is work.\n\n> [!NOTICE]\n> The exceptions block surfaces only the problems it is built to detect. A refund issued outside the Platform, or a posting that failed without an error reaching the operator, appears nowhere on the dashboard. Troubleshooting (§4.3.15) governs the checks for the problems the dashboard cannot see.\n\n## Start-of-day routine\n\nThe Accounting Operator works the dashboard in this order each working day:\n\n1. Check the cash position first. Confirm that the previous day's card payments settled, and that no payment remains in pending longer than the processor's normal authorization window.\n2. Resolve every stale pending payment. A payment stuck in pending is either a failed capture to retry or a customer to contact; pending is a state to resolve, not a state to monitor.\n3. Scan receivables. Send a reminder from the invoice screen for every past-due invoice, following Invoices (§4.3.5). Before the first reminder on any invoice, verify the billing contact; a reminder sent to an address that receives nothing is not a contact, whatever the reminder count on the record says.\n4. Treat repeated reminders without payment as a collections decision, not a reminder problem, and refer the invoice to the Accounting Manager.\n5. Review payouts in flight for any payout past its scheduled date.\n6. Work the exceptions block from top to bottom until it is clear or every item carries a named next step.\n\n## Reading the blocks together\n\n1. Compare cash position with payouts in flight before relying on either: a payout schedule built on pending payments is a shortfall waiting to happen.\n2. Compare receivables aging with the exceptions block: an overdue invoice with no recent reminder is not being worked, whatever the receivables total says.\n3. Compare payables due with cash position weekly: what must go out against what has actually come in is the weekly solvency check.\n\n## Controls\n\n- The dashboard is read-only. Every correction is made on the record's own screen — the invoice, the payment, the refund — so that every correction leaves its own record of who acted and when.\n- No operator acts on a dashboard total without opening the underlying records. A total that appears sound can conceal one large invoice masking many small overdue ones; the composition is where the problems are.\n- The month-to-date revenue figure on the dashboard is directional only. The authoritative figures are the Profit & Loss and the Trial Balance in Reports (§4.3.14), which read posted journal entries only. Where the dashboard and a report disagree, the report governs, and the usual cause is unposted activity, addressed in Troubleshooting (§4.3.15).\n- Separation of duties applies to everything the dashboard surfaces: the person who records a payment does not reconcile it. Reconciliation is governed by Banking (§4.3.10).\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nUse of the dashboard creates no records. The records it reads — invoices, payments, refunds, deposits, payouts, bank accounts, and payables — are created and kept in their own parts of the accounting system under Invoices (§4.3.5), Payments (§4.3.4), Deposits (§4.3.6), and Purchases (§4.3.11), and are retained under the Regulatory Record Retention Policy (§2.9.28).\n\n## Escalation\n\nOperational problems the operator cannot resolve — a payment the processor shows captured with no Platform record, an exception that recurs after correction — go to support@unsaidmemories.com. Legal process, including any demand or notice touching a receivable or a payout, goes to legal@unsaidmemories.com on the day it is received.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Accounting Operator works the dashboard daily. The Accounting Manager reviews the exceptions pattern weekly, and reviews this standard whenever a new exception type is added to the dashboard or a recurring exception shows the routine is missing a class of problem.\n",
  "banking": "\n# Banking\n\nThis standard governs banking in the accounting system of UnSaid Memories, LLC: the bank accounts the business operates, the transactions that flow through them, the payouts that leave them, and the reconciliation that proves the books and the bank statements agree. Every other standard in this section records what should have happened; banking proves what did. The Accounting Operator prepares reconciliations and works payout records; the Accounting Manager approves each reconciliation, and — under the separation of duties stated below — never reconciles an account whose payments the manager recorded.\n\n## Bank accounts\n\nEach bank account record carries the institution, the account name, the account type, the currency, and the opening balance with its date.\n\n1. The opening balance is the anchor every reconciliation starts from. The operator verifies it against the actual statement on the day the account is set up; an opening balance wrong by a dollar leaves every later reconciliation wrong by a dollar.\n2. An account may be marked as the default payout account and as the primary account. Only accounts marked reconcilable enter the reconciliation procedure, so that a petty-cash holding is never drawn into a bank reconciliation.\n\nBanking credentials are never shared between operators, never recorded in an account record, a memo, or any other document, and never sent by email or message. Access is individual, and a change of role or a departure ends it the same day, under Admin Users (§4.4.2).\n\nThe connected processor accounts are the other half of banking: for each tenant, which connected account belongs to it, whether charging and payouts are enabled, and whether onboarding is complete. The business and every franchise tenant link their own bank through the processor's onboarding; bank details are collected and held by the processor and are never typed into or stored in the Platform. Where a tenant cannot receive payouts, the operator checks the onboarding flags first, under Payments (§4.3.4).\n\n## Bank transactions\n\nThe bank transaction record is the statement side of the reconciliation: date, description, amount, type (deposit, withdrawal, transfer, fee, interest), whether it has been matched, and the books entry it matched to. Bank transactions are facts from the bank; journal entries are facts from the books. Reconciliation is the act of proving they describe the same events.\n\nA fee or interest line from the bank requires its own journal entry — the bank charged a monthly fee, and the books shall show it. No operational event in the Platform creates these entries, so they are easy to forget. The month-end close includes a standing step: review the bank's fee and interest lines and post each one.\n\n## Reconciliation\n\nThe reconcile action compares the books balance for an account against the statement balance for a period, computes outstanding deposits and outstanding payments, and derives the adjusted balance. It records the reconciliation as complete **only when the statement-minus-books difference is exactly zero** — not approximately zero, and never by means of an invented balancing entry. A one-cent difference is an unreconciled period.\n\nThe operator works a difference in this order:\n\n1. **Outstanding items** — deposits recorded in the books but not yet on the statement, and payments recorded but not yet cleared. These are timing differences, and the reconciliation arithmetic accounts for them.\n2. **Bank-only items** — fees, interest, and automatic charges the books never saw. Post them, then run the reconciliation again.\n3. **Book-only items** — a journal entry with no real-world counterpart, such as a refund recorded through the administrative action whose processor transfer failed. Reverse or correct it under Books (§4.3.2).\n4. **Wrong-account postings** — the known hazard: a shop sale posts to checking, but the refund action credits cash. A refund on a card sale leaves the books' checking balance wrong by the refund amount while the bank is right. Move the refund entry to checking by correcting entry.\n\nReconcile monthly at minimum, and weekly for the operating account. A reconciliation three months behind is archaeology, not accounting.\n\n## Payouts\n\nPayout records track money leaving the business — Seller payouts, staff payouts, vendor payouts, refunds, and transfers — each with its type, amount, destination, scheduled and processed dates, and the processor payout or transfer identifier tying it back to the processor. The Seller payout record adds the period, gross sales, fees, refunds, adjustments, and net amount, and records any transfer reversal and its amount against the payout it came from, so a dispute clawback is traceable to the payout it reduced; see Refunds and Disputes (§4.3.7).\n\n1. Seller payouts settle on a 7–14 day rolling delay, under Payments (§4.3.4). Payout states — scheduled, processing, paid, failed, cancelled — show where each payout stands in that window.\n2. A payout whose state has not moved past its scheduled date is investigated at the processor, not in the books: the books record only what happened.\n3. Failed payouts are the urgent ones: wrong bank details, a closed account, a capability freeze during a dispute investigation. The destination and the failure reason are on the record. The operator corrects the cause and then re-issues; a failed record is never edited into a success.\n4. \"Paid\" means the processor accepted the instruction; the money then travels on the processor's timeline. Where a Seller reports a paid payout never arrived, the operator checks the payout identifier in the processor's console before treating the Platform as having failed.\n\n## The payout-account discipline\n\nEach tenant's default payout account is where its Seller payouts land. Confirming the payout account is part of a franchise tenant's go-live checks: a tenant with charges enabled and no payout account set is collecting money with nowhere for it to go. The Platform never routes one tenant's payouts through another tenant's bank account; payout records are kept per tenant precisely so that cannot happen by accident. If two tenants' payouts ever appear mixed, that is a defect in the payout run, not an operator error: the operator stops the run and escalates the same day, and does not attempt to repair it by editing payout records.\n\n## Failure modes\n\n1. **Reconciliation out by a round number.** A missing entry, not a typing error. Round-number differences are whole transactions; search for an unposted refund, an unposted fee, or a duplicate entry before hunting for keying errors.\n2. **Reconciliation out by a small amount.** Usually bank-only fees or interest the books missed, or the known cash-and-checking refund-account mismatch. Post the missing items; never force the difference.\n3. **Payout marked paid, Seller reports nothing arrived.** See the payout procedure above: check the processor first.\n\n## Controls\n\n- **Separation of duties:** the person who records a payment does not reconcile it. The operator who prepares a reconciliation is not the operator who recorded the payments in the period reconciled, and the Accounting Manager approves the completed reconciliation.\n- No reconciliation is completed with a difference, and no balancing entry is ever posted to make a reconciliation agree. A difference is worked until its cause is found and recorded.\n- Banking credentials are never shared and never recorded in a document, a record, or a message; see the bank accounts procedure above.\n- A closed period is never altered. A banking correction belonging to a closed period is posted as a reversing or correcting entry in the open period, under Books (§4.3.2).\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nBank account records, bank transaction records, reconciliation records, and payout records are kept in the accounting system. Reconciliations and the statements they were proved against are retained under the Regulatory Record Retention Policy (§2.9.28).\n\n## Escalation\n\nA reconciliation difference that survives the procedure above, mixed payouts between tenants, or a suspected unauthorized banking change goes to the Accounting Manager the same day. Suspected fraud and any legal process touching a bank account go to legal@unsaidmemories.com on the day they arise. Processor or connection failures go to support@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nReconciliation is monthly at minimum and weekly for the operating account. The Accounting Manager reviews failed payouts weekly, reviews banking access quarterly under Admin Users (§4.4.2), and reviews this standard annually.\n",
  "purchases": "\n# Purchases\n\nThis standard governs purchases in the accounting system of UnSaid Memories, LLC — the buy side of the books: what the business owes its suppliers, when it is due, and what was paid. The sale side of the Platform is heavily automated; the buy side is where the Accounting Operator does the most manual work, because supplier invoices arrive as documents in an inbox, not as system notices. The Accounting Manager approves bill payments and owns the payables aging review.\n\n## The two supplier realities\n\nThe Platform sells from two suppliers — PetDropshipper and CJ Dropshipping — and the buy side differs for each. Dropship supplier obligations are typically settled per order: the Company pays the wholesale cost when the customer order is placed. That money moves as order-level payments, not as supplier bills. The payables procedure below governs obligations that do run as bills — wholesale invoices, freight bills, service vendors, rent, and utilities. When dropship settlement moves onto billed terms, such as a monthly supplier statement, those statements enter here.\n\nThe pricing rule ties the buy side to the sell side: the displayed price is supplier cost multiplied by 1.55 plus shipping, computed once when the product is written. The supplier cost is the buy-side fact the whole pricing model rests on. Where a supplier's cost changes and the stored price does not, margin erodes silently. The Accounting Operator's buy-side duty includes flagging cost changes that should trigger repricing: the books know what was paid, and the catalog needs to hear about it. See Sales (§4.3.3).\n\n## Bills\n\nA bill is the supplier's invoice entered as a payable: bill number, vendor, bill date and due date, lines with quantities, unit prices, and expense accounts, tax, total, amount paid, and state.\n\n1. Enter bills when they arrive, not when they are paid. A bill entered on arrival is a liability the aging can see; a bill entered at payment time is a surprise.\n2. The bill date is the invoice date, not the entry date. Dating the entry to the invoice keeps the expense in the right period.\n3. The due date drives the payables aging, so it is entered from the invoice, not from memory.\n4. Each bill line carries the expense account it posts to. As with invoice lines on the sell side, this is what makes the Profit & Loss right: a freight bill coded to cost of goods sold and a freight bill coded to shipping expense tell different stories about margin. Code deliberately.\n\n**New vendors.**\n\n1. Set up the vendor record before the first bill: legal name, remit-to address, payment terms, and tax identifier where required.\n2. Guard against duplicates by tax identifier, not by name — names vary, tax identifiers do not. A bill entered against a misspelled or duplicate vendor splits the payables history and corrupts year-end information reporting, under Taxes (§4.3.13).\n\n## Paying bills\n\n1. Record each payment with its amount, method, date, and reference — the check number or transfer confirmation, the thing the vendor will cite when they say they did not receive it.\n2. Payments apply to bills through payment applications, which support partial application: a payment against part of a bill leaves the balance owing and a clear application record.\n3. A bill shall never be marked paid without a payment record. The paid mark is a statement of fact, and the payment record is the proof.\n\nThe payables aging is the buy-side counterpart of the receivables aging: what the Company owes, to whom, and when. The Accounting Operator works it weekly. A vendor paid late twice starts adding late fees; a vendor paid late habitually starts shipping late.\n\n## Purchase orders\n\nWhere the business uses purchase orders, the order is the commitment and the bill is the fulfilment.\n\n1. Convert the purchase order to a bill when the goods arrive and the supplier invoices. The conversion carries the lines across, so the bill can be matched against what was ordered and what was received.\n2. Match all three: order, receipt, and bill. A bill for goods never ordered is either a duplicate invoice or fraud, and the purchase order is what allows the operator to tell the difference.\n\n## The month-end buy-side checklist\n\n1. All bills for the period entered — check the inbox, not only the system. A bill nobody entered is a liability nobody sees.\n2. All payments applied to their bills.\n3. The payables aging agrees with the payables control account. Where the detail and the books disagree, find the bill that posted to the wrong account before closing.\n4. Accrue what arrived without a bill: goods received but not yet invoiced are still a liability at month-end. Post an accrual entry — expense debited, accrued payables credited — and reverse it in the following month when the bill arrives, so the period stays honest.\n\n## Failure modes\n\n1. **A vendor says unpaid; the books say paid.** The payment record's reference decides: check number, transfer confirmation, date. Where the reference is real, the vendor mislaid it — send the proof. Where the reference is missing or vague, the payment may never have happened; verify with the bank before paying again.\n2. **Duplicate bill.** The same supplier invoice entered twice, usually once from the emailed copy and once from the paper copy. Match on vendor and bill number before entering, every time. A duplicate bill paid twice is cash out the door that takes months to recover.\n3. **Bill paid, expense in the wrong period.** The bill date drives the period, not the entry date. A December invoice entered in January with a January date overstates January expense and understates December. Correct the bill date — or accrue at close — rather than moving the entry.\n4. **Supplier cost changed, price did not.** When a supplier invoice shows a cost increase on a product the Platform sells, the operator flags it for repricing. The pricing rule is cost multiplied by 1.55 plus shipping; where cost moved and the stored price did not, every sale since has been at the old margin. This is the Accounting Operator's most valuable buy-side habit.\n\n## Controls\n\n- Separation of duties: the person who enters a bill does not approve its payment, and the person who records a payment does not reconcile the bank account it left, under Banking (§4.3.10).\n- No bill is paid twice: the vendor-and-bill-number match in the failure modes above is a control, performed before entry, not a courtesy.\n- Banking credentials used to make vendor payments are never shared and never recorded in a bill, a payment record, or any other document.\n- Bills are never deleted to correct them. A wrong bill is reversed or credited on the record, so the payables history stays complete, under Books (§4.3.2).\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nBill records and their lines, vendor records, payment records and payment applications, purchase orders and their conversions, and accrual entries are kept in the accounting system and retained under the Regulatory Record Retention Policy (§2.9.28).\n\n## Escalation\n\nA bill for goods never ordered, a suspected duplicate payment, or a vendor dispute over payment goes to the Accounting Manager the same day. Suspected supplier fraud and legal process go to legal@unsaidmemories.com. System failures in bill entry or conversion go to support@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Accounting Operator works the payables aging weekly and completes the buy-side checklist at each month-end close. The Accounting Manager reviews vendor records for duplicates quarterly and reviews this standard annually.\n",
  "payroll": "\n# Payroll\n\nThis standard governs payroll in the accounting system of UnSaid Memories, LLC: what the payroll run computes, what it deliberately does not compute, and the procedures that close those gaps. Payroll is the least forgiving workflow in accounting — the amounts must be exact, the timing is set by law, and the records are legal documents. The Payroll Administrator runs payroll and maintains employee pay profiles; the Accounting Manager approves each run and posts its journal entry; managers approve their teams' timesheets. Payroll records are confidential and are handled under the confidentiality control below. Payroll tax records are kept under Taxes (§4.3.13) and the Marketplace Facilitator Tax Policy (§2.1.17) does not govern them; employer payroll obligations are the Company's own.\n\n## What the payroll run does — and does not do\n\nThe run-payroll action computes a pay run from approved timesheets: each employee's gross pay from their time entries multiplied by their profile rate, plus any commissions moved to paid state for the period. It writes the run record, the per-employee run items, and the deductions. The run record carries the period, the totals (gross, net, tax withheld), the run's state, and the approval record.\n\nTwo limitations are stated in the run procedure itself, and every step below is built around them:\n\n1. **No withholding computation.** Tax withheld is always zero; the run does not compute federal, state, or local withholding. Net pay shall therefore be computed outside the Platform — by the external payroll provider or from accountant-prepared figures — and the Platform records the run summary. The run's net figure shall never be presented or paid as actual net pay: with zero withholding, it overstates what the employee receives and leaves the tax liability unfunded.\n2. **No books posting.** The run writes operational records and posts no journal entry. Every payroll run therefore requires a manual journal entry: wage expense debited, payroll liabilities credited (taxes payable, benefits payable), and cash credited for net pay. Without it, labor cost never reaches the Profit & Loss and the business appears more profitable than it is. This is the single most important payroll procedure on the Platform today.\n\nUntil both limitations are resolved, payroll runs gross-to-net through the external payroll provider, and the Platform records the run summary and the real withheld figures once the provider's computation replaces the run's placeholders.\n\n## The pay calendar\n\n1. Payroll runs on a calendar the business sets — weekly, biweekly, or semimonthly — and the calendar is a commitment. Employees plan their lives around payday; moving it casually destroys trust faster than any pay error.\n2. Publish the pay calendar for the full year: pay period end dates, run dates, pay dates, and the holiday adjustments. Where a pay date falls on a bank holiday, the pay date moves earlier, never later, and the moved date is communicated before the period starts, not after.\n3. Each run's manual journal entry posts in the period the wages were earned, not the period they were paid. A run whose pay date falls in the following month still accrues the earned portion to the current month at close; otherwise a month containing three paydays appears heavily overstaffed and the next appears impossibly lean.\n\n## New hires and terminations\n\n**New hires.**\n\n1. Complete the new hire's profile before their first run: rate, pay type, start date, withholding elections, and deduction enrolments.\n2. Build the profile on the start date, not the first pay date. A hire who works before their profile exists is paid through a correction run — and a correction run for a first paycheck is the worst possible introduction to the Company's payroll.\n\n**Terminations.**\n\n1. Handle terminations the same day: final timesheet approved, final pay computed under the state's final-paycheck rules — some states require payment the same day or the next day, and the Payroll Administrator confirms the rule that applies — deductions stopped, and system access revoked under Admin Users (§4.4.2).\n2. An employee who has left and remains active in payroll is a ghost-paycheck risk; a final paycheck computed late is a compliance violation. Neither is an edge case; both are handled in the termination checklist, not from memory.\n\n## The run procedure\n\n**Before the run:**\n\n1. Confirm all timesheets for the period are approved. Unapproved time does not compute; the Payroll Administrator pursues the approving managers and does not run without it.\n2. Verify the commission records moved to paid state for the period, and reconcile them against the run items before the run is finalized: a commission moved to paid but not in the run, or in the run but not moved, is a missed payment or a double payment waiting to happen.\n3. Confirm employee profiles are current. A rate change effective this period shall be entered before the run, not after; a rate change entered after the run means a correction run.\n\n**During the run:**\n\n1. Execute the run for the pay period.\n2. Verify the run totals against a spot check: select three employees and compute gross by hand from their timesheets. Where the spot check fails, the run fails; run items are never adjusted by hand to make them agree.\n\n**After the run:**\n\n1. Post the manual journal entry — wage expense, payroll liabilities, cash — because the run does not post one.\n2. Compute actual withholding through the external provider and record the real withheld figures against the run, replacing the run's zero-withholding placeholders.\n3. Release pay only after the Accounting Manager has approved the run and the journal entry is posted.\n\n## Deductions and tax forms\n\n1. The deductions record carries each employee's deductions — benefits, garnishments, retirement — with type, amount, and effective dates. A deduction without an end date runs forever; the Payroll Administrator reviews active deductions quarterly, because the most common payroll error after wrong rates is a deduction that should have stopped.\n2. Tax form records track what has been issued and filed: W-2s, 1099s, and quarterly filings. The filing calendar is set by law, not by operations — a missed filing deadline is a penalty, not an inconvenience. The filing calendar is kept separate from the pay calendar and reviewed monthly, under Taxes (§4.3.13).\n\n## Controls\n\n- **Payroll confidentiality.** Payroll records — rates, gross and net pay, deductions, garnishments, and tax forms — are confidential. Access is limited to the Payroll Administrator, the Accounting Manager, and the employee's own record for the employee concerned. Payroll figures are never recorded in a shared document, a general memo, or any record outside the payroll system, and are never disclosed to another employee.\n- Separation of duties: the person who approves timesheets does not run payroll for those timesheets, and the person who runs payroll does not reconcile the bank account the pay leaves, under Banking (§4.3.10).\n- A completed run is a legal record and is never edited. Corrections are made by a new correction run; the original run and the correction together are the record.\n- Banking credentials for payroll disbursement are never shared and never recorded in a payroll record or any other document.\n- Where a deduction that should have stopped is found: stop it, compute the total over-withheld, and refund or credit the employee — documented on the record, never netted silently against the next run.\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nPayroll run records and run items, pay period records, deduction records, commission records, and payroll tax form records are kept in the accounting system, under the confidentiality control above, and retained under the Regulatory Record Retention Policy (§2.9.28).\n\n## Escalation\n\nA wrong payment, a missed final-paycheck deadline, a garnishment question, or any legal process touching payroll — a levy, a wage claim, a subpoena — goes to legal@unsaidmemories.com on the day it arises. Payroll system failures go to support@unsaidmemories.com. Employees' questions about their own pay go to the Payroll Administrator, who answers from the run record.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Payroll Administrator reviews active deductions quarterly and the filing calendar monthly. The Accounting Manager verifies, at each month-end close, that every payroll run in the period has a matching journal entry — checking by run identifier, not by total, because a total can agree while one run is missing and another is doubled. This standard is reviewed annually.\n",
  "taxes": "\n# Taxes\n\nThis standard governs tax configuration and tax evidence in the accounting system of UnSaid Memories, LLC. The configuration — codes, rates, jurisdictions, and rules — determines what the system calculates on every sale and purchase. The evidence — what was collected, what was paid, and what was filed — is what the Company shows an examiner. Configuration set correctly once lets the evidence accumulate correctly on its own; configuration set wrong means every transaction in the period needs re-examination. The Accounting Operator maintains the day-to-day tax records and reconciles before filing; the Accounting Manager owns the configuration, the filing calendar, and every filing. The Company's tax position is governed by the Marketplace Facilitator Tax Policy (§2.1.17) and the tax documents in §2.9 of this library; this standard states the accounting procedure under them and is not tax advice. No rate is stated in this standard: rates come from the configuration and from the tax computation service, never from this document.\n\n## How sales tax works online: the tax computation service\n\nOnline sales tax is computed by the processor's tax service, not by the Platform's own tables. The service is active on the account, the head office is registered in Bartlett, Tennessee, and the account default is **tax-inclusive pricing**: the price the customer sees is the final price, with sales tax inside it. Every checkout line declares tax-inclusive treatment, and the checkout session enables automatic tax calculation, so the service computes the included tax from the billing or shipping address the customer provides.\n\nTwo operational consequences follow:\n\n1. **The price never changes at checkout because of tax.** A product priced at a given amount is that amount at the register and online; the service determines how much of that amount is tax. Where a customer asks how much tax they paid, the answer is inside the price, and the sale's tax record shows the division.\n2. **A tax-service failure never blocks a sale.** If the tax calculation fails, checkout retries without the tax parameters. The fallback is deliberate. A sale completed during a tax-service outage may carry no calculated tax; those sales require review, because the tax obligation did not disappear with the error.\n\nThe payment notice then posts the books entry from the computed totals: checking debited for the full amount, revenue credited net of tax, and **sales tax payable credited for the tax portion — only where tax was actually collected**. A zero-tax sale posts no tax line; that is correct behaviour, not a missing posting. It also means the tax-payable balance reflects only what the service computed. Where the service computed nothing because it had failed, the payable is understated and the obligation still exists.\n\nPer-sale tax records — the code, jurisdiction, rate, taxable amount, and tax amount calculated on each sale line — are written by the register for counter sales. Where a customer questions the tax on a receipt, those records are the answer: which code applied, at what rate, on what amount. See Register (§4.3.9).\n\n## The Platform's tax configuration\n\nThe Platform's own tax configuration covers everything the tax computation service does not: counter sales, invoices, and purchases.\n\n1. **Tax codes** define each tax the business handles: the code, the jurisdiction, the type, the rate, whether it compounds, the effective dates, and the payable and receivable accounts. The effective dates matter: a rate change mid-year means transactions before and after the change calculate differently, and the date range makes that happen correctly.\n2. When a jurisdiction changes a rate, the operator updates the effective dates; the rate is never edited in place, or history is recalculated.\n3. **Jurisdictions** define where: country, state, locality, and the business's registration number in each. A jurisdiction record is required everywhere the business has nexus — everywhere it must collect, file, or remit. Nexus is a legal determination, not an accounting one; the accounting system's duty is to reflect the jurisdictions the business has determined it operates in, accurately and completely.\n4. **Calculation rules** drive automatic computation per jurisdiction and tax type.\n\nThe Accounting Manager reviews the configuration quarterly at minimum, and immediately when the Company begins selling into a new state or a jurisdiction changes its rates.\n\n## Marketplace facilitator position\n\nFor marketplace sales, the Company collects and remits sales tax as the marketplace facilitator where the law requires it, under the Marketplace Facilitator Tax Policy (§2.1.17) and the tax documents in §2.9: the checkout calculates it, the sale records it, and the remittance procedure pays it. The operator's duty is not to decide the tax law; it is to make sure the configuration reflects the current rules, the calculations ran on every taxable sale, and the collected amounts reconcile to what was remitted. A growing gap between tax collected and tax remitted is either a timing difference — normal, where it clears monthly — or a configuration error, which is not normal and is investigated immediately.\n\n## Filing discipline\n\nTax filings are the output: what the business reported to each authority, when, and the confirmation. Payroll tax forms — W-2s, 1099s — are governed by Payroll (§4.3.12); this standard covers the business's own filings: sales tax returns, business tax filings, and information returns.\n\n1. **Reconcile before filing, every time.** The return's figures shall agree with the tax collected in the system for the period. Where the return and the system differ by any amount, the operator finds the cause before filing — a transaction in the wrong period, or a rounding difference that must be understood. Filing with an unexplained difference is how notices begin.\n2. The reconciliation has three parts: the tax service's computed totals, the Platform's sales-tax-payable balance, and the return. All three shall agree; where they do not, the disagreement is the work.\n3. File by each jurisdiction's deadline — the deadlines do not align, and each is calendared separately — and keep the confirmation and the filed return together with the underlying detail. A filed return without supporting detail is a claim the Company cannot defend.\n\n## Failure modes\n\n1. **Tax collected but never remitted.** The payable balance grows month after month. The cause is procedural, not technical: nobody ran the remittance. The filing calendar control above prevents it.\n2. **A sale with no tax where tax was due.** Check whether the tax service failed at checkout — the retry-without-tax fallback — or whether the jurisdiction configuration is missing. The journal faithfully records what the sale computed; a missing tax line means the computation produced zero, and the fix is in the configuration, not in the books.\n3. **Customer charged tax in a no-tax jurisdiction.** The automatic calculation used the wrong address — usually a billing address applied by default where the shipping address was not collected. Refund the tax portion under the Returns and Refunds Policy (§2.1.19) and correct the address capture; do not adjust it with a journal entry and call it done.\n4. **Rate change applied retroactively.** Someone edited the rate in place instead of dating the change, and every historical transaction now computes differently. Restore the old rate with its original dates, add the new rate with the change date, and re-examine any filings prepared in between.\n\n## Controls\n\n- Tax records — configuration, per-sale tax records, reconciliations, filed returns, and confirmations — are kept under the Marketplace Facilitator Tax Policy (§2.1.17), the tax documents in §2.9, and the Regulatory Record Retention Policy (§2.9.28).\n- No rate, threshold, or figure is taken from this standard or from memory; every figure comes from the configuration in force for the transaction's date or from the tax service's computation for that sale.\n- Separation of duties: the person who prepares a return does not approve its filing, and the person who records a tax payment does not reconcile the tax-payable account.\n- A closed period's tax figures are never altered. A correction is made by an amended filing where the jurisdiction requires one and by a correcting entry in the open period, under Books (§4.3.2).\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nTax codes, jurisdictions, and calculation rules; per-sale tax records; reconciliations; filed returns and their confirmations; and remittance records are kept in the accounting system and retained under the Regulatory Record Retention Policy (§2.9.28).\n\n## Escalation\n\nA nexus question, a notice from a tax authority, an examination, or any legal process touching tax goes to legal@unsaidmemories.com on the day it is received. Configuration and computation failures go to the Accounting Manager and, where the cause is a system failure, to support@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Accounting Manager reviews the tax configuration quarterly, reconciles before every filing, and reviews the filing calendar monthly. This standard is reviewed whenever the Marketplace Facilitator Tax Policy (§2.1.17) or a §2.9 tax document changes.\n",
  "reports": "\n# Reports\n\nThis standard governs the financial reports of UnSaid Memories, LLC — the Profit & Loss, the Balance Sheet, the Trial Balance, and the General Ledger — how they are run and, more importantly, how they are read. The reports are the authoritative financial statements: they read the books. The Accounting Dashboard (§4.3.1) is a summary built from operational records; where the dashboard and a report disagree, the report governs, subject to the exclusion stated below. The Accounting Operator runs the reports; the Accounting Manager reads them first, owns every explanation attached to them, and approves their release outside the accounting function.\n\n## The four reports\n\n1. **Profit & Loss.** Takes a tenant and a date range. Returns one row per revenue and expense account with its debit total, credit total, and net — revenue netted as credits minus debits, expenses as debits minus credits — plus a final total row carrying rolled-up revenue, expense, and net income. This is the \"did the Company make money\" report. It is run monthly, and always for a closed period; a Profit & Loss run mid-month is a progress report, not a result.\n2. **Balance Sheet.** Takes a tenant and an as-of date. Returns assets, liabilities, and equity with section totals, treating contra accounts correctly — a contra asset reduces its section rather than appearing as a negative asset. This is the \"what the Company owns and owes\" report. The as-of date matters: a balance sheet is a snapshot, and running it for yesterday rather than today can differ by every transaction posted in between.\n3. **Trial Balance.** Takes a tenant and an as-of date. Returns every account with its total debit and credit activity through that date, plus a totals row in which debits shall equal credits. This is the integrity report: its first job is not to inform but to prove the books are internally consistent. A Trial Balance whose totals do not agree means a posting broke double-entry somewhere, and nothing else that day matters more than finding it.\n4. **General Ledger.** Takes a tenant and a date range, defaulting to year-to-date where no range is given. Returns line detail — every posted debit and credit — with a running balance per account, ordered by account, then date, then entry. This is the \"show everything\" report and the starting point of every investigation. Where a Profit & Loss figure looks wrong, the operator does not argue with it; the operator runs the ledger for the account and reads the lines.\n\nAll four are produced through the finance actions, which pass the operator's tenant and dates through unchanged.\n\n## The exclusion that matters\n\nEvery report includes **posted entries only**. Unposted entries, unposted invoices, pending payments, and failed postings are invisible to all four reports. That is correct accounting — what has not posted is not yet in the books — but it creates the Platform's most common confusion: the dashboard shows the sale and the Profit & Loss does not. The sale's bookkeeping entry either failed without reaching the operator, never existed — subscription renewals, processor-console refunds, and manual payments, under Sales (§4.3.3) and Payments (§4.3.4) — or remains unposted. The reports are not wrong; they state exactly which events have not reached the books. The gap list in Troubleshooting (§4.3.15) exists for this.\n\n## Date discipline\n\n1. The reports select on the **entry date**, not the date the entry was keyed. A March sale whose bookkeeping entry was posted in April still lands in March's Profit & Loss. That is correct — the economics happened in March — but it means late postings change closed results. The discipline is: post promptly, and where a posting must be late, note which period the entry actually belongs to.\n2. A Profit & Loss that changes after it has been reported requires an explanation attached to the revised report, under the failure modes below.\n3. For the Balance Sheet and Trial Balance, the as-of date is inclusive: \"as of March 31\" includes March 31. Off-by-one date errors are the most common reason two people's balance sheets disagree.\n\n## Reading the reports\n\n**Profit & Loss, first pass:**\n\n1. Revenue by account — does the mix look right? A month in which revenue is sound but the wrong accounts carry it indicates line-level coding errors: invoice lines posted to the wrong revenue account, under Invoices (§4.3.5).\n2. Expenses — any account at twice its normal rate has its ledger pulled and read.\n3. The total row: net income, compared with the plan and with the same month of the prior year before anyone draws a conclusion.\n\n**Balance Sheet, first pass:**\n\n1. Cash and checking — do they agree with the bank reconciliation under Banking (§4.3.10)?\n2. Receivables — does the total agree with the receivables aging under Invoices (§4.3.5)? Where the control accounts do not agree with their detail, stop: statements built on top are unreliable until that is resolved.\n3. Liabilities: tax payable, deposits, and gift cards — all three should move in a sensible relation to sales. A deposits liability growing while appointments are level means deposits are not being applied, under Deposits (§4.3.6).\n\n**Trial Balance, first pass:**\n\n1. The totals row: debits equal credits — always, without exception.\n2. Then scan for accounts with unexpected balances: an asset with a credit balance, a revenue account with a debit balance. Some are legitimate — a refund-heavy day can net revenue negative — and most are coding errors.\n\n**General Ledger, first pass:**\n\n1. Filter to the account in question and read in date order. Every line's memo and source reference shall explain itself.\n2. Lines with empty memos and no source reference are defects to correct at the source record, under Books (§4.3.2).\n\n## Failure modes\n\n1. **Reports disagree with the dashboard.** Expected: the sources differ — posted books against operational records. Reconcile by listing the unposted and never-posted events from the gap list in Troubleshooting (§4.3.15), not by adjusting either side.\n2. **Profit & Loss changed after month-end.** A late posting landed in the closed period. Identify the entry by its source reference, confirm it belongs in that period, and attach the explanation to the revised report. Where late postings are routine, the defect is in the posting process, not the report.\n3. **Trial Balance out of balance.** The posting function rejects unbalanced entries, so a persistent imbalance means either a manual entry bypassed the safeguards or an earlier correction edited posted lines directly. Find the entry in which debits do not equal credits — the ledger ordered by entry shows it — and correct it with a balancing entry, never an edit.\n4. **Balance Sheet does not balance.** Assets not equal to liabilities plus equity means the underlying Trial Balance is off, or a report ran while a posting was half-completed. Re-run the Trial Balance first; where it agrees, re-run the Balance Sheet. The Balance Sheet is never adjusted to force it to balance.\n\n## Controls\n\n- Reports are produced from posted entries only and are never edited, restated by hand, or adjusted to agree with the dashboard, a bank statement, or an expectation. Every correction is an entry in the books under Books (§4.3.2); the report is then re-run.\n- A closed period's reports are the record of that period. Where a late entry changes them, the revised report carries the explanation; the earlier report is retained, not replaced silently.\n- Separation of duties: the person who posts the entries a report is drawn from does not approve the report's release outside the accounting function.\n- Tax figures in any report released for filing are drawn under Taxes (§4.3.13) and reconciled before filing.\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nEach run of the four reports for a closed period, with any explanation attached, is kept with the period's close records in the accounting system and retained under the Regulatory Record Retention Policy (§2.9.28).\n\n## Escalation\n\nA Trial Balance that will not agree, a control account that will not agree with its detail, or a report required for legal process goes to the Accounting Manager the same day; legal process goes to legal@unsaidmemories.com. Report or posting system failures go to support@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe four reports are run at every month-end close; the Trial Balance is also run weekly as an integrity check. The Accounting Manager reviews this standard annually and whenever a report's basis changes.\n",
  "accounting-troubleshooting": "\n# Accounting Troubleshooting\n\nThis standard is the accounting runbook for UnSaid Memories, LLC: symptom, where to look, and recovery, for the money problems that recur on the Platform. The Accounting Operator works the steps in order — each step eliminates a class of cause before the next is considered — and the Accounting Manager owns any problem that survives the runbook. One rule governs all of it: **the books are never corrected by editing a posted entry.** Corrections are reversing entries linked to the original, under Books (§4.3.2). A troubleshooting session that ends with an edited posted entry has created a second problem.\n\n## \"The money arrived but the books do not show it\"\n\nThe most common money problem on the Platform, with four distinct causes. Eliminate them in order:\n\n1. **The event never posts.** Subscription renewals write the payment records and mirror the register — no books entry, by the present arrangement, under Sales (§4.3.3). Manual payments recorded through the finance action create the register record — no books entry, under Payments (§4.3.4). Refunds issued from the processor's own console write a negative payment record — no books entry and no refund record, under Refunds and Disputes (§4.3.7). Where the missing money is one of these three, the recovery is the same: create the missing record and post the journal entry by hand. The month-end close includes standing steps for all three until the postings are connected.\n2. **The posting failed without stopping the event.** The payment notice's books posting and every finance action's posting are best-effort: an error is recorded and the operational event completes anyway. Search the journal by the event's source reference — payment identifier, order identifier, refund number. Where the entry is absent, the posting failed: post it manually with the same lines the automated posting would have used, as documented in Sales (§4.3.3) through Register (§4.3.9). Then check the error record for why it failed — an unknown account code from a deactivated account, a missing accounting entity for a new tenant, or an unbalanced entry from a discount line added to one side are the usual causes, and the cause determines whether other postings are failing too.\n3. **The entry is unposted.** Automated postings go directly to posted; manual entries begin unposted, and an unposted entry is invisible to every report under Reports (§4.3.14). Where the journal holds the entry and the Profit & Loss does not, check its state and post it.\n4. **Wrong period.** Reports select on entry date. A sale dated in March but posted in April is in March's Profit & Loss and absent from April's. Confirm the entry date before concluding the entry is missing.\n\n## \"The books and the operational records disagree\"\n\n1. Pick a direction and quantify it. Where the payment register shows more revenue than the books: list the register's succeeded payments for the period, list the books' revenue postings, and match by source reference. The unmatched items are the never-posted and failed-posting events from the gap list above.\n2. Where the books show more than the register: look for duplicate journal entries. The books posting carries no duplicate check of its own, so a repeated payment notice that re-ran the sale handling posts twice. Search the journal by the payment's source identifier and reverse the extra entry.\n3. For receivables: the invoices' outstanding total shall agree with the receivables control account. Where it does not, the usual causes are a mark-paid whose books posting failed — the invoice reads paid and the books never relieved the receivable — or a voided paid invoice whose reversal entry failed. Both leave the detail and the books differing by exactly the invoice's amount; match invoice by invoice, under Invoices (§4.3.5).\n\n## \"The Trial Balance does not agree\"\n\nThe posting function rejects unbalanced entries, so a persistent imbalance is serious.\n\n1. Re-run the report first: a report executed while a posting was half-completed can catch a partly written set of entries.\n2. Where the imbalance persists, the General Ledger ordered by entry shows the offending entry — the one whose debits do not equal its credits. The causes are a manual entry that bypassed the posting function, or an earlier correction that edited posted lines directly.\n3. Recovery is a balancing entry that returns the books to balance, with a memo explaining the correction and referencing the offending entry. The lines are never edited to repair it; an edit is what caused it.\n\n## \"The bank reconciliation will not clear\"\n\nWork the difference in the order given in Banking (§4.3.10): outstanding items (timing, legitimate), bank-only items (fees and interest — post them), book-only items (entries with no real-world counterpart — reverse them), and wrong-account postings. The known wrong-account hazard: the shop sale posts to **checking**, but the refund action credits **cash**. A card sale refunded through the administrative action leaves the books' checking balance wrong by the refund amount while the bank is right; move the refund entry to checking by correcting entry.\n\nRound-number differences are whole missing transactions: search for an unposted refund or fee before hunting for keying errors. Small differences are usually bank fees the books missed. A one-cent difference is still unreconciled — the reconciliation is recorded complete only at exactly zero, and that is the standard.\n\n## \"A payment is stuck in pending\"\n\nOnly succeeded means money moved.\n\n1. Check the processor first. Where the processor shows the payment captured, the Platform's record-keeping missed it: record it manually and post the books entry by hand, noting that manual records post no journal entry.\n2. Where the processor shows it uncaptured, it was never money: follow up with the customer or let the authorization expire.\n3. Pending is not a state to monitor; it is a state to resolve. See Payments (§4.3.4).\n\n## \"The deposits liability keeps growing\"\n\nDeposits are being collected but never applied, released, or forfeited, under Deposits (§4.3.6).\n\n1. Run the stale-deposit check: every held deposit past its expected service date is either work performed but never invoiced — invoice it and apply the deposit — or work never performed — release or refund it.\n2. Assign the queue to a named operator. A liability that only grows is a queue nobody is working.\n\n## \"A Seller's payout never arrived\"\n\n1. \"Paid\" means the processor accepted the instruction; the money then travels on the processor's timeline. Check the payout's processor identifier in the processor's console before treating the Platform as having failed.\n2. Where the payout failed, the destination and failure reason are on the record — wrong bank details and closed accounts are the usual causes. Correct the cause and re-issue; never edit the failed record.\n3. Where the Seller's balance grows with no payouts at all, that is a capability freeze during a dispute investigation — an enforcement hold, released only by the trust and safety decision, not an operator action. See Payments (§4.3.4) and Refunds and Disputes (§4.3.7).\n\n## \"The payment notice is failing\"\n\nNotice signature failures mean the processor's events never reach the Platform: no operational updates, no books postings, nothing. The symptom in the accounting records is silence — payments the processor shows as captured with no Platform record at all.\n\n1. Check the notice endpoint's recent deliveries in the processor's console. Failed signatures point to a signing-secret mismatch — the secret was rotated without updating the Platform's configuration. Repeated timeouts point to the handler taking too long.\n2. Until delivery is restored, every event in the gap is reconciled by hand: take the processor's event list for the outage window and match each event against the Platform's records.\n\n## \"Tax payable does not match what was collected\"\n\nThe reconciliation has three parts: the tax service's computed totals, the Platform's sales-tax-payable balance, and the filed return, under Taxes (§4.3.13).\n\n1. Where the service computed tax but the payable is short: the tax line posts only where tax was collected, and a sale whose tax computation failed at checkout — the retry-without-tax fallback — carries no tax. Those sales are the gap.\n2. Where the payable exceeds the service's totals: a manual entry or a register sale posted tax the automatic calculation did not. Verify the manual entries before assuming the automatic side is wrong.\n\n## The recovery order\n\nWhere the cause is found, correct it at the right level, in this order:\n\n1. **The source** — a deactivated account, a missing configuration, a failing notice delivery. Correct the source, or every future event fails the same way.\n2. **The missing record** — create the refund record, the payment record, or the books entry that should exist, posting with the same lines the automated posting would have used.\n3. **The correction** — a reversing entry linked to the original for anything posted wrong. Never an edit.\n\nAfter every incident, the operator records what would have caught it earlier — a weekly processor-refund-to-books match, a stale-deposit review, a weekly Trial Balance check — and the Accounting Manager adds that check to the standing routine where it belongs. The runbook grows with every failure it survives.\n\n## Controls\n\n- No posted entry is edited, no period is reopened, and no reconciliation is forced under any circumstance in this runbook; corrections follow Books (§4.3.2) and Banking (§4.3.10).\n- Separation of duties holds during recovery: the person who posts a recovery entry does not reconcile the account it lands in.\n- Refund recoveries are made under the Returns and Refunds Policy (§2.1.19) and disputes under the Chargeback Policy (§2.1.35); a recovery never creates a second refund to repair a missing record.\n- Banking and processor credentials encountered during diagnosis are never recorded in an incident note, a memo, or any other document.\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nRecovery entries with their explanatory memos, incident notes, the processor-match lists, and the updated close checklist are kept with the accounting records for the period concerned and retained under the Regulatory Record Retention Policy (§2.9.28).\n\n## Escalation\n\nA problem that survives this runbook, a Trial Balance that will not agree, or any indication that funds are missing goes to the Accounting Manager the same day. System causes go to support@unsaidmemories.com with the source references and error records attached. Suspected fraud and legal process go to legal@unsaidmemories.com on the day they arise.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Accounting Manager reviews this runbook after every incident it is used for, adds the new check the incident produced, and reviews the whole standard quarterly.\n",
  "books": "\n# Books\n\nThis standard governs the books of UnSaid Memories, LLC — the chart of accounts, the journal, and the general ledger — the formal accounting record into which every operational event in this section must eventually be posted. Invoices, payments, refunds, and payroll are operational events; the books are where those events become accounting. The Accounting Operator posts and investigates entries; the Accounting Manager owns the chart of accounts, period closing, and every correction to a posted entry. If an event is not in the journal, it has not happened for the purposes of the financial statements.\n\n## How money reaches the books\n\nEvery automated posting passes through one journal-posting function, called by the payment webhook for shop sales and booking deposits, by the finance actions for refunds, deposits, and gift cards, and by the same pattern at the register. The function enforces the following before anything is recorded, and each rule is a hard stop, not a warning:\n\n1. Total debits shall equal total credits to the cent, or the posting is rejected.\n2. An empty posting is rejected.\n3. Every account code shall resolve to an active account in the tenant's chart of accounts.\n4. An accounting entity shall exist for the tenant, or nothing posts for that tenant at all.\n\nThe function then assembles the context itself: the tenant's accounting entity, the main book (accrual basis, United States dollars), the fiscal year and the current month's period (left open), and a batch for the posting run. The entry number is assigned by the system and never by a caller; it is the permanent identifier cited in every later inquiry about the entry. Automated postings go directly to posted state.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> When a posting fails, the operational event still happened: the processor holds the money, the order is paid, the operational records exist. What is missing is the journal entry, and nothing retries the posting on its own. The books are behind reality until an operator recovers the entry by hand under Troubleshooting (§4.3.15).\n\n## Chart of accounts\n\nThe chart of accounts is the vocabulary of the books. Each account carries a code, a name, a type, and a normal balance. The accounts used most in daily work are:\n\n| Code | Account | Type | What posts here |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1000 | Cash | Asset | Cash drawer receipts, manual cash payments, deposit collections, gift card issuance |\n| 1010 | Checking | Asset | Card payments — processor settlements land here, not in cash |\n| 1200 | Accounts Receivable | Asset | Invoice balances owed by customers |\n| 1400 | Inventory | Asset | Relieved when cost of goods sold is recognized |\n| 2200 | Sales Tax Payable | Liability | Tax collected at sale, owed to the jurisdiction |\n| 2300 | Customer Deposits | Liability | Booking deposits held — not revenue until earned |\n| 2310 | Gift Card Liability | Liability | Unredeemed gift card balances |\n| 4000 | Revenue | Revenue | Sales, forfeited deposits, gift card redemptions |\n| 4900 | Sales Discounts | Revenue (contra) | Discounts, kept separate so gross revenue stays visible |\n| 5000 | Cost of Goods Sold | Expense | Matched against inventory relief on each sale |\n\nThree flags on each account control behavior:\n\n1. **Posting allowed.** Control accounts accept postings only from their own detailed records, never manually; that restriction is what keeps the detail and the books in agreement.\n2. **Reconcilable.** The account shall be reconciled to an external statement, as bank and card-clearing accounts are, under Banking (§4.3.10).\n3. **System.** The Platform created the account and automated processes depend on it. A system account shall not be renamed or deactivated.\n\nAdding an account is deliberate. The Accounting Manager codes it inside the existing numbering scheme, sets the type and normal balance, and writes a description that distinguishes it from its neighbours. Before deactivating any account, the Accounting Manager confirms what posts to it; deactivating an account that automated postings use breaks those postings.\n\n## Journal entries, batches, and periods\n\nA journal entry is a balanced set of debit and credit lines under a header carrying the entry number, the entry date and posting date, the source and source identifier tracing the entry back to the originating document, a reference, a memo, and the debit and credit totals. When something looks wrong in the books, the operator follows the source reference; the operator does not edit the books.\n\n1. New manual entries begin unposted; automated postings post directly.\n2. A posted entry records who posted it and when.\n3. Batches group the entries from one source — a payroll run, a bank import, a night's processor postings.\n4. Periods are monthly. The posting function opens the current month's period where none exists. The Accounting Manager closes a period when the month's books are final.\n5. For foreign-currency activity, entries carry the currency and exchange rate, and lines carry the transaction-currency amounts alongside the converted amounts. Where a foreign-currency invoice looks wrong, check the rate on the line before questioning the total.\n\n## The general ledger\n\nThe ledger is every posted debit and credit organized by account, and it is the source from which every financial report in Reports (§4.3.14) is drawn. The ledger report returns line detail with a running balance per account, ordered by account, then date, then entry.\n\n1. To answer what happened in an account in a period, filter by account and date range.\n2. To answer why a balance is what it is, read the lines in date order; each line's memo and source reference shall explain itself.\n3. A line with an empty memo and no source reference is a defect to correct at the source record, not in the ledger.\n\nThe ledger contains posted entries only. Unposted invoices and pending payments remain in their operational records until they post. Where the dashboard and the ledger disagree, the question is always which operational events have not posted yet — not whether the ledger is wrong. See Accounting Dashboard (§4.3.1).\n\n## Controls\n\n- A posted entry shall never be edited. A correction is made by a reversing entry linked to the original, followed where needed by the corrected entry. The entries together are the record of what was believed true at each point, mistakes included.\n- No entry shall be posted into a closed period. An event belonging to a closed period is corrected by a reversing or correcting entry in the open period that follows it, with a memo naming the period it belongs to.\n- Separation of duties: the person who records a payment does not reconcile it, and the person who posts a manual entry does not approve the close of the period it lands in.\n- Banking credentials are never recorded in a journal memo, a batch name, or any other field of the books, and are never shared between operators. Access to banking is governed by Banking (§4.3.10).\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nThis standard creates journal entries, batches, periods, and ledger history, all kept in the accounting system as the Company's formal books. They are retained under the Regulatory Record Retention Policy (§2.9.28) and are the records produced for any audit, tax examination, or legal process.\n\n## Escalation\n\nA posting rejected for a missing accounting entity, an imbalance that cannot be traced to its source record, or any request to alter a posted entry goes to the Accounting Manager the same day. Operational failures that prevent posting go to support@unsaidmemories.com. Legal process touching the books goes to legal@unsaidmemories.com on the day it is received.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Accounting Manager reviews the chart of accounts quarterly, closes each monthly period after the close checklist in Troubleshooting (§4.3.15) is complete, and reviews this standard annually.\n",
  "sales": "\n# Sales\n\nThis standard governs how sales reach the books of UnSaid Memories, LLC. A sale is not one thing on the Platform: it travels one of four paths — online checkout, counter sale, subscription renewal, or invoice — each with its own operational records and its own bookkeeping, and two of the four do not reach the books automatically. The Accounting Operator works the daily sale records and the month-end entries this standard requires; the Accounting Manager owns the close. An operator who closes the month from the books alone, without the manual entries below, understates revenue.\n\n## Path 1 — Online checkout\n\nWhen a customer checks out online and the processor confirms payment, the Platform marks the order paid, reduces inventory, creates the supplier orders, posts loyalty points, records the customer, sends the receipt, and then posts the bookkeeping entry:\n\n1. **Checking debited** for the full amount. Card money lands in checking, not cash.\n2. **Revenue credited** for the amount net of tax.\n3. **Sales tax payable credited** for the tax portion, where tax was collected, under Taxes (§4.3.13).\n\nThe entry carries the processor payment as its source and the payment identifier and amounts in its memo. The posting is attempted once; if it fails, the error is recorded and the sale completes without it. Recovery is manual, under Troubleshooting (§4.3.15). This posting recognizes revenue and tax only: it includes no cost of goods sold and no inventory relief. Where product costs are matched against online revenue, that matching is a separate entry and shall not be assumed to happen through the register's cost treatment.\n\n## Path 2 — Counter sale\n\nThe register builds the fullest bookkeeping entry of any sale path and refuses to complete the sale unless the entry balances; see Register (§4.3.9). Its lines are:\n\n1. The payment-method accounts debited — cash and check to the cash drawer, card to checking, and a gift-card tender relieves the gift card liability instead of touching cash. The debit is the **sale total, not the tendered amount**; change due is a separate cash-out, not part of the revenue entry.\n2. Customer deposits debited for any booking deposit applied, relieving the deposit liability against the revenue below; without this line the entry is short by exactly the deposit amount.\n3. Cost of goods sold debited and inventory credited — the only sale path that recognizes cost at the moment of sale.\n4. Sales discounts debited, kept separate so gross revenue stays visible.\n5. Revenue credited at the gross subtotal, and sales tax payable credited for the tax.\n\nA tip folded into the tendered amount is limited so it cannot unbalance the entry.\n\n## Path 3 — Subscription renewals\n\nClub membership renewals — the grooming club and the shipping club — are handled when the subscription invoice is paid. The Platform writes the payment record, mirrors the payment into the business payment register so the administrative views show the revenue, updates the customer record, and posts loyalty points for grooming-club renewals.\n\nRenewals do **not** post to the books. No journal entry is created for renewal revenue. Until that connection exists, the month-end close shall include a manual or batched entry for subscription renewals: the Accounting Operator takes the totals from the subscription payments in the payment records and posts checking debited, revenue credited, before the period closes.\n\n## Path 4 — Invoices\n\nInvoices are the formal claim-for-payment path, governed by Invoices (§4.3.5). Each invoice line carries its own tax code, discount, and revenue account; the line-level revenue account is what makes the Profit & Loss correct. Where invoice totals are right but revenue reporting is wrong, the lines are the first place to look. A deposit-paid amount on the invoice tracks deposits already collected so that marking the invoice paid does not collect them twice.\n\n## The pricing rule and the books\n\nThe displayed price is computed once, when the product is written, as supplier cost multiplied by 1.55 plus shipping, and is then stored. Revenue per unit is therefore fixed at sale time, and no later recalculation can make the journal disagree with the receipt. Manually overridden prices are meant to be protected from automated repricing. The Accounting Operator shall spot-check overridden prices against posted revenue periodically; if an automated job overwrites a manual price, revenue posts at the wrong price with no marker on the record.\n\n## What does not post — the close list\n\nThe month-end close shall include manual entries or accruals for each of the following, or the books understate revenue and expense:\n\n1. **Subscription renewals** — payment records only, no books entry (Path 3 above).\n2. **Refunds issued directly in the processor's own console** — a negative payment record, no journal entry; see Refunds and Disputes (§4.3.7).\n3. **Manual payments recorded through the finance actions** — the payment register record is created, no journal entry; see Payments (§4.3.4).\n4. **Online checkout cost of goods** — revenue posts, cost does not; a cost accrual is required where costs are matched.\n\n## Controls\n\n- A sale shall never be recorded twice. Where revenue appears doubled for one payment, the operator searches the journal by the payment's source identifier and reverses the extra entry; posted entries are never edited, under Books (§4.3.2).\n- Tax is credited to sales tax payable only where tax was actually collected. A sale on which tax should have been collected and was not is a checkout configuration problem, not a posting problem; the operator refers it under Taxes (§4.3.13) rather than adjusting the journal to conceal it.\n- Refunds of sales are handled only under the Returns and Refunds Policy (§2.1.19) and Refunds and Disputes (§4.3.7); a sale is never quietly reversed at the register or in the books.\n- Separation of duties: the person who records a sale or a manual sale entry does not reconcile the account it posts to.\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nSale records, payment records, invoice records, and the journal entries described here are kept in the accounting and commerce records of the Platform and retained under the Regulatory Record Retention Policy (§2.9.28).\n\n## Escalation\n\nA sale whose bookkeeping entry has failed, a suspected duplicate posting, or a pricing overwrite goes to the Accounting Manager the same day and, where the cause is a system failure, to support@unsaidmemories.com. Disputes and legal process touching a sale go to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Accounting Manager reviews the close list monthly as part of the close, spot-checks overridden prices quarterly, and reviews this standard whenever a sale path is added or its posting changes.\n",
  "accounting-payments": "\n# Accounting Payments\n\nThis standard governs the payment records of UnSaid Memories, LLC: how money moves for first-party sales today, how it is designed to move for marketplace Seller sales, and how the Accounting Operator works the records day to day. Where funds sit at each stage determines whether a payment problem belongs to the Company, to a Seller, or to the payment processor, so every operator who touches payments shall understand both tracks below. Payment collection for Sellers is governed by the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1); this standard states the accounting practice under it and does not restate it.\n\n## The two tracks\n\n**Track 1 — the Company is the merchant (first-party sales).** Shop checkout creates a payment session in the Company's own processor account. The buyer pays the Company. There is no platform fee taken from the payment and no transfer to a Seller in this path: the books entry debits checking for the full amount and credits revenue net of tax. This is correct while the only seller is the Company itself — products sold by the Company are Sold by Portal Shop.\n\n**Track 2 — destination charges (marketplace Seller sales).** Under the decided arrangement for third-party Sellers, the buyer's charge carries a platform fee taken at capture, and the remainder routes directly to the Seller's connected account. The payment records already carry the fields for the split — the Seller's account and the platform fee amount. Under the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1), the Company acts as the Seller's limited agent for collecting the buyer's payment: the buyer's obligation is satisfied when the Company receives the funds, and the Company holds the remedy rights stated there, including transfer reversals and payout freezes.\n\nA payment record with an empty Seller-account field is not broken; it is a first-party, merchant-model payment. When destination charges begin, that field becomes the most important field on the record, and the operator shall not confuse the two tracks when diagnosing a problem.\n\n## Connected account tracking\n\nFor each tenant, one record tracks its connected processor account: the account identifier, whether charging is enabled, whether payouts are enabled, and whether onboarding is complete. The main business and every franchise tenant link their own bank account through the processor's onboarding; bank details are collected and held by the processor and are never typed into or stored in the Platform.\n\n1. Where a tenant cannot receive payouts, the operator checks these flags first; incomplete onboarding is the usual cause and is visible on the record.\n2. The flags are a copy of the processor's position, kept current by the processor's account-update notices. Where the flags and the processor's own console disagree, the processor's console governs, and the operator records the disagreement rather than editing the flags.\n\nSeller identity verification and tax forms at connected-account onboarding are the processor's responsibility, not the accounting team's. The accounting team's responsibility is the onboarding flags and the two halves of every split payment.\n\n## The payment records\n\nTwo records do two jobs:\n\n1. **The processor-facing record** logs every processor event — charges, refunds (as negative amounts), and subscription payments — recorded once per processor transaction, so a repeated notice never double-counts.\n2. **The business-facing register** is the payment list staff work from: payment number, customer, method, amount, state, and the sale or invoice it belongs to. Subscription payments are mirrored into the register so the administrative views show the revenue; see Sales (§4.3.3).\n\nRegister records are never deleted. A payment is voided or refunded only with a linked record stating the reason, under Refunds and Disputes (§4.3.7). The states in use are pending, succeeded, refunded, and cancelled; only succeeded means money moved.\n\n## Working payments — the daily procedure\n\n1. Review the two working filters: payments in pending older than one business day (captures that never completed), and failed payments in the last 24 hours (customers to contact about retrying). Check failure codes before contacting anyone; a sudden fall in the success rate is almost always a processor or connection problem, not a customer problem.\n2. Resolve every stale pending payment: it is either a capture that never completed or an authorization that will expire.\n3. **Recording a manual payment** — for phone orders, in-person catch-ups, and corrections — creates the register record as succeeded, using the tenant's manual cash method where none is supplied. A manual payment posts **no journal entry**; the register shows the money and the books do not. The operator flags every manual payment for the manual reconciliation in Troubleshooting (§4.3.15). Before recording anything by hand, match on the processor payment identifier: the processor-facing record cannot duplicate, but the register can, and a mirrored subscription payment plus a manual entry for the same charge is the classic double count.\n4. **Receipts** print from the payment record. Reprinting is always safe and changes nothing. A payment shall never be recreated to generate a receipt.\n5. **Batch export** for the bank or the tax preparer is run only after the period's payments are final and reconciled. Exporting mid-day and again the next morning double-counts everything that arrived in between.\n\n## Money movement for marketplace Sellers\n\nWhen destination charges begin, the sequence the records are shaped for is:\n\n1. At checkout, one charge for the full order amount.\n2. At capture, the processor splits it: the platform fee is taken first, and the remainder lands directly in the Seller's connected account balance.\n3. The payment record preserves both halves — the Seller's account and the platform fee. The Company's books show fee revenue; the Seller's side shows sale proceeds. Neither side shows the other's money as its own.\n4. Between checkout and the Seller's payout date, funds sit in the Seller's connected account balance — not in the Company's bank account and not yet in the Seller's bank account. The Company never holds third-party funds in its own bank account: buyer to processor to Seller. The Company directs the flow; it does not hold the funds.\n5. Seller payouts settle on a 7–14 day rolling delay, keeping funds available while the dispute window is open. The Seller's balance therefore shows an available amount and a held amount; a Seller asking why a balance has not paid out is asking about this delay.\n\nA dispute decided against the Seller triggers a **transfer reversal**: the disputed amount is pulled back out of the Seller's balance and appears as a transaction against the Seller. Where the Company returns its own fee to make the buyer whole, that is a reduction of fee revenue, not an expense. Fraud or ignored disputes trigger capability gating: payouts freeze during investigation, which appears in the records as a growing balance with no payouts. That is an enforcement hold, not a defect, and it is released only by the trust and safety decision, under the Chargeback Policy (§2.1.35).\n\n## Controls\n\n- Separation of duties: the person who records a payment does not reconcile it. Reconciliation is governed by Banking (§4.3.10).\n- Banking credentials and processor credentials are never shared between operators and are never recorded in a payment record, a memo, or any other document.\n- A manual payment shall carry the method and a reference identifying where the money actually arrived. A manual record without a reference is an incomplete record.\n- Refunds are issued only under the Returns and Refunds Policy (§2.1.19) through Refunds and Disputes (§4.3.7), never by recreating or editing a payment.\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nPayment records, connected account records, receipts, and batch exports are kept in the Platform's commerce and accounting records and retained under the Regulatory Record Retention Policy (§2.9.28).\n\n## Escalation\n\nMoney in the processor with no Platform record, a payment carrying a Seller destination with no Seller account recorded, or any suspected duplicate goes to the Accounting Manager the same day and, where the cause is a system failure, to support@unsaidmemories.com. Chargebacks and legal process go to legal@unsaidmemories.com under the Chargeback Policy (§2.1.35).\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Accounting Operator works the payment filters daily. The Accounting Manager reviews manual payments and the pending and failed queues weekly, and reviews this standard when destination charges begin or the processor arrangement changes.\n",
  "invoices": "\n# Invoices\n\nThis standard governs invoices in the accounting system of UnSaid Memories, LLC — the formal claim for payment and the basis of receivables. The Accounting Operator creates, sends, reminds, marks paid, applies deposits to, and voids invoices; the Accounting Manager approves voids of paid invoices and owns collections decisions. The governing rule is simple: an invoice that has been sent to a customer is a promise the business made. It may be corrected, credited, or voided — it may not be treated as though it never existed.\n\n## The invoice record\n\nThe invoice carries the commercial facts — number, dates, lines, tax, discounts, total, amount paid, amount credited, and state — and the full communication history: when it was sent, how many times it was resent, the last reminder, and whether it was viewed.\n\n1. Where a customer says an invoice never arrived, the operator answers from these fields: when it was sent, how many times, and whether it was opened. An unviewed invoice after two sends indicates a wrong address or a mail filter, not a forgetful customer; the operator confirms the address before a third send.\n2. Each line carries a description, quantity, unit price, discount, tax code, tax amount, and the revenue account the line posts to. The line-level revenue account is what makes the Profit & Loss right; where totals are correct but revenue reporting is wrong, the lines are the first suspect.\n3. An invoice may link to a pet, appointment, or booking. Where an invoice's service details look wrong, the operator checks the linked appointment before editing the invoice.\n4. A deposit-paid amount on the invoice tracks deposits already collected, so the paid-marking procedure does not collect them twice. Deposits are governed by Deposits (§4.3.6).\n\n## The lifecycle\n\n**Create.**\n\n1. Build the header and the lines, pricing each line carefully; the invoice inherits whatever is entered, and each line's tax code and revenue account travel with it.\n2. Confirm the billing contact before the invoice is first sent.\n\n**Send and resend.**\n\n1. Send promptly — the day the work is done or the goods ship. Every day between the sale and the invoice is a day added to the collection cycle.\n2. Each send records the time and increases the resend count on the invoice record.\n\n**Mark paid** — for money that arrived outside the normal payment flow: a check in the mail, cash at the register, a bank transfer.\n\n1. Confirm the money actually arrived, the amount, and the method.\n2. Mark the invoice paid, recording the payment method and reference with the mark. \"Paid\" without a method is an incomplete record and confuses the bank reconciliation under Banking (§4.3.10).\n3. The action refuses an already-paid invoice — no second books entry is posted — and refuses a void invoice. On success it records the paid state and time, computes the paid amount as the total minus credits minus deposits already paid, and posts the books entry: **cash debited, accounts receivable credited**.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> An invoice shall never be marked paid to make the aging look better. The paid mark is a statement of fact, and the payment record behind it is the proof.\n\n**Remind.**\n\n1. Send reminders from the invoice record; each reminder is recorded with its count.\n2. Reminders increase in firmness by count: the first is a prompt, the second is firm, and the third contact is a telephone call.\n3. A customer reminded four times with no payment is not a reminder problem; the operator refers the invoice to the Accounting Manager as a collections decision. Before escalating, the operator verifies that the billing contact is the right person — invoices and reminders sent to the same unreceiving address fail the same way.\n\n**Void.**\n\n1. Void cancels the invoice: the balance goes to zero, the history is preserved, and the void is recorded with who acted, when, and why. The reason shall be complete — for example, \"Voided in error, reissued as\" followed by the new invoice number.\n2. Void is only for an invoice that should never have existed: wrong customer, duplicate, or a sale that fell through before delivery.\n3. Void is not a substitute for a credit memo. Where goods or services were delivered and the amount is being adjusted, the operator issues a credit memo so the revenue and the adjustment both remain visible. Void says \"this never happened\"; a credit memo says \"this happened and the amount is adjusted.\"\n4. Voiding a **paid** invoice posts a reversal books entry — the cash and the receivable unwind. A voided invoice can never be restored; the wrongly voided invoice is reissued under a new number, with the void referenced in the memo.\n\n**Apply deposit.**\n\n1. Check the deposit-paid amount first; applying a deposit that has already been applied double-counts it.\n2. Applying the deposit relieves the customer-deposits liability against the receivable, under Deposits (§4.3.6).\n\n## Editing discipline\n\n1. An unsent invoice may be edited freely.\n2. A sent invoice: descriptions may be corrected, but a price the customer has already received shall never be changed silently. The operator issues a credit memo for the difference, or voids and reissues.\n3. Line edits carry weight beyond the words: changing a tax code changes the tax liability, and changing a revenue account moves the sale on the Profit & Loss. Every edit to a sent invoice carries a note stating why; the next reader is the operator in three months or the accountant at year-end.\n\n## Controls\n\n- Separation of duties: the person who marks an invoice paid does not reconcile the bank account the money arrived in.\n- The mark-paid books posting is attempted automatically and can fail without changing the invoice's paid state. Where an invoice reads paid and the books show no cash, the operator searches the journal by the invoice's source identifier and, if the entry is missing, posts it manually under Troubleshooting (§4.3.15).\n- The aging total can conceal its own problem: a level total with a growing oldest bucket means new sales are masking uncollected old invoices. The operator reads the buckets, not only the total, and checks the viewed markers — unviewed invoices are not being received.\n- A customer shall not be collected twice for one invoice. A processor payment and a manual mark-paid for the same invoice produce a paid amount exceeding the total — an overpayment to refund under the Returns and Refunds Policy (§2.1.19) or to credit, identified by matching payments to invoices at the time of payment.\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nInvoice records, line records, reminder and viewing history, void records with their reasons, and the related journal entries are kept in the accounting system and retained under the Regulatory Record Retention Policy (§2.9.28).\n\n## Escalation\n\nA collections decision, a disputed invoice, or a void of a paid invoice goes to the Accounting Manager. Legal process touching an invoice — a demand, a bankruptcy notice, a subpoena — goes to legal@unsaidmemories.com on the day it is received. System failures in sending or posting go to support@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Accounting Operator reviews the receivables aging daily through the Accounting Dashboard (§4.3.1). The Accounting Manager reviews the oldest aging bucket and all voids weekly, and reviews this standard annually.\n",
  "deposits": "\n# Deposits\n\nThis standard governs customer deposits in the accounting system of UnSaid Memories, LLC — money collected before the work is done, such as a grooming appointment hold or a down payment on a custom order. A deposit is a liability, not revenue: the Company holds the customer's money and owes the customer the service. A deposit becomes revenue only when it is applied to an invoice for completed work or forfeited under the terms stated to the customer at collection. The Accounting Operator collects, applies, releases, and refunds deposits; forfeiture requires the Accounting Manager's approval recorded on the deposit. Everything in this standard protects the distinction between money held and money earned, because recognizing deposit money as revenue early misstates the books.\n\n## The lifecycle\n\nEvery deposit moves through a fixed sequence of states — held, released, refunded, forfeited — and the actions refuse a move the state does not allow, with an error, rather than completing silently.\n\n**Collect.**\n\n1. Create the deposit record in held state, recording the collection time and the deposit number.\n2. State the deposit terms at collection — the refund terms and the forfeiture terms. That conversation happens once, at collection, not when the customer cancels.\n3. Post the books entry: **cash debited, customer-deposits liability credited**.\n4. Mind the units: the entry screen sends cents and the record stores dollars. A cents-and-dollars mix-up is a hundredfold error, and the operator verifies the recorded amount against the amount collected before leaving the record.\n\n**Apply.**\n\n1. Apply the deposit against the invoice balance when the work is complete and the invoice is issued — not before. See Invoices (§4.3.5).\n2. In the register sale's books entry, an applied deposit appears as a debit relieving the liability against the revenue credited on the sale; without that line the entry is short by exactly the deposit amount and the register's balance check stops the sale, under Register (§4.3.9).\n\n**Release.**\n\n1. Release returns the money without applying it, where the service was cancelled on refundable terms. Only a held deposit may be released; any other state is refused.\n2. Post the books entry: **liability debited, cash credited**.\n3. Record the release reason, distinguishing a refund under the stated terms from an exception made for the customer.\n\n**Forfeit.**\n\n1. Forfeiture applies where the customer cancelled outside the refund terms — the no-show, the late cancellation. Only a held deposit may be forfeited.\n2. Post the books entry: **customer-deposits liability debited, revenue credited**.\n3. Record, on the deposit, the terms the customer agreed to at collection, the cancellation facts, and the Accounting Manager's approval. Forfeiture is the one deposit movement that creates revenue without an invoice, so it carries the fullest record of any deposit action.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> A forfeited deposit without a recorded agreement to the terms is a dispute the Company is likely to lose: the processor sees a customer charged for nothing received. No operator forfeits a deposit whose collection-time terms are not on file. Disputes are handled under Refunds and Disputes (§4.3.7) and the Chargeback Policy (§2.1.35).\n\n**Refund.**\n\n1. A refund returns collected deposit money outside the standard release path, by the Accounting Manager's decision or under the deposit terms, with a reason recorded on the deposit.\n2. Deposit refunds are handled under the Returns and Refunds Policy (§2.1.19).\n\n**Transfer.**\n\n1. A transfer moves a deposit from one appointment or customer to another, recording the source, the destination, the time, and the reason, so the deposit's history remains one connected record.\n2. A transfer is not a release followed by a new collection. Two separate records invite the argument that the first deposit was never returned; one transfer record with both ends closes that argument.\n3. Before training staff on transfers, the Accounting Manager confirms the transfer action is present in the administrative build in use.\n\n## The online path: booking deposits\n\nOnline booking deposits enter the books through the payment webhook, which posts **checking debited, customer-deposits liability credited** — expressly not revenue. When the appointment completes and the sale posts through the register, the applied-deposit line relieves the liability. The full course of a booking deposit is therefore: liability created at payment, liability relieved and revenue recognized at completion. A deposits liability that grows without bound means the second half is not happening — deposits are being collected but never applied or released — and the queue procedure below applies.\n\n## Watching the queue\n\n1. Work from the collected-but-unapplied view. Every deposit in it is either future work to perform or money to return.\n2. Run the stale-deposit check at least monthly: every deposit held past its expected service date needs a disposition. A deposit for an appointment that has passed and was never applied means either the appointment happened and nobody invoiced it, or it never happened and the deposit is still owed back.\n3. Keep deposit types separate. An appointment hold and a custom-order deposit carry different terms when the customer cancels and are not interchangeable.\n4. Reconcile the deposits liability monthly: every held deposit maps to a future appointment or a pending decision. Anything else is stale.\n\nUnclaimed deposits that sit indefinitely become a stale liability and a customer-relations problem when the customer eventually asks, and may carry obligations under state unclaimed-property rules; the Accounting Manager refers any long-unclaimed deposit to legal@unsaidmemories.com before treating it as the Company's money.\n\n## Controls\n\n- Every deposit action posts its books entry automatically on a best-effort basis: a failed posting is recorded as an error and the deposit record still stands. The operator searches the journal by the deposit number for any action whose entry is in doubt and posts the missing entry manually under Troubleshooting (§4.3.15).\n- No operator works around a state refusal by editing a deposit's state directly. The refusal is part of the record. Where money must come back after a deposit has been applied, that is a refund against the invoice, not a deposit release.\n- Separation of duties: the person who collects a deposit does not approve its forfeiture, and the person who releases or refunds a deposit does not reconcile the account it posts to.\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nDeposit records, their state history, transfer records, terms acknowledgments, forfeiture approvals, and the related journal entries are kept in the accounting and commerce records and retained under the Regulatory Record Retention Policy (§2.9.28).\n\n## Escalation\n\nA forfeiture the customer disputes, a deposit with no terms on file, or a long-unclaimed deposit goes to the Accounting Manager; disputed forfeitures and legal process go to legal@unsaidmemories.com. Operational failures in the deposit actions go to support@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Accounting Operator works the unapplied queue weekly. The Accounting Manager reconciles the deposits liability monthly, reviews every forfeiture monthly, and reviews this standard annually.\n",
  "refunds-and-disputes": "\n# Refunds and Disputes\n\nThis standard governs refunds and payment disputes in the accounting system of UnSaid Memories, LLC: how a refund is issued, how the refund the Platform did not issue is caught, and how a dispute or chargeback is worked to its deadline. Money flowing backward needs more documentation than money flowing forward, because unwinding a sale touches the payment, the inventory, the revenue, the Seller's balance where a Seller is involved, and sometimes the processor — and every touch must be traceable. Whether a refund is due, and to whom, is governed by the Returns and Refunds Policy (§2.1.19); chargebacks are governed by the Chargeback Policy (§2.1.35). This standard states the accounting procedure under those policies and does not restate them. The Accounting Operator issues refunds and assembles dispute evidence; the Accounting Manager approves refunds above the operator's authority and owns the dispute calendar.\n\n## Issuing a refund\n\nThe issue-refund action protects against the most expensive refund mistake, the double refund: before doing anything, it checks the refund records for the order. A repeat request for the same order returns the existing refund instead of creating a second one. The operator shall nevertheless confirm no refund already exists for the order before issuing, including any refund made outside the Platform.\n\n1. Confirm the refund is due under the Returns and Refunds Policy (§2.1.19), and confirm the amount and the lines it covers.\n2. Issue the refund through the administrative action. The sequence is: the order moves to refunded; the refund record is created (completed state, original-payment method, the reason supplied); the books entry posts — **revenue debited, cash credited**; and the action is recorded for audit.\n3. Complete the refund lines: what was refunded per sale line — quantity, amount, and restock disposition. Line detail is what makes a partial refund auditable; refunding two of five items shall say which two, or inventory and revenue diverge.\n4. Refund to the original payment method whenever possible. It is what the customer expects, what the processor's rules favour, and what keeps the payment and refund records matching.\n\nTwo cautions belong to every refund:\n\n- The books entry credits **cash**, while the shop sale debited **checking**. For a card sale refunded to the original method, the money leaves the checking account, not the cash drawer. Where the bank reconciliation under Banking (§4.3.10) shows a checking difference equal to a recent refund, this account mismatch is the first suspect, and the entry may need to be moved to checking by a correcting entry under Books (§4.3.2).\n- The books posting is best-effort: an error is recorded and the refund still completes. A refunded order with no journal entry is revenue that was never reversed. The operator searches the journal by the refund or order identifier and posts the entry by hand where it is missing, under Troubleshooting (§4.3.15).\n\n## The refund the Platform did not issue\n\nA refund made in the processor's own console, instead of through the administrative action, produces a negative row in the payment records — and stops there. No refund record is created, no journal entry posts, and the order's state does not change. This is the most common source of a refund that is in the processor and not in the books.\n\n1. Weekly, the Accounting Operator takes the processor's refund list and matches each refund against the Platform's refund records.\n2. For every refund with no matching record, the operator creates the refund record and posts the revenue-reversal entry manually.\n3. Staff shall be trained to check the Platform's refund records for an order before touching the processor's console. The administrative action cannot double-refund an order; the console can, and an administrative refund plus a console refund for the same order is the classic double payment.\n\nA refund the books never see overstates revenue by exactly the refund amount, and the month-end close in Troubleshooting (§4.3.15) includes this match as a standing step.\n\n## Disputes and chargebacks\n\nA dispute is a buyer contesting a payment, through the Platform's process or through the buyer's bank. The dispute record carries the dispute number, the linked payment, sale, and refund records, the amount, the reason, the processor's dispute identifier, and the opened, responded, and resolved times. A dispute is not a refund; it is a contest over whether the payment was valid, with its own course.\n\n1. When a dispute opens, calendar the processor's deadline first. A missed deadline loses by default.\n2. Gather the evidence: the order record, delivery confirmation, customer communications, and any refund record. Evidence submitted late might as well not exist.\n3. Submit the evidence before the deadline and record the outcome either way. The win rate by reason shows which disputes are worth contesting and which recurring cause to fix at the source.\n\nChargebacks carry fees regardless of outcome, and the chargeback rate — chargebacks per hundred transactions — is a business-health measure. A rate approaching the card networks' limits is an emergency, and the fix is operational, not financial. Two rules protect the rate and the money:\n\n- Never issue a courtesy refund on a chargeback already decided against the Company; the money has already moved.\n- Never ignore a chargeback notice because a refund was already given. Submit the refund as evidence: an unanswered chargeback counts against the rate even where the customer had already been made whole.\n\nDisputes and chargebacks are handled under the Chargeback Policy (§2.1.35), which governs deadlines, evidence, and who bears each cost.\n\n## Marketplace clawbacks\n\nWhen destination charges begin under Payments (§4.3.4), a dispute decided against the Seller triggers a **transfer reversal**: the disputed amount is pulled out of the Seller's connected account balance and appears as a transaction against the Seller. Where the Company returns its own fee to make the buyer whole, that return is booked as a **reduction of fee revenue, not an expense** — a debit against the fee revenue account — because the fee was never truly earned on a reversed sale. Fraud or ignored disputes trigger capability gating: payouts freeze during investigation, appearing in the records as a balance that grows with no payouts. That is an enforcement hold, not a defect, and it is released only by the trust and safety decision.\n\n## Reading the refund measures\n\nThe refund rate — refunds as a share of sales — broken down by reason is the diagnostic: defective, not as described, late, changed mind. A steady climb indicates a product, service, or Seller problem. A sudden spike in one reason is an incident: the operator investigates the underlying sales before processing the refunds. A staff member with an unusually high refund rate may simply be working the complaints desk; the Accounting Manager establishes the context before the figures alarm anyone.\n\n## Controls\n\n- Separation of duties: the person who issues a refund does not reconcile the account it posts to, and no operator approves a refund the operator issued.\n- No refund is issued outside the administrative action where the action can issue it; console refunds are the exception path and are caught by the weekly match above.\n- A completed refund means the instruction went to the processor, not that the money arrived; card refunds take days to appear on a customer's statement. Before treating a refund as failed, the operator checks the refund's state and the processor's timeline.\n- Where a dispute is won but funds remain held, the release is the processor's act on the processor's timeline. The operator records the win, calendars the expected release, and does not adjust the Seller's balance by hand — a manual adjustment double-counts when the processor's release lands.\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nRefund records and refund lines, dispute and chargeback records with their evidence and outcomes, Seller dispute records, and the related journal entries are kept in the accounting and commerce records and retained under the Regulatory Record Retention Policy (§2.9.28).\n\n## Escalation\n\nA chargeback approaching a network limit, a dispute involving alleged fraud, or any legal process — a subpoena, a regulator's inquiry, a court notice — goes to legal@unsaidmemories.com on the day it is received. Refund system failures and unmatched console refunds go to the Accounting Manager and, where the cause is a system failure, to support@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Accounting Operator runs the processor-to-Platform refund match weekly. The Accounting Manager reviews the refund rate by reason and the dispute win rate monthly, and reviews this standard whenever the Returns and Refunds Policy (§2.1.19) or the Chargeback Policy (§2.1.35) changes.\n",
  "gift-cards-and-credits": "\n# Gift Cards and Credits\n\nThis standard governs gift cards and store credits in the accounting system of UnSaid Memories, LLC. Both are stored value — money the customer holds with the business — but they are tracked separately because they arise differently. A gift card is **purchased** value, usually given to someone else. Store credit is **granted** value, usually arising from a return and tied to the customer who earned it. Confusing the two corrupts both the liability accounting and the customer's experience. The Accounting Operator issues and redeems cards and credits; the Accounting Manager owns the outstanding-liability review and any recognition of expired value. Refunds that produce store credit are governed by the Returns and Refunds Policy (§2.1.19).\n\n## The one accounting fact\n\nGift card issuance is **not revenue**. The money collected is a liability: the Company owes the holder goods or services. Revenue is recognized when the card is **redeemed**. Getting this wrong overstates revenue in the selling period and understates it in the redemption period. The books entries state the distinction:\n\n1. **Issue:** **cash debited, gift-card liability credited** — posted automatically on a best-effort basis.\n2. **Redeem:** **gift-card liability debited, revenue credited** — the liability converts to revenue at the moment of the sale.\n\nThe gift-card liability balance on the balance sheet is real money owed. Outstanding liability that grows every month while redemptions do not grow alongside it means cards are being sold but not used — sound for cash flow today, a liability accumulating silently.\n\n## Issuing a gift card\n\n1. Record the card number (generated and unique), the initial value and currency, the purchaser and the recipient, the delivery method, the expiration, and the design.\n2. For a delivered card — by email or message — confirm the delivery recorded on the card. A card sold but never delivered is a support problem waiting to be reported; the operator confirms delivery on every digital issuance.\n3. Track and send expiration reminders. An expired card the customer did not know was expiring produces a complaint; a reminded customer who lets a card expire produces recognized breakage, addressed below.\n\nExpired unredeemed value may be subject to state unclaimed-property laws, which turn apparent free money into a compliance obligation. The Accounting Manager establishes the position for each relevant state before any expired value is treated as the Company's, under the control on breakage below.\n\n## Redeeming at the register\n\nA gift-card tender at the register is routed to **the gift-card liability directly**: the tender relieves the liability instead of touching cash. An earlier practice recorded gift-card tenders to checking, as though the card brought cash in; that overstated the bank and left the liability standing after redemption, so the books showed the Company owing money it had already honoured. Operators investigating a gift-card difference shall confirm the tender relieved the liability; see Register (§4.3.9).\n\n1. Look the card up by number: current balance, state, and expiration. The lookup is immediate; the customer is at the register.\n2. Where the card shows zero and the customer maintains there is value, read the transaction history before declaring the card empty. The value may have been converted to store credit, replaced by a new card, or redeemed in a transaction the customer has forgotten.\n\n## Store credit\n\nStore credit is issued, not sold — from a return, as a goodwill gesture, or by converting a gift card.\n\n1. Every credit carries a credit number, the customer, the original and current balances, and the **source**. A credit from a return references that return; a goodwill credit references the incident.\n2. A credit without a source is indistinguishable from money handed out without a record, which is exactly how it reads in an audit. No credit is issued without its source reference.\n3. Store credit posts differently from a gift card sale: it is a reduction of the original sale's revenue, through the refund, paired with a new liability — not new cash in. Refund to the original payment method whenever possible; where the governing policy calls for credit instead, the credit record is the proof, under Refunds and Disputes (§4.3.7).\n\n## Card lifecycle\n\nCards move through active, redeemed, expired, cancelled, converted, and replaced states.\n\n1. A **converted** card became store credit; the operator follows it through to the credit record and confirms the value arrived.\n2. A **replaced** card supersedes the original; the original's balance moves to the replacement and is never duplicated.\n3. The transaction history records every issuance, redemption, adjustment, and replacement with the balance before and after. Where a customer disputes a balance, the operator reads that history line by line before saying anything.\n4. Value, balance, and transaction history are never edited directly. Value changes happen only through recorded transactions. Administrative fields — recipient contact, delivery details, notes — may be edited; where a card's details are wrong beyond that, the operator replaces the card rather than altering the record.\n\n## Controls\n\n- **Breakage.** Expired unredeemed value is not automatically revenue. No operator recognizes breakage without a written position stating the dormancy period applied, the unclaimed-property rules checked, and the Accounting Manager's approval of the recognition entry (liability debited, revenue credited). Breakage recognized without that position is a liability removed by assertion.\n- **Missing books entry.** Issuance posts on a best-effort basis. A card sold without its cash-debited, liability-credited entry means the books show neither the money nor the obligation. The operator searches the journal by the card's source identifier and posts the entry manually where it is absent, under Troubleshooting (§4.3.15).\n- **Liability that never declines.** Counter redemptions relieve the liability through the register; redemptions through other paths — online checkout with a gift-card tender, manual adjustments — shall also relieve it. Where the liability only grows, some redemption path is posting to the wrong account, and the operator traces each path's journal entries by their source identifiers.\n- Separation of duties: the person who issues a goodwill credit does not approve it, and the person who records a redemption does not reconcile the gift-card liability.\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nGift card records and their transaction histories, card designs, store credit records and their transactions, and the related journal entries are kept in the commerce and accounting records and retained under the Regulatory Record Retention Policy (§2.9.28).\n\n## Escalation\n\nA disputed card balance the transaction history cannot resolve, any question of unclaimed-property treatment, and any suspected misuse of store credit go to the Accounting Manager; unclaimed-property and other legal questions go to legal@unsaidmemories.com. Issuance or redemption system failures go to support@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Accounting Manager reviews the outstanding gift-card liability and all goodwill credits monthly, reviews unredeemed expired value quarterly, and reviews this standard annually.\n",
  "register": "\n# Register\n\nThis standard governs the register — the point of sale where counter sales happen at the salon front desk, at pop-up events, and anywhere staff sell face to face. The register produces the fullest bookkeeping of any sale path on the Platform and carries the strictest safeguards, because counter sales move fast and mistakes at the counter accumulate by end of day. Register Operators run sales and the end-of-day drawer count; the Accounting Operator reviews register totals against the books; the Accounting Manager owns every drawer difference that cannot be explained on the day. The sale entry the register builds is described in Sales (§4.3.3).\n\n## What the register computes\n\nA register sale produces one balanced journal entry built from the sale's components. The register computes total debits and total credits independently, and if they differ by even a cent, **the sale is stopped** — no partial posting, no unbalanced entry. The safeguard exists because an unbalanced sale once passed through earlier handling and concealed understated revenue: balance cannot reliably be verified after the fact, so it is enforced at the moment of sale.\n\nThe entry, every time:\n\n1. **Payment-method accounts debited** — cash and check to the cash drawer, card to checking, and a gift-card tender relieves the gift-card liability instead of touching cash. The gift-card routing is deliberate: an older practice recorded gift-card tenders to checking, which overstated the bank and left the liability standing after redemption. The debit is the **sale total, not the tendered amount**; change due is a separate cash-out, not part of the revenue entry.\n2. **Customer deposits debited** for any booking deposit applied — the deposit was collected earlier and has been held in the liability account; applying it relieves the liability against the revenue below. Without this line the entry is short by exactly the deposit amount and the safeguard stops the sale. See Deposits (§4.3.6).\n3. **Cost of goods sold debited, inventory credited** — the register is the only sale path that recognizes cost at the moment of sale.\n4. **Sales discounts debited** — kept separate so gross revenue stays visible; net revenue is gross minus discounts.\n5. **Revenue credited** at the gross subtotal, and **sales tax payable credited** for the tax, under Taxes (§4.3.13).\n\nA tip folded into the tendered amount is limited so it cannot unbalance the entry.\n\n## Tenders and card payments\n\n1. Cash and check are simple: the drawer opens, the money goes in, and the record keeps the tendered amount and the change.\n2. Card payments follow one of four paths — keyed entry, payment link, card on file, manual entry — and each passes through a pending-charge record: authorize first, finalize on success, with the same journal posting as a completed cash sale.\n3. A card payment that authorizes but never finalizes is not a sale; it is an authorization that will expire. Where a receipt printed but the sale never completed, the operator checks the pending charges for an orphaned authorization before running the card again. Running it again creates a duplicate, and the customer receives two authorizations.\n4. Double-submission protection records each sale attempt for 24 hours and blocks the same submission twice. Where the register appears to do nothing on a second tap, the protection is working; the operator checks whether the first attempt completed before retrying. The protection covers the register submission, not the card authorization — a re-run card after an unclear failure is a separate risk, addressed below.\n\n## The end-of-day discipline\n\n1. **Count the drawer before closing.** Expected cash equals the opening float plus cash sales minus cash refunds minus cash paid out, all from the register's shift records.\n2. Record any difference in a cash over/short record — never in the sales figures. A drawer that does not balance is investigated, not adjusted: recount; check for tenders keyed to the wrong method (a card sale rung as cash is the classic); check for refunds paid from the drawer without a record.\n3. **Mid-day spot check.** On busy days, count the drawer mid-shift without closing it. A mid-day count that agrees means the morning was clean, and any end-of-day difference belongs to the afternoon. The spot check takes two minutes and halves every investigation that follows.\n4. **Reconcile payment methods.** The register's card totals shall match the processor's payout for the day, and the gift-card redemption totals shall relieve the liability by the same amount. Where card totals match the processor but the books do not, the sale posted operationally and its journal entry failed; the entry is posted manually under Troubleshooting (§4.3.15).\n\n## Failure modes\n\n1. **Sale stopped by the balance safeguard.** Something in the sale does not add up — usually a discount or tax line applied to one side, or a deposit applied whose amount does not match the held deposit. The operator corrects the components; the safeguard is reporting that the entry is wrong, and forcing it would post the wrong entry.\n2. **Duplicate charge from a re-run card.** The submission protection blocks a repeated register attempt, but re-running a card after an unclear failure can authorize twice. The operator always checks pending charges for an orphaned authorization from the first attempt before re-running.\n3. **Drawer short with no explanation.** After recounting and checking tender types, look at refunds: a cash refund paid from the drawer without a record is the most common unexplained shortage. Every cash movement requires a record, including the movements that feel like exceptions.\n4. **Gift-card tender that does not relieve the liability.** Where a card was accepted but the liability balance did not fall, the tender was recorded to the wrong account somewhere in the sequence. The operator verifies the sale's journal lines by their source reference, under Gift Cards and Credits (§4.3.8).\n\n## Controls\n\n- No operator overrides the balance safeguard, edits a completed sale entry, or recreates a payment to produce a receipt; corrections follow Books (§4.3.2) by reversing entry.\n- Every cash movement — sale, refund, payout from the drawer — carries a record at the time it happens. A refund from the drawer without a record is a control failure, not an informality, and refunds themselves are governed by the Returns and Refunds Policy (§2.1.19).\n- Separation of duties: the Register Operator who counts the drawer does not reconcile the register totals to the bank; the Accounting Operator performs that reconciliation under Banking (§4.3.10).\n- Drawer differences are recorded as over/short and investigated the same day; they are never absorbed into sales, discounts, or tips.\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nRegister sale records and their journal entries, pending-charge records, submission-protection records, shift and drawer-count records, and cash over/short records are kept in the commerce and accounting records and retained under the Regulatory Record Retention Policy (§2.9.28).\n\n## Escalation\n\nAn unexplained drawer difference, a duplicate customer charge, or a sale the safeguard repeatedly stops goes to the Accounting Manager the same day. Register or card-payment system failures go to support@unsaidmemories.com. Suspected theft or legal process goes to legal@unsaidmemories.com on the day it arises.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nDrawer counts happen daily and at mid-shift on busy days. The Accounting Manager reviews over/short records weekly and reviews this standard whenever the register's posting or tender handling changes.\n",
  "organization": "\n# Organization\n\nThis standard governs the organization record of UnSaid Memories, LLC — the single tenant record the whole Platform reads to know who the business is — together with its brand fields, its locations, and its operating hours. The storefront header, booking confirmations, receipts, transactional email, and franchise portals all take brand, contact, and legal identity from this one record; there is no second copy. Where a surface displays the wrong business name or telephone number, the correction is made here, not in the surface that displayed it. The Organization Administrator maintains this record; only a holder of the administration grant at the highest level, or an owner, may change the legal identity, domain, or currency fields, under Admin Users (§4.4.2). No setting in this record overrides a governing legal document: where this record and a signed agreement, a filing, or a policy in §2 of this library differ, the legal document governs and this record is corrected to match it.\n\n## The tenant record\n\nThe record carries three kinds of fields:\n\n1. **Identity:** the display name customers see, and the slug — the human-readable identifier referenced across the Platform. The slug is treated as permanent; changing it breaks references in places nobody will think to check, and it is changed only by an owner, with the consequences recorded.\n2. **Legal:** the legal name, the doing-business-as name, and the tax identifier. These fields flow into invoices, receipts, payout tax documents, and franchise agreements. The display name is not a substitute for the registered entity name on a tax form or a franchise agreement.\n3. **Contact:** the support email, support telephone number, and website address presented to customers. The only email addresses presented as ways to reach the Company are support@unsaidmemories.com for operational matters and legal@unsaidmemories.com for legal notices; no other address is entered as a Company contact point.\n\nThe legal procedure is:\n\n1. Enter the legal name exactly as it appears on the state registration, and complete the legal fields before they are needed — they are needed at the worst times.\n2. When any identity fact changes in the real world — a rebrand, an entity change, a new tax identifier — update this record in the same week, then verify the places it flows to: produce a test invoice, check the payment processor's business profile, and confirm a transactional email footer.\n\n**Domains.** Two fields do different jobs. The primary domain is the domain the Platform treats as home: the links the Platform publishes as its own, its search identity, and its site map are built from it. The custom domain points the business's own branded domain at the Platform. The primary domain is changed only during a quiet period, and the search indexing is re-verified afterward; every published link and every absolute link in email already sent shifts with the change.\n\n**Currency.** The default currency, United States dollars, is the currency of record: every price, payout, and report is stated in it. Changing it on a live business is a migration, not a switch — historical data does not convert itself — and it is not done by an operator.\n\n**Presentation preferences.** A small set of presentation preferences — a geographic suffix, a price-range indicator, display limits — changes presentation, not data. The operator reads the current value before changing any of them.\n\n**Processor linkage.** The tenant record also carries the Platform-level processor account linkage, separate from the per-settings processor keys in Settings (§4.4.4). Where payouts misbehave, this record and the processor's own console are the two places to check; see Payments (§4.3.4).\n\n## Brand and identity\n\nThe logo, the dark-mode logo, the favicon, the primary, secondary, and accent colours, the brand font, and the social links in this record are the single source for how the business looks on every surface: storefront header, customer portal, email templates, receipts, and the franchisee portals that inherit the brand.\n\n1. Logos are entered as addresses of hosted images the Company controls. If the hosted image becomes unavailable, every surface referring to it breaks at once; there is no local fallback.\n2. The dark-mode logo keeps the mark legible on dark headers and in email clients that invert backgrounds. Where it is absent, the standard logo is used everywhere, washed out in dark contexts.\n3. Colours are entered as hexadecimal values. The primary colour drives buttons and key actions, the secondary supports, and the accent is used for highlights and badges. A change made here propagates, but not instantly everywhere: stored storefront pages and email already sent keep the old values. A rebrand has a transition period, and yesterday's email showing yesterday's colour is not a defect.\n4. Social links are the business's social profiles, rendered in footers and on the contact page. A link to a profile that no longer exists is worse than no link; the Organization Administrator keeps them current.\n\n## Locations\n\nThe Platform keeps two location systems, and the operator shall know which one a given screen reads before changing either.\n\n**The current system** holds the live locations — for the Company, its main location, in the America/Chicago time zone, marked primary and active. The administrative Locations screen reads and writes this system, and the tenant switcher in the administrative sidebar reads from it. Franchise signup, under Franchise Signup (§4.4.7), creates a location row in this system for a new shop's primary physical location. Each row carries a name, a structured address — street lines, city, state, postal code, telephone, email — a time zone, a primary marker, and an active marker.\n\n1. Only one location is ever primary. The primary location is the default wherever one location is required: tax jurisdiction defaults, the return address on shipping labels, and the fallback where nothing more specific is set. Two primaries mean downstream handling guesses, and guessing produces intermittent wrong-address faults that take weeks to notice.\n2. Older rows may hold the address as a single line of text rather than the structured form; the screens accommodate both, and operators do not rewrite old rows by hand for tidiness.\n\n**The older system** holds earlier location rows — including near-duplicate rows for the same premises, differing only by a dash character in the name. This is recorded duplication, not a mystery: the administrative Locations screen, the tenant switcher, employee time records, and franchise signup read the current system, while one settings component still refers to the older table. Until that reference is retired, both sets exist and both can appear in different pickers. The procedure is:\n\n1. Do not delete the older rows yet. Something still reads them; deleting first and discovering what second is how outages happen.\n2. Merge or deactivate the near-duplicate main-location rows. Two rows that differ by a dash character will eventually diverge — one receives the new telephone number, the other does not — and then there are two sources of truth. Deactivate, rather than delete, the row that loses, so the history remains auditable.\n3. Add every real new premises in the current system, through the Locations screen. That is the system the current procedures read.\n\n**Time zone discipline.** Every slot a customer sees, every \"today\" in every report, and every cutoff calculation resolves in the location's time zone. America/Chicago is set on the first day for any new row. A wrong time zone produces no error; it produces appointments off by an hour, which customers experience as the business being unreliable.\n\n## Operating hours\n\nOperating hours are kept one row per location per day, and the booking engine enforces them as binding: no slots are offered outside them. Holiday and one-off closures are kept as blackout records, which the system also applies to availability. Booking rules that sit alongside hours are governed by Booking Operations (§4.4.3).\n\nBoth are managed from the Operating Hours & Holidays screen, which also exposes the online-booking switch — the exact setting the public booking path checks. Where online booking is unexpectedly off, that switch is checked before anything else.\n\n1. Never delete an hours row for a closed day; mark the day closed. A missing row is ambiguous — closed, or data missing — and different parts of the Platform resolve the ambiguity differently. An explicit closed marker is unambiguous everywhere.\n2. The Platform does not move existing appointments when hours change. After any hours change, the operator takes the upcoming appointments for the affected days and handles the exceptions personally. A personal call costs less goodwill than an automated cancellation.\n3. Enter blackouts the moment they are decided — Thanksgiving, Christmas, staff training. Online booking reads them as they are entered, so a customer booking in October for late December correctly sees the closure where it has been entered. Where it has not, the customer books a slot the business must then cancel, and a cancellation the business initiates always costs more goodwill than a slot never offered.\n\n## Adding a location — the sequence\n\n1. **Create the location.** In the Locations screen, add the new location: name, address, time zone, and the tenant it belongs to. Leave the primary marker off unless this location replaces the current primary.\n2. **Set the hours.** Add the weekly schedule in Operating Hours & Holidays — every day, including closed days marked closed. A location with no hours rows is effectively closed every day as far as the booking engine is concerned; that is the most common cause of \"the location was added but nobody can book.\"\n3. **Enter blackouts** for any known closures.\n4. **Confirm capabilities.** Where the location fulfils orders, confirm it is the location the fulfilment handling expects. Where customers book grooming there, confirm it appears among the bookable locations.\n5. **Verify.** Book a test appointment, confirm it lands in the right staff calendars, and check the address on a test order. Then tell the team the location is live.\n\nWhere something goes wrong mid-sequence — hours attached to the wrong location, a typing error in the address — the operator updates the rows in place. Locations are not deleted and recreated: deletions cascade in ways that are hard to audit, while updates leave a clear record.\n\n## Controls\n\n- **Who may change what.** Legal identity, tax identifier, domains, and currency: an owner, or an administrator holding the administration grant at the highest level. Brand fields, contact fields, and presentation preferences: the Organization Administrator. Locations and hours: the Organization Administrator and location managers for their own location. Every change is attributable to the person who made it.\n- A setting in this record never overrides a governing legal document — a franchise agreement, a filing, the Terms of Use (§2.1.1), or any policy in §2. Where a value here conflicts with such a document, the document governs and the record is corrected.\n- The slug, the primary domain, and the currency are treated as structural: they are changed rarely, deliberately, and with the downstream verification this standard states.\n- Banking and processor credentials are never entered in this record and never recorded in any document; processor linkage here is an account reference, not a credential.\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nChanges to the tenant record, location rows and their deactivations, hours and blackout records, and the verification results this standard requires are kept in the Platform's administrative records, with the change history the Platform records for administrative actions. Organization records are retained under the Regulatory Record Retention Policy (§2.9.28).\n\n## Escalation\n\nA conflict between this record and a legal document, a domain or identity change with legal effect, or service of process addressed to the Company goes to legal@unsaidmemories.com on the day it arises. Operational problems — a location that will not appear, hours that will not apply — go to support@unsaidmemories.com, after the checks in Troubleshooting (§4.4.8).\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Organization Administrator reviews the full record quarterly — legal fields, domains, brand fields, social links, locations, and hours — verifies the registered identity against the state registration annually, and reviews this standard whenever the Platform's location or booking handling changes.\n",
  "admin-users": "\n# Admin Users\n\nThis standard governs administrative access to the Platform: who holds an account, what role they hold, which modules that role opens, and how access is granted, suspended, and reviewed. Access control here is role first, then module by module: the operator selects the role on the Users screen, and the screen presents the editor that matches that kind of relationship to the business. Hand-tuning grants before choosing the right role produces an illegible access position. Only an owner, or an administrator holding the administration grant at the highest level, may create accounts, change roles, or change grants; every such change is recorded with the person who made it, the earlier value, and the new value. No access setting in this standard overrides a governing legal document, an employment decision, or a franchise agreement.\n\n## The eight roles\n\nThe roster recognizes eight role types. Each is a different kind of relationship to the business, and each receives a different editor:\n\n1. **Owner** — passes every check. Presented as a simple control. There shall be very few owners; every owner is a person who can undo anything, including another operator's restrictions.\n2. **Internal employee** — the Company's staff. Receives the module tree described below: every module at None, Read, Write, or All. This is the role used most.\n3. **Franchise owner** — full administration, limited to their own tenant. Receives a tenant selector, or the new-franchise flow in Franchise Signup (§4.4.7). They run their shop; they do not touch the Company's.\n4. **Franchise employee** — the module tree again, limited to franchise-scoped modules.\n5. **Seller** — category selections, Pro standing, and capabilities. Marketplace Sellers, not staff, governed additionally by the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1).\n6. **Veterinarian** — action controls plus a licence profile, for telehealth providers.\n7. **Pharmacy** — action controls, a licence profile, and an acknowledgment concerning protected health information. That acknowledgment is not decoration; it records that this role touches prescription handling.\n8. **Groomer partner** — lead modules and lead actions only. Referral partners, not employees.\n\nCustomers hold no rows on this roster. That is deliberate: customers have a uniform experience, and there is nothing to configure per customer on the access side.\n\nThe roster shows, for each person, a count of the explicit grants they hold beyond their role. Where that count is high for someone whose job is simple, that is the cue to review. Grants accumulate; nobody removes them unprompted, which is why the review cadence below exists.\n\n## The module tree\n\nModules are the coarse control. Five are in use:\n\n1. **Accounting** — settings, reports, taxes, payroll, books, invoices, payments, deposits, refunds, gift cards, and the processor connection, governed by §4.3 of this library.\n2. **Customer management** — the dashboard, customers, pets, appointments, grooming records, calendar, services, staff, and schedule.\n3. **Orders** — inventory, orders, purchase orders, returns, and shipping.\n4. **System** — settings, governed by Settings (§4.4.4) and System (§4.4.5).\n5. **Support** — reply, approve actions, delete, export, merge, view, confidential material, internal notes, macros, administration, and copyright-removal handling.\n\nEach module is granted at one of four levels: **None**, **Read**, **Write**, or **All**. The matrix is designed around least privilege, kept legible:\n\n1. Owners hold everything.\n2. Managers hold their department's modules at All.\n3. Front-desk staff hold customer appointments and customers at Write, but not refunds or settings.\n4. Accountants hold Accounting, but not staff management.\n\nWhen a person changes jobs, their grants are updated the same day. Stale permissions are the most common source of \"how did they do that,\" and they are entirely preventable. Every permission change is recorded in the permission audit record with the earlier and new values, who made the change, and when. That record answers \"who gave the new hire refund permission\" and allows a bad grant to be unwound without guessing.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> A grant is a setting, and a setting never overrides a governing legal document. A module grant does not authorize a person to act contrary to a policy in §2 of this library — for example, an Accounting grant does not authorize a refund outside the Returns and Refunds Policy (§2.1.19), and a Support grant does not authorize disclosure contrary to the Privacy Policy (§2.1.2).\n\n## Invitations and lifecycle\n\nNobody receives a sign-in directly. Access begins with an invitation:\n\n1. The administrator creates the membership record and sends the invitation. Until the invitation is accepted, the person has nothing, whatever the role says.\n2. The record carries who sent the invitation, when it was sent, and when it expires. Invitations expire on purpose; an invitation months old in an inbox is a standing vulnerability.\n3. The invitation link is stored only as a protected reference, never in readable form. A database read cannot reconstruct a working link. Where someone asks an operator to simply state the link, the honest answer is that the operator cannot — the administrator resends, and the system issues a fresh link, leaving the old one dead.\n\nThe pending-invitation list is worked as a queue, not read as a report. Every pending invitation is a person who cannot yet do their job:\n\n1. **Expired:** resend. The old link stays dead; that is the secure behaviour.\n2. **Never arrived:** check the sent time first; the cause is almost always a mail filter or a mistyped address. Correct the address, then resend.\n\nThe last-active time on each membership is the access-review tool. Quarterly, the administrator sorts by it: anyone inactive for ninety days probably should not hold an active role. Access nobody uses is access nobody will notice being misused.\n\nOne person holds one membership record. Where someone needs a different role, the administrator changes the role on the existing record; a second record is never created. Duplicates are how a person ends up suspended in one record and fully active in another.\n\n## Suspending and deactivating\n\nSuspension is the emergency brake: the membership is made inactive, and, for scheduling and register access, the staff record is made inactive with it. History is preserved — appointments the person handled, notes they wrote, audit entries. The past is not rewritten when someone leaves.\n\n1. **Suspend first, investigate second, decide third.** In a termination conversation, suspension happens during it, not after. Where misuse is suspected, suspend, then read the audit records; a person under investigation with live access does more damage in an hour than the investigation takes.\n2. Suspension does not reassign open work. Appointments under a suspended groomer still show that groomer until someone moves them; open register sessions, pending approvals, and assigned conversations sit where they were. After suspending, the administrator walks the person's active responsibilities and reassigns each one. The Platform preserves the data; the handoff is the administrator's.\n3. Reactivation restores the record as it was — history, permissions, and settings intact. That is the advantage over deletion: a pause, not an ending. Deletion is reserved for the rare case it truly fits, and even then the deactivated record is preferred. The audit history is worth more than tidiness.\n\n## Two-factor authentication resets\n\nA reset is the recovery path where a person loses the device their second factor lives on, and it is two steps by design: an administrator records the request — who requested it and which administrator recorded it — and the user enrols afresh at the next sign-in. A reset is never silent; there is always a record of who asked and who approved.\n\n1. Verify identity before approving. \"I am locked out, please reset my authentication\" is exactly what an attacker says too. The administrator calls back on a known number or confirms in person. Thirty seconds of awkwardness is the entire security of this procedure.\n2. After a reset, the old device is fully detached; the new enrolment replaces it.\n3. Repeated resets for the same person are a signal, not a routine. The administrator establishes which it is: a chaotic device situation, or someone testing the procedure.\n\n## What the Users screen enforces\n\nEvery read and write on the Users screen passes through the administration gate: the person operating the screen needs the administration module at the right level. Where the screen loads empty or saves fail, the cause is usually the gate, not the data. The permission model applies to its own administration — an operator needs permission to grant permission.\n\n## Controls\n\n- **Who may change access.** Owners, and administrators holding the administration grant at the highest level. Nobody grants a module they do not themselves hold at the level granted, and nobody approves their own grant increase.\n- Access is granted by role and least-privilege module levels, reviewed quarterly against last-active times and grant counts.\n- Payroll-related access is additionally limited under Payroll (§4.3.12): payroll records are confidential, and an Accounting grant is narrowed for payroll to the roles that standard names.\n- Banking and processor credentials are never created, shared, or reset through this screen, and are never recorded in any document; see Banking (§4.3.10).\n- Suspension takes effect immediately and is never delayed for convenience; reactivation requires the same authority that suspension does.\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nMembership and roster records, invitation records with their send and expiry times, the permission audit record for every grant change, suspension and reactivation records, and authentication-reset requests are kept in the Platform's administrative records and retained under the Regulatory Record Retention Policy (§2.9.28).\n\n## Escalation\n\nSuspected misuse of access, a grant nobody can account for, or a reset request that fails identity verification goes to an owner the same day; suspected compromise of an account and any legal process touching access records go to legal@unsaidmemories.com. Operational failures of the Users screen go to support@unsaidmemories.com, after the gate check above and the checks in Troubleshooting (§4.4.8).\n\n## Review cadence\n\nAccess is reviewed quarterly: last-active times, grant counts beyond role, pending invitations, and owner accounts. This standard is reviewed whenever a role or module is added or its scope changes.\n",
  "booking-operations": "\n# Booking Operations\n\nThis standard governs the booking settings of UnSaid Memories, LLC: every rule about when customers can book, how far ahead, what cancellation costs, and whether money is taken in advance. The rules are held as booking settings, read directly by the booking engine, and managed through the Booking Rules and Operating Hours & Holidays screens in the settings hub. There is no separate booking configuration elsewhere. The Booking Operations Administrator — the operations manager for grooming and services — owns these settings; front-desk staff propose changes and do not make them. A booking setting never overrides a governing legal document: cancellation and deposit terms shown to a customer are applied as the governing policy states them, including the Returns and Refunds Policy (§2.1.19), and a fee is charged only where the customer agreed to it at booking under those terms.\n\n## When a rule does not appear to apply\n\nThe investigation order is fixed:\n\n1. Is the setting saved?\n2. Is it saved in the right place?\n3. Is the stored copy the engine reads still current? Settings reads are held briefly before refreshing, so a change is given a few minutes before it is judged broken.\n4. Is something more specific overriding it — hours, a blackout, or a service-level value?\n\nHours live with the organization record and are governed by Organization (§4.4.1); this standard governs the booking rules that work alongside them.\n\n## The booking settings and their defaults\n\nThe following are the Platform defaults. A tenant's values may differ where they have been changed; where a setting has never been touched, the default is what the engine uses.\n\n1. **Booking horizon — 90 days.** How far ahead anyone can book. Without a cap, a customer books next year's holiday appointment under this year's prices and hours. Ninety days covers the holiday booking window for grooming; it is shortened only where the planning horizon genuinely cannot hold that far.\n2. **Cancellation cutoff — 24 hours.** The line between free cancellation and late cancellation. It is matched to reality: where a cancelled slot refills from the waitlist within hours, a shorter cutoff can be justified; where a late cancellation means an empty station, 24 hours stands. Whatever is set is enforced for everyone or for no one — staff waiving it \"just this once\" for regulars creates two policies, the written one and the real one, and customers talk to each other.\n3. **Cancellation fee — none by default. No-show fee — none by default.** A no-show charge works only where the customer agreed to it at booking and a payment method is on file, which is why these settings are companions to the deposit settings below. Where a fee is set, it is set high enough to change behaviour and not so high it manufactures disputes, and it is applied the same day: a charge landing three days later feels arbitrary, while a same-day charge reads as the policy working.\n4. **No-show grace period — 15 minutes** after the appointment time before the appointment is counted a no-show. This is not set to zero.\n5. **Deposits — off by default; where on, 25 percent, with a flat-amount alternative.** The deposit requirement is the single most effective no-show measure: a customer with money down attends. A percentage scales with the service — a full groom carries a larger deposit than a nail trim, matching the larger hole a no-show leaves; a flat amount is simpler to state aloud. Deposit handling in the books is governed by Deposits (§4.3.6). Where deposits are enabled, the full course is tested before real customers touch it: book, pay the deposit, cancel inside the window, and verify the refund lands cleanly. Discovering a broken refund path through an angry customer is the expensive way to learn.\n6. **Turnaround buffer — 15 minutes** of invisible time between appointments at a station: cleaning, tool sanitizing, table reset. The engine adds it to the service duration when looking for the next free slot, so a 60-minute groom occupies 75 minutes of station time. It is set honestly: too short creates no capacity, only a cascade in which every appointment starts late and by afternoon the salon is half an hour behind with a full lobby. It is reviewed seasonally; shedding-season cleaning takes longer than January cleaning.\n7. **Default duration — 120 minutes,** the fallback where a service has no duration of its own.\n8. **Overbooking threshold — 10 percent** past nominal capacity before the engine treats booking as overbooking.\n9. **Automatic confirmation — off by default.** Where it is off, bookings need a human confirmation — the right posture for a chronically full schedule. Where it is on, the engine confirms directly. The posture is chosen to match the current goal and revisited quarterly; the right answer changes as the business does.\n10. **Required vaccines — Rabies, DHPP, Bordetella.** The intake requirement, shown where customers book and checked at arrival.\n\n## Hours, blackouts, and the online switch\n\nHours are kept one row per location per day and are enforced as binding by the booking engine: no slots exist outside them, ever. The hours view brings together the hours, the blackout list, and the online-booking switch in one place.\n\n1. Blackouts record closures and are applied to availability as they are entered. The big ones are added the moment they are decided — Thanksgiving, Christmas, New Year. Online booking reads them as entered, so an October booking for late December correctly shows the closure. The note on each blackout is written for the operator's future self: months later, facing a revenue gap, \"Thanksgiving\" is the difference between an answer and a mystery. Old blackouts are not deleted; they are the record of when the business was actually closed.\n2. The online-booking switch is the master control the public booking path checks. Where online booking is unexpectedly unavailable, that switch is checked first, before hours or rules are touched.\n3. Hours-change discipline follows Organization (§4.4.1): the Platform does not move existing appointments when hours change, so after any change the operator takes the upcoming appointments for the affected days and handles the exceptions personally; and a closed day is marked closed, never deleted, because a missing row is ambiguous and an explicit closed marker is not.\n\n## How the pieces interact\n\nThe engine needs hours and rules to agree, and the administrator changes one thing at a time so that, where the figures look wrong afterward, there is exactly one suspect:\n\n1. Shortening hours without checking strands existing appointments outside the new window; the system will not move them.\n2. Enabling deposits without a working refund path manufactures disputes.\n3. A 24-hour cancellation cutoff without a waitlist feels punitive; with one, a cancelled slot resells itself.\n\n## Controls\n\n- **Who may change booking settings.** The Booking Operations Administrator, and an owner. Front-desk staff, groomers, and franchise staff for another tenant may not change the Company's booking rules; franchise tenants set their own booking rules within their own tenant, under Franchise Signup (§4.4.7).\n- **What a change causes.** A booking-rule change applies to new bookings as the engine next reads the settings; it does not move, reprice, or cancel existing appointments. A change to fees or deposit requirements applies only where the customer agrees to the new terms at booking; appointments already booked are handled under the terms in force when they were made.\n- A booking setting never overrides a governing legal document. Where a setting and the Returns and Refunds Policy (§2.1.19), a cancellation term shown to the customer, or any policy in §2 differ, the legal document governs and the setting is corrected.\n- Deposit money is handled only under Deposits (§4.3.6): a deposit is a liability until applied or forfeited under its terms, and it is never treated as revenue at booking.\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nBooking setting values and their change history, hours and blackout records, and the test-booking results this standard requires are kept in the Platform's settings and administrative records and retained under the Regulatory Record Retention Policy (§2.9.28).\n\n## Escalation\n\nA booking rule that will not apply after the investigation order above, or a deposit refund path that fails its test, goes to support@unsaidmemories.com. A dispute with a customer over a cancellation fee, a no-show charge, or a deposit goes to the Booking Operations Administrator, and legal process to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nBooking settings are reviewed quarterly — horizon, cutoff, fees, buffer, and confirmation posture — and the buffer is reviewed seasonally. This standard is reviewed whenever the booking engine's use of a setting changes.\n",
  "settings": "\n# Settings\n\nThis standard governs the Platform's settings: the two stores that look like one, what each holds, who may change each class of value, and what a change causes. Confusing the two stores is the source of half of all \"it was changed and nothing happened\" reports. The System Administrator owns the settings stores; money, tax, and payment values are changed only with the Accounting Manager's concurrence, under §4.3 of this library. Every change made through the administrative settings screens is recorded — who changed what, and when — and that record is where an operator looks first when figures look wrong after a change. A setting never overrides a governing legal document: fees exist only as the Seller Fee Schedule (§2.3.14) states them, tax is applied as Taxes (§4.3.13) and the Marketplace Facilitator Tax Policy (§2.1.17) state it, and no setting changes an obligation stated in §2 of this library.\n\n## The two stores\n\n1. **The administrative settings store** holds the content values every screen in the settings hub reads and writes — business profile, brand, booking rules, processor connection, invoices, portals, social, and system — grouped as general, contact, social, hours, and footer values. Where the store cannot be reached, the screens fall back to the built-in defaults rather than failing. A settings page showing suspiciously clean defaults is therefore reporting that the database connection is down, not that the settings were wiped; the operator checks the connection before touching any setting, under Troubleshooting (§4.4.8).\n2. **The runtime settings store** holds the key-and-value rows the storefront and scheduled work read directly — pricing, catalog, stock, synchronisation, shipping, telehealth, and breed data. Reads are held briefly in a short-lived copy before refreshing, and a missing or malformed row yields the documented default rather than a failure. A change here takes effect within that refresh interval, not instantly.\n\n## Company and currency\n\nThe tenant-level facts — legal name, doing-business-as name, tax identifier, support contacts — are set in the business profile under Organization (§4.4.1) and appear in settings for a different purpose: compliance, not identity. The legal name and tax identifier here shall match the payment processor's records for the Company. Mismatches do not fail quickly; they fail expensively — a year-end tax form under the wrong name, a payout held for verification.\n\nThe tenant's default currency, United States dollars, is the currency of record. Changing it on a live business is a migration, not a switch, and is never done as a settings change. See Organization (§4.4.1).\n\n## Taxes — the displayed rate is derived, not stored\n\nThe tax rate shown in the business profile is **not stored anywhere**. It is a live view of the tax rules, computed on every read, because the register prices from the tax rules and the displayed rate is derived so that no separate copy can drift from them.\n\nOperationally:\n\n1. The tax rate is not set in Settings. The operator maintains the tax rules, under Taxes (§4.3.13), and the displayed rate follows.\n2. Saving a new rate in the business profile rebalances the tenant's tax rules to the new total; it never writes a rate value into the settings store. Where tax totals look wrong, the operator checks the rules, not the settings screen: the screen is a mirror, and the rules are the thing.\n3. A companion set of tax definitions carries each tax's name, rate, type, whether it compounds, and whether it is enabled. Rate-change discipline: where a rate changes, the old definition is disabled and a new one created with the new rate and its effective date in its name. Historical invoices keep pointing at the rate actually in effect when they were issued, which is what an examiner wants. Editing a rate in place rewrites history, and rewritten history is what gets questioned.\n4. Jurisdiction comes from the location involved in the sale, under Organization (§4.4.1), not from a global switch. A pickup at the Memphis store and a shipment to Nashville can carry different treatment. Where tax totals look wrong, the operator checks the location's address data before the tax definitions: the rate is usually right, and the jurisdiction is usually what is off.\n5. The whole tax setup is reviewed with the Company's accountant annually. Tax rules change quietly, and the cost of being wrong accumulates with every transaction.\n\n## Pricing, shipping, and the runtime keys\n\nThe runtime values that shape what customers pay and see include:\n\n1. **The markup multiplier,** which derives retail prices from cost. This is a business-strategy figure wearing a settings costume: changing it reprices the pricing basis for every cost-derived product. Before touching it, the operator establishes whether stored prices recompute or only new ones do. The pricing rule itself is stated in Sales (§4.3.3) and Purchases (§4.3.11).\n2. **Autoship discounts** — the first-order and recurring subscribe-and-save economics.\n3. **Shipping values** — the shipping tiers, the holiday surcharge, and the shipping club's monthly and annual amounts, as data.\n4. **Stock values** — the low-stock threshold (default 5), whether exact counts are shown (default off), and whether backorders are allowed (default off). These shape the urgency a customer sees and whether out-of-stock items remain purchasable.\n5. **Catalog values** — products per page and whether empty categories are hidden. Presentation values, but they interact: hiding empty categories while showing exact stock counts produces a very different browsing experience from either alone.\n6. **Synchronisation values** — whether feed synchronisation is enabled, its interval in minutes, and its rate-limit pause in minutes.\n\nThe discipline for all of them: change deliberately, one at a time, know what reads each value before changing it, look at the storefront after each change, and never change two money-related settings at once.\n\n## Payments and the processor connection\n\nThe processor connection is held as an account reference, a connection state (connected, not connected, or pending), and a mode (live or test) in the settings store, together with the tenant-level connected account reference in Organization (§4.4.1).\n\n1. The default mode is **test**, and the default account reference is a placeholder. An operator who has never completed the connection flow is looking at placeholders, not a live connection; connection is verified in the processor's own console, not only in the settings screen.\n2. The two flags that matter day to day are whether charges are enabled and whether payouts are enabled. Charges without payouts means the Company can take money it cannot receive — an unpleasant discovery on payout day. Where onboarding stalls, the operator checks the processor's console for outstanding verification requirements before treating the Platform as at fault.\n3. The money arrangement these settings control is stated in Payments (§4.3.4): connected accounts with destination charges, the platform fee taken at capture, a 7–14 day rolling payout delay, and clawbacks as transfer reversals. The settings are the control surface for that arrangement, not a substitute for understanding it.\n\nProcessor credentials are never shared between operators, never recorded in a settings note or any other document, and never read back from a screen to be written down. A credential is verified as configured, never copied.\n\n## Financial connections\n\nConnected bank accounts are recorded with a name, the bank, identifiers, the currency, the processor linkage, and the last synchronised time.\n\n1. Accounts are named clearly — an operating checking account named for its bank and purpose, not \"Account 1\" — because the name is what an operator reads during a midnight reconciliation, under Banking (§4.3.10).\n2. Where the last synchronised time goes stale, the operator re-authenticates the connection; the record is never edited by hand. Editing a bank record by hand is how the Platform and the bank begin to disagree about what the account is, and that is how reconciliation breaks.\n3. Access to financial connections is the most restricted in the administrative surface — few enough holders to count on one hand, with every change attributable. Bank fraud does not look like a break-in; it looks like a legitimate-looking change by someone with legitimate-looking access. The grant discipline in Admin Users (§4.4.2) exists for exactly this: the Accounting module is granted narrowly, and grants are reviewed when anyone with access changes roles or leaves.\n4. Adding or replacing an account: add the new one, verify a small test transaction, then remove the old. The only payout destination is never removed while payouts are pending; the failure that follows is not an error message, it is money with nowhere to go.\n\n## Controls\n\n- **Who may change each class of setting.** General, contact, social, and footer values: the System Administrator and the Organization Administrator. Booking values: the Booking Operations Administrator, under Booking Operations (§4.4.3). Pricing, shipping, and stock values: the System Administrator with the Accounting Manager's concurrence. Tax values: only through the tax rules, under Taxes (§4.3.13). Processor and bank connection values: the System Administrator and the Accounting Manager jointly, and no one else.\n- **What a change causes.** Administrative settings take effect as the screens next read them; runtime settings take effect within their refresh interval. A change to a money value affects new transactions only; it never reprices, retaxes, or re-fees a completed transaction, which is corrected only under Books (§4.3.2).\n- A setting never overrides a governing legal document. Where a setting value and a document in §2 of this library — a fee schedule, a tax policy, the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1) — differ, the legal document governs and the setting is corrected to match.\n- Every change through the administrative settings screens is recorded with its maker and time; an unrecorded change is treated as not having been made through an approved path and is investigated.\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nSetting values in both stores, the change record for every administrative change, processor and bank connection records, and test-transaction results are kept in the Platform's settings and administrative records and retained under the Regulatory Record Retention Policy (§2.9.28).\n\n## Escalation\n\nA change that takes effect wrongly, a money or tax value changed without the concurrence this standard requires, or a bank connection change nobody can account for goes to the System Administrator and the Accounting Manager the same day; suspected fraud goes to legal@unsaidmemories.com. Settings that will not save or that show defaults go first through Troubleshooting (§4.4.8), then to support@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nMoney, tax, pricing, and connection settings are reviewed quarterly against the governing documents they implement. The full settings position is reviewed annually with the Company's accountant for tax values. This standard is reviewed whenever a new settings store, class, or key is introduced.\n",
  "system": "\n# System\n\nThis standard governs the system health position of the Platform: backups, tenant live state, connection health, and the administrative access record. Everything else in this section configures the business; this standard states whether the machinery underneath it is healthy. Most days it is glanced at for ten seconds. The days it is needed, it is needed badly — usually at the worst possible moment — so every System Administrator learns what each measure means before a warning appears. The System Administrator owns system health, backups, and restore testing; tenant live state is changed only by an owner or the System Administrator, for a recorded reason. A system setting never overrides a governing legal document, and no health measure in this standard changes an obligation stated anywhere in this library.\n\n## Backups — the measure to be certain about\n\nEvery backup is recorded: its type, scope, start and finish times, size, storage location, row and table counts, and — on failure — the error message. Completed, running, and failed backups all appear in the record.\n\n1. The failed record is the most important one in the list. A backup system that never reports failure is a backup system that cannot be trusted; a reported failure is the system doing its job.\n2. On a failure, the System Administrator reads the error message first — it is usually specific: storage unreachable, a timeout on a large table — corrects the cause, and then runs a fresh backup. A backup is never simply re-run in hope; hope is not a backup strategy.\n\nBackups are a paid addition, enabled per tenant. A tenant without the addition sees the offer state, and backup runs are refused by the system regardless of what the screen shows — there is no way around the entitlement and no accidental way into it. An entitled tenant receives nightly backups and a Run Backup Now control, with download links that expire after 1 hour. The hour is short on purpose: the link admits its holder to the entire database. Where a longer window is needed, a fresh backup is run rather than a link extended.\n\nTwo disciplines govern backups:\n\n1. **Know the recovery window.** The oldest available backup is the furthest back a restore can reach. Where that point is newer than the problem being corrected, there is no restore path. That is discovered during a calm quarterly review, not during an incident.\n2. **Test a restore once a quarter.** A restore that has been tested is a restore that works; one that has not is a hypothesis.\n\n## Tenant live state\n\nThe live indicator reads the tenant record's active marker and state, under Organization (§4.4.1). Active means the storefront, booking, portals, and event notices run normally. Legitimate reasons to be offline are staging a new franchise before opening day, maintenance, and an account issue.\n\n1. Taking a tenant offline is deliberate, and bringing it back is equally deliberate; both are recorded with a reason.\n2. Where the indicator ever shows a state nobody set, the System Administrator checks who changed it and when in the administrative change record **before** changing it back. An unexpected state change is information; overwriting it without understanding destroys the information.\n\n## Connection health\n\nConnection health is kept as one record per dependency — the payment processor, email, messaging, the database, the website, and the customer portal — with its state, the last check, the last success, the most recent error, the check duration, and a count of consecutive failures. As health checks report in, this becomes the first place to look when something is wrong and nobody yet knows what.\n\nIt is read as a summary, not a log:\n\n1. One failed check with quick recovery is noise.\n2. A rising consecutive-failure count is signal, and the error text usually names the cause.\n3. Check durations creeping up over days are the early warning — still working, degrading, and the time to act before it becomes an outage.\n\nWhere a summary position is presented from these records, it reflects the **worst** component state, not the average. An honest warning is worth more than a reassuring summary that conceals a degraded email service; \"all operational\" over a failing dependency is a falsehood that costs trust the first time anyone notices.\n\n## Administrative access record\n\nAdministrative access events are recorded: the person, the tenant touched, the reason, the time, the network address, and any revocation. The System Administrator reviews the record monthly — not line by line, but for anomalies: unfamiliar network addresses, vague reasons, access at hours when the person does not work. The reason field is the whole point: every access carries one. An unread access record is decoration; a reviewed one is how a problem is caught before it becomes a story. Access itself is granted and reviewed under Admin Users (§4.4.2).\n\n## The failure patterns worth memorizing\n\nThese are not tied to one measure; they are how failures present:\n\n1. **Everything breaks at once** — the storefront will not load data, bookings will not save, the administrative screens spin. That is the database or the Platform core, not any single connection. A single connection failing produces local symptoms; the foundation failing produces global ones. Check the core first.\n2. **\"The site is down\" but the site loads** — usually the sign-in path. Sign-in failing while everything else is healthy has a distinctive reach: nobody can get in and everything else works. It is never corrected by editing user records. A sign-in outage is corrected in sign-in configuration, not in account data; touching user records during one converts a temporary outage into a data problem that outlives it.\n3. **Money failing** — the highest-urgency failure there is, because every minute is revenue lost. The first question is whether the fault is the Company's or the processor's: the processor's published state is checked before anything is touched. Platform-wide processor incidents happen, and correcting the Company's configuration during one adds a second problem. Where the processor is healthy, the usual suspects are expired keys, incomplete onboarding — charges enabled but payouts not — and event endpoints that have stopped responding. The Platform's processor health measure states that the connection is alive; the processor's console states what the money is doing. Both are needed. See Payments (§4.3.4) and Settings (§4.4.4).\n4. **Silence** — an order placed with no confirmation; an appointment booked with no reminder. Notifications fail after the triggering action succeeds, so nobody reports an email they were not expecting. The fault surfaces from downstream effects weeks later unless someone is watching. The operator checks the email and messaging connection health, then the recipient data — blank or malformed addresses fail in the data, not the system — then the consent state: messaging to a number that has opted out fails by design, and that is the compliance position protecting the Company, never to be overridden, under Troubleshooting (§4.4.8). For franchise locations, the sender identity is checked: mail from an unauthenticated domain is filtered as spam — delivered from the Platform's perspective, invisible from the customer's.\n5. **Slow, not down** — a homepage that took 800 milliseconds last month and 3 seconds today is reporting something even though it is technically up. Duration trends are watched, not only up and down states. Where the website degrades while everything else is healthy, the most recent change is the prime suspect, and it is checked before anything else.\n\n## Controls\n\n- **Who may change system state.** Backups and restores: the System Administrator. Tenant live state: an owner or the System Administrator, with a reason recorded. Connection and credential configuration: the System Administrator jointly with the Accounting Manager where money is involved, under Settings (§4.4.4).\n- Backup download links expire after 1 hour and are never extended, forwarded, or recorded in a document; a backup file is Company data of the highest sensitivity and is stored only in the backup location the system records.\n- A restore is performed only by the System Administrator, only to correct a recorded problem, and its result is verified against the books under §4.3 before the tenant returns to live state.\n- No system setting, health measure, or restore decision overrides a governing legal document or an accounting control in §4.3, including the rule that a closed period is corrected only by reversing entry.\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nBackup records and the backups themselves, tenant live-state changes with their reasons, connection health records, administrative access records, and quarterly restore-test results are kept in the Platform's system and administrative records and retained under the Regulatory Record Retention Policy (§2.9.28).\n\n## Escalation\n\nA failed backup that cannot be corrected the same day, an unexplained tenant state change, a rising failure count on a money connection, or any suspected unauthorized administrative access goes to an owner the same day; suspected compromise and legal process go to legal@unsaidmemories.com. Operational support for system faults goes to support@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nConnection health is glanced at daily and read in full weekly. The administrative access record is reviewed monthly. Backups, the recovery window, and a restore test are reviewed quarterly. This standard is reviewed after every incident it is used in.\n",
  "quick-links": "\n# Quick Links\n\nThis standard governs Quick Links: the tenant-scoped list of shortcuts to the places administrators open constantly, kept as records in the Platform and shown in the command palette and in the Quick Links card on the Settings Overview screen. Quick Links records exist to be created and managed; where a Quick Links card is empty, it is not broken — nobody has added any yet. The Organization Administrator maintains the list for the tenant; any administrator with the administration grant at Read level may follow the links, and the Write level is required to change them, under Admin Users (§4.4.2). A quick link is a convenience only: it grants no access, changes no permission, and never overrides a governing legal document or an access decision.\n\n## The link record\n\nEach link is a label, an address, an optional icon, an order position, and a marker for whether it opens in a new tab. The list is kept per tenant, so a franchise tenant's launchpad holds its own destinations and not the Company's.\n\n## Managing links\n\n1. **Add** a link with its label, address, icon where wanted, and new-tab choice.\n2. **Reorder** by assigning a new order position; the palette and the card follow the order.\n3. **Retire** a link rather than deleting it: retiring marks the link inactive, the views show active links only, and a retired link can be reactivated without reconstructing it. Retiring preserves history and allows a link to return; row deletion is for data the Company never wants to see again, and retiring is for links not needed this quarter.\n4. Where the management screen will not save, the operator checks their own administration grant level before suspecting the data: reads require the administration grant at Read level and changes require Write, under Admin Users (§4.4.2).\n\n## What belongs on the list\n\nThe value of the launchpad is entirely in being correct and current. A link pointing somewhere stale is worse than no link: it trains people not to trust the launchpad, and then everyone returns to individual bookmarks and the purpose is lost. Good candidates are:\n\n1. **The storefront** — opened in a private window when checking what customers see, because an administrative session, stored page copies, and a signed-in state all make the site look different to staff than to a first-time visitor.\n2. **The customer portal sign-in.**\n3. **The payment processor's console** — payouts and disputes live there, not in the Platform; the processor is the record of authority for money, under Payments (§4.3.4).\n4. **The booking calendar.**\n5. **The settings hub itself,** governed by Settings (§4.4.4).\n\nOne habit is built on this list: after any change to products, categories, pricing, or hours, the operator opens the storefront link and checks the change as a customer would. The storefront keeps stored copies aggressively; where a change does not appear, a forced refresh comes before any conclusion that the site is broken. A large share of \"the site is broken\" alarms resolve at that step.\n\n## Controls\n\n- **Who may change the list.** The Organization Administrator, and any administrator holding the administration grant at Write level for the tenant concerned. Franchise tenants maintain their own lists and do not alter the Company's.\n- A link's address is verified when it is added and at each quarterly review. A link is never pointed at a page that collects credentials, a banking sign-in, or any destination whose address an operator has not verified; a launchpad that staff trust is a high-value target for a substituted address.\n- Banking and processor credentials are never placed in a link, a label, or any other record; a link points to a sign-in page, never through it.\n- A quick link confers nothing: following one into a screen the operator has no grant for results in the ordinary access refusal, under Admin Users (§4.4.2).\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nQuick link records, including retired links and their order positions, are kept in the Platform's administrative records for the tenant and retained under the Regulatory Record Retention Policy (§2.9.28).\n\n## Escalation\n\nA link found pointing to an unverified or substituted destination is retired immediately and reported to an owner the same day, and to legal@unsaidmemories.com where substitution is suspected. Management-screen failures go to support@unsaidmemories.com after the grant check above.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe list is reviewed quarterly. Businesses accumulate links the way drawers accumulate cables; every link earns its place by being opened weekly. Anything else is clutter that slows down finding the ones that matter, and it is retired at the review.\n",
  "franchise-signup": "\n# Franchise Signup\n\nThis standard governs franchise signup: the single administrative action that creates a franchise on the Platform. A franchise is a tenant whose parent reference points at the Company's tenant. That one reference is the whole structural relationship: the child inherits the Company's brand, catalog, and policies, and keeps its own customers, appointments, money, and staff. Creating a franchise creates that reference plus the records that make it operational, and the Platform does it in one submission, because a half-created franchise is worse than none. The Franchise Administrator performs signup; only an owner may approve the commercial terms entered. Signup configuration never overrides a governing legal document: the offer and sale of a franchise are governed by the Franchise Disclosure Document and the executed franchise agreement, state franchise laws prevail where they give the franchisee greater rights, and no figure, screen, or setting in the signup flow is an earnings representation or a substitute for those documents.\n\n## The signup form\n\nThe signup screen is one form. Its fields are:\n\n1. **Business name** (required) — the name the franchisee operates under. It becomes the child tenant's name.\n2. **Slug** (optional — derived from the business name where left blank) — the address slug. The system checks it against both the tenant records and the franchise location records: where the slug is taken anywhere, the submission fails with a message stating that the slug is already taken. It is gotten right the first time; slugs are effectively permanent, under Organization (§4.4.1).\n3. **Franchisee name and email** (required), and telephone — the accountable person, named expressly, not \"the team.\"\n4. **Address** — street lines, city, state, postal code — plus the location's telephone and email.\n5. **Existing website address,** where the franchisee has one.\n6. **Royalty rate** — the commercial term, entered as a number. The rate applied to a given franchise is the rate stated in its executed franchise agreement; this standard states no standard rate, and the Franchise Administrator enters only the rate the owner has approved for that franchise.\n7. **Send invitation** (on by default) — whether the franchisee receives the invitation email immediately.\n\nOne submission creates the franchise. The submission requires the business name, the franchisee's name, and the franchisee's email; everything else is optional. Where the franchise location records are not present in the system's schema, the submission fails at once with a specific message — a loud, particular error rather than a half-written franchise — and nothing is created.\n\n## What the submission creates — all seven records\n\nIn order, inside one operation:\n\n1. **Child tenant** — a tenant record with its parent reference set to the Company's tenant, its type set to franchise, and its state active.\n2. **Franchise business record** — the franchise location record: tenant linkage, business name, slug, full address, telephone, email, and existing website address.\n3. **Franchise agreement** — an agreement record, created in pending state. Pending until signed: the commercial relationship is not in force until the document is executed, and the state says so plainly.\n4. **Primary physical location** — a location record for the shop, marked primary, in the current location system. This is what puts the new shop in the tenant switcher and the location pickers, under Organization (§4.4.1).\n5. **Franchisee portal account** — an account record with the franchise-owner role and owner standing for the child tenant. The franchisee receives full administration limited to their own tenant, under Admin Users (§4.4.2): they run their shop; they do not touch the Company's.\n6. **Permission audit entry** — a record of the grant, so every permission the franchisee holds is attributable from the first day.\n7. **Invitation email** — sent to the franchisee where the invitation choice is on and an email address is present. The response states whether the email was sent. The Franchise Administrator checks it: a franchisee who never received the invitation cannot begin setup, and \"the email never arrived\" three days later is a preventable delay.\n\nThe response returns every identifier needed afterward: the tenant identifier, the franchise location identifier, the agreement identifier, the location identifier, the portal account identifier, and the slug. The Franchise Administrator records them; they are the handles for everything that follows.\n\n## After the submission: the setup sequence\n\nThe submission creates the records; the Franchise Administrator and the franchisee make them operational, in this order:\n\n1. **Agreement execution.** The agreement is pending. It is signed, and the signed document is kept on the agreement record. Until it is executed, the royalty rate is a number in a form, not an obligation.\n2. **Email identity.** Franchise email comes from the franchise's own identity, not the parent's — customers reply to the location they visited. The from-name, from-address, and sending domain are set for the tenant, with the domain authenticated before the first send. Unauthenticated first emails land in spam filters and the launch begins with silence. See Settings (§4.4.4).\n3. **Location setup.** Hours and blackouts are entered for the new shop, following the sequence in Organization (§4.4.1), as the franchisee's data under their tenant.\n4. **Booking rules.** The franchise's booking settings are set deliberately under Booking Operations (§4.4.3); they inherit nothing automatically.\n5. **Staff accounts.** Created through the Users screen with franchise-scoped roles, under Admin Users (§4.4.2).\n6. **Processor connection.** The franchise's own connection is completed; money is per tenant, under Payments (§4.3.4).\n\nThen, before launch day: a test booking, a test transaction, confirmation that email arrives from the right address, and a check that the location displays correctly. Then a soft opening — controlled volume — before the public launch. The submission gets the franchise technically ready; the soft opening gets it actually ready.\n\n## Failure modes\n\n1. **Slug taken.** The submission fails cleanly before creating anything. A different slug is chosen — usually with a geographic suffix.\n2. **Franchise records not present in the schema.** The submission reports that the franchise permissions schema has not been applied. It is applied, and the submission retried. Nothing was created, so there is nothing to clean up.\n3. **Invitation not sent.** The response states the email was not sent. The Franchise Administrator checks the tenant's email configuration before resending; resending into a broken mail setup produces a second silent failure.\n4. **Wrong royalty rate.** While the agreement is still pending, the Franchise Administrator corrects the agreement record before execution. After signing, the rate is contractual: changing it is a renegotiation, not a settings edit. That is why the rate field deserves care at signup — every special arrangement becomes the precedent the next applicant cites.\n\n## Controls\n\n- **Who may sign up a franchise.** The Franchise Administrator creates the tenant, location, accounts, and invitation. The commercial terms — including the royalty rate — are entered only as approved by an owner, and take effect only on execution of the franchise agreement.\n- Signup is a configuration step, not the offer or sale of a franchise. The Franchise Disclosure Document controls the offer and sale; no earnings representation is made by this standard, the signup screens, or any portal content; and where a state franchise law gives the franchisee greater rights, that law prevails over anything in the signup configuration.\n- A setting created by signup — brand inheritance, booking rules, email identity — never overrides the franchise agreement or any governing legal document. Where they differ, the legal document governs and the setting is corrected.\n- The franchisee's banking and processor credentials are collected only through the processor's own onboarding, are never entered by Company staff, and are never recorded in any document, under Banking (§4.3.10).\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nThe child tenant record, the franchise business record, the franchise agreement record and its executed document, the primary location record, the franchisee's portal account, the permission audit entry, and the invitation record are kept in the Platform's administrative and franchise records and retained under the Regulatory Record Retention Policy (§2.9.28).\n\n## Escalation\n\nA question about whether a franchise may be offered or sold, the terms of an agreement, or a franchisee's rights goes to legal@unsaidmemories.com before signup proceeds. Operational failures of the signup submission go to support@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Franchise Administrator reviews pending agreements and unaccepted invitations weekly until each new franchise launches. This standard is reviewed whenever the signup flow, the franchise documents, or the disclosure position changes.\n",
  "organization-settings-troubleshooting": "\n# Organization Settings Troubleshooting\n\nThis standard is the diagnostic runbook for organization and settings problems on the Platform: the failures that present as \"it should work and it does not,\" worked by elimination. Administrative configuration failures rarely produce an error message; the failure mode is always something that should work and does not. The method is therefore fixed: verify each part in order, most common cause first, check the actual stored data at each step, and skip nothing because the operator is sure. The Organization Administrator and the System Administrator work this runbook; front-desk staff report symptoms and do not change settings to test a theory. Most problems below resolve in minutes when the order is followed.\n\n## A user cannot access the portal\n\nAccess is worked from the access record, because that is where access lives — and from the right record for the right population. Staff and administrator access is held in membership and roster records; customer access is held in customer account records, under Admin Users (§4.4.2).\n\n1. The most common misdiagnosis in this standard is treating an access problem as a password problem. Where the membership is inactive or the invitation was never accepted, no password reset helps, and each failed attempt frustrates the person further. The record is verified first, always.\n2. **For a staff member:** does the membership record exist, is it active, and is its state what is expected? A missing record means the person was never added; the correction is an invitation, not a password reset. An inactive record means someone suspended them; the operator establishes who suspended them and why before reactivating, because the suspension may have been deliberate. A record still in invited state with no acceptance time means setup was never completed; the operator checks the invitation expiry — past expiry means the link is dead and the person needs a fresh invitation, not a resend of the dead one.\n3. **For a customer:** is there a customer account record linking their customer record to a sign-in identity, and is its state active? An invited-but-never-activated account behaves exactly like a broken sign-in from the customer's perspective. The activation is resent.\n4. Where the records are sound and access still fails, check the gate: administrative writes pass through the administration module check, and the Users screen itself requires the administration module at the right level for whoever is operating it. An administrator who cannot manage users usually lacks the grant themselves — the permission model applying to its own administration.\n5. On resolving the problem, the operator tells the person what was actually wrong. \"Your invitation had expired; a fresh one has been sent\" teaches them the system is explicable. Unexplained corrections teach them it is mysteriously unreliable, and mysteriously unreliable is worse than broken.\n\n## A location will not switch\n\nThree causes, told apart by where the operator looks:\n\n1. **The location does not exist where the switcher looks.** The administrative tenant switcher reads the current location system; the older location table is a different system, under Organization (§4.4.1). Where the premises exist in one and the picker reads the other, the location will not appear however often it is clicked. The operator verifies the record exists in the system the switcher queries, with its active marker set — inactive locations are filtered by design, and that is deactivation working, not a fault.\n2. **The user has no scope there.** A groomer assigned to the main salon legitimately has no access to another location's data; that is the permission model working correctly. \"Correcting\" it with broad access creates the over-permissioning problem Admin Users (§4.4.2) warns against. Multi-location access is granted expressly, with the reason recorded.\n3. **Genuinely duplicate locations.** The live data holds a real example: near-identical main-location records across the two location systems, differing by dash characters. Where pickers show indistinguishable options, users choose at random and land in the wrong location's data, which presents as \"switching does not work\" or \"the data is wrong.\" The correction is record hygiene: merge or deactivate the duplicates under Organization (§4.4.1). Where doubling appears in a location picker, duplicates are checked before anything else is investigated.\n\n## Booking settings are not applying\n\nThe natural order is: is the value saved, is it saved in the right place, is the stored copy stale, is something more specific overriding it?\n\n1. Read the actual setting — not the administrative screen, the stored value. The screen shows what was entered; only the store shows what the system kept. Booking rules are the booking settings under Booking Operations (§4.4.3); runtime values are read through a short-lived stored copy refreshed on a 5-minute cycle. Where a setting was just changed and is not yet reflected, the operator waits out the refresh cycle before concluding it is broken; a significant share of apparent \"settings do not work\" reports resolve within one cycle.\n2. Where the value is right and the behaviour still does not match, check hours and blackouts: hours and blackout records are enforced by the booking engine as binding, and a booking rule cannot offer a slot the hours forbid.\n3. Check the online-booking switch itself — the master setting the public booking path reads. Debugging rules while the master switch is off is a particular kind of frustration; it is checked first among the rule-level causes.\n\n## The deposit requirement is not working\n\nDeposits touch booking rules, payment processing, and the invoice, so the operator first narrows the symptom: is no deposit requested at booking, is it requested but the charge fails, or is it taken but not applied?\n\n1. **No deposit requested:** verify the deposit requirement is actually on **and** an amount is configured — a percentage (the default is 25 percent) or a flat amount. The classic misconfiguration is enabling the requirement with no amount: the system is told to require a deposit of nothing, and nothing is what it requires. Both the switch and the amount are set.\n2. **The charge fails:** that is the payment path, not the booking rules. The operator checks the processor linkage — the connection settings and the tenant's connection state, under Settings (§4.4.4) — and confirms the mode is live, not test. The Platform default is test mode with a placeholder account reference; a deposit charge attempted against that goes nowhere. The processor's console is also checked for incomplete onboarding: charges enabled but payouts not.\n3. **Taken but not applied to the invoice:** that is a payment-to-invoice application problem, governed in the books by Deposits (§4.3.6). The operator does not adjust the invoice by hand — manual adjustments on top of systematic misapplication compound the confusion. The exact sequence is documented — booking identifier, payment identifier, invoice identifier, amounts — and escalated with that evidence. Precise reports get corrected; vague ones get deferred.\n\n## Notifications are not sending\n\nNotifications fail after the triggering action succeeds: the appointment books correctly, then the confirmation never arrives. The customer experiences unreliability, not a notification problem, which is why it surfaces as \"customers say they never received the confirmation\" rather than as an error. The parts are checked in order:\n\n1. **Sender identity.** The tenant's email sending credentials are held on the Email settings page, shown masked: the operator can verify that a credential is configured but can never read it back. Where mail broke immediately after someone \"checked\" the key by re-saving the form blank, the credential is sound and the cause lies elsewhere. Templates are fixed to the brand; the Email page manages only the sender credential. For franchise locations, the sending domain shall be authenticated: unauthenticated mail is filtered as spam — delivered from the Platform's perspective, invisible from the customer's.\n2. **Recipient data.** Blank or malformed addresses fail in the data, not the system.\n3. **Consent.** Messages to opted-out numbers fail by design; that is the compliance position protecting the Company, and overriding it is not an available correction. A channel the customer consented to is used instead.\n\nWhere one customer reports a missing notification while others receive theirs, the cause is their address, their spam folder, or their carrier — not the Platform. The operator verifies the send was attempted and accepted by the provider. Where it was, the trail ends at their inbox provider, and the customer is told the message was sent and accepted, to check spam, and how to allowlist the sender. Where it was not attempted, the operator works backward through the parts above until the sequence breaks.\n\n## The settings page shows defaults, or saves do not hold\n\nTwo distinct causes:\n\n1. **Every settings screen shows clean defaults.** The settings service has fallen back to the built-in defaults because it cannot reach the database. That is a connectivity signal, not a data loss. The operator checks the database connection before touching any setting; see System (§4.4.5).\n2. **Saves appear to succeed but values revert.** The operator checks the administrative change record for whether the write actually committed: every committed change through the settings path is recorded there, so a missing record means the write never landed. The stored-copy refresh is also remembered: runtime reads carry a 5-minute cycle, and a save followed by an immediate check that reads the old value is usually the refresh, not a failed write. The operator waits, re-reads, and then escalates with the change record as evidence.\n\n## Controls\n\n- Diagnosis never proceeds by changing settings to see what happens. A value is changed only where the stored data shows it wrong, one change at a time, under the change authority in Settings (§4.4.4) and Booking Operations (§4.4.3).\n- No access record, location record, or settings value is deleted to resolve a symptom; deactivation and correction leave the record that explains the outcome, under Admin Users (§4.4.2) and Organization (§4.4.1).\n- A setting changed during diagnosis never overrides a governing legal document, and consent failures are never overridden for any reason.\n- Credentials encountered during diagnosis — sender keys, processor keys, banking access — are verified as configured only. They are never read back, copied, shared, or recorded in an incident note or any other document.\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nIncident notes stating the symptom, the stored values found, the correction made, and the evidence relied on — together with the administrative change records the corrections produce — are kept with the tenant's administrative records and retained under the Regulatory Record Retention Policy (§2.9.28).\n\n## Escalation\n\nA problem that survives this runbook is escalated to support@unsaidmemories.com with the stored values and change records as evidence, not with a summary of impressions. A substituted or unverified link, a suspected unauthorized settings change, or any legal process goes to legal@unsaidmemories.com the same day.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe System Administrator reviews this runbook after every incident it is used for and quarterly in full, adding each new verified cause in the order it should be checked.\n",
  "services-and-pricing": "\n# Services and Pricing\n\nThis standard governs how grooming services, packages, add-ons, and surcharges are maintained in the content management system. It is used by the Store Manager, who owns the booking catalog and pricing; by Content Editors, who maintain the public services pages; and by Front Desk staff, who quote prices and apply surcharges at booking and checkout. Its central rule is that services exist in two separate stores that do not update each other: the booking catalog, which the appointment book reads, and the public site content, which the website displays. An operator shall confirm which store a task concerns before making any change.\n\n## Roles and duties\n\n- **Store Manager** — owns the booking catalog, packages, add-ons, and surcharge rules; approves every price, duration, and deposit change.\n- **Content Editor** — maintains the public services content so that displayed names and prices match the booking catalog.\n- **Front Desk** — applies manual surcharges, advises customers of price changes at booking and checkout, and reports mismatches between the site and the book.\n\n## The booking catalog\n\nThe booking catalog is the working list of bookable services. Each service carries a permanent code, a display name, a price, a default duration in minutes, a deposit requirement and deposit amount ($25 across the current catalog), an online-booking switch, a rebooking interval in days, and an active switch. The duration drives the appointment book: the calendar blocks exactly that many minutes, so an incorrect duration creates overruns or unfillable gaps. The rebooking interval drives customer reminders.\n\nOperators shall observe the following rules:\n\n1. The service code is permanent once created. Reports and integrations key from the code, not the display name. The display name may be renamed; the code shall not change.\n2. A service that has ever been booked shall never be deleted. Deactivate it. Past appointments and revenue reports continue to reference the record.\n3. Price and duration changes apply to future quotes immediately and never rewrite history. A past appointment keeps the price quoted at booking time.\n4. After creating a service, confirm it is offered at each location where it may be booked and set a location price override wherever the price legitimately differs. A service with no location record is invisible at booking time even though it exists in the catalog, and a service is selectable by customers only when online booking is switched on for it.\n5. When a price increase takes effect, the Store Manager shall notify Front Desk for one week, because customers reminded at the old price will see the new price at checkout.\n\nCurrent examples from the catalog illustrate the scale: Bath & Brush at $95, Full Groom at $115, and Deluxe Spa at $145.\n\n## Packages and add-ons\n\nA package is created as a catalog record in the package category. Every package shall state its name, code, price, number of included visits, and validity period in days. A package with no expiry is a liability on the books and shall not be published. The public package cards are maintained separately as public site content; the Content Editor shall keep the card and the bookable package consistent, because the site shall not advertise what the book cannot sell.\n\nAdd-ons exist in the same two forms. The bookable add-on is a catalog record flagged as an add-on and attached to a parent service, with its own duration and deposit rules, offered inside the booking flow. The public add-on price list is public site content (Nail Trim $15, Teeth Brushing $15, Flea Bath $10, De-shedding $15–$35, Paw Treatment $15). Where an add-on appears in both stores, the Store Manager shall set one price and keep both records in agreement; Front Desk staff shall not be placed in the position of deciding between two prices at checkout.\n\n## The public services content\n\nThe homepage service grid and the services page are maintained in the public content store: service categories (GROOMING, BATH & SPA, NAIL & PAW CARE, ADD-ON SERVICES), the items within each category including size pricing and member pricing, and the storefront service tiers. Each record carries a title, description, icon name, image, display order, and a visible switch. The visible switch is the public on/off control, and display order shall be set deliberately. A tier record with an empty starting price renders a blank price on the page; the Content Editor shall fill the price or hide the record.\n\n## Surcharges and pricing rules\n\nConditional fees are maintained as surcharge records, each with an amount or percentage, an automatic-application switch, an approval requirement, taxable and commissionable flags, an active switch, and an optional restriction to a single service. The Store Manager shall decide the application story before the amount:\n\n1. Automatic application is reserved for fees that require no judgement, such as holiday fees. Judgement-based fees shall require manager approval and shall not apply automatically.\n2. Duplicate active surcharges for the same event are prohibited. Two active weekend surcharges ($20 and $15) are a data error: the Store Manager shall retain the correct amount and deactivate the other record. Deactivation, not deletion, is the remedy, because past bookings reference the record.\n3. Matting and senior or special-handling fees shall be flat amounts, shall require approval, and shall carry their criteria in plain language in the record description — for matting, dematting requiring more than 15 minutes or a shave-down; for senior handling, dogs over 12 years or any pet flagged as a bite risk in its profile. A groomer shall assess the animal before either fee is charged.\n\n## Lookup lists\n\nThe booking flow's reference lists — breeds, haircut styles, coat types, textures, lengths and conditions, shedding levels, clip lengths, style options, and service options — are maintained through the same content editor. Deactivation is the only safe edit: deactivating an entry removes it from future bookings without breaking existing records that reference it. Entries shall not be deleted or renamed for tidiness.\n\n## Controls\n\nEvery change made through the services and pricing screens is recorded in the audit log with the operator, the time, and the resulting state. The Store Manager shall review the audit log whenever a price, duration, or surcharge changes unexpectedly, before questioning staff recollection. Only the Store Manager role may approve price and surcharge changes; Content Editors may prepare public content changes but shall not alter catalog prices.\n\n## Records\n\nThis standard produces catalog records, package and add-on records, public content records, surcharge records, and the audit log entries for each change. Catalog and surcharge history is retained permanently because appointments and revenue reports reference it.\n\n## Escalation\n\nPricing disputes a customer raises at checkout are handled by Front Desk and the Store Manager in the store. Operational questions about this standard go to support@unsaidmemories.com. A disagreement about what a published price promised is escalated to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Store Manager reviews the full catalog, the public services content, and the surcharge list against each other once a month, and confirms that no duplicate surcharge and no blank public price remains.\n",
  "website": "\n# Website\n\nThis standard governs the public website content maintained through the content management system: pages, banners, the photo gallery, navigation, reusable content blocks, search metadata, promotions, and shop products. It is used by Content Editors, who prepare and publish website content; by the Store Manager, who approves promotions and product changes; and by the Web Administrator, who owns publishing rights and the audit log. The website is assembled from database records, and each part of the site reads from one specific store. Editing the wrong store is the most common cause of \"the change did not appear,\" and this standard states which store each part reads.\n\n## Roles and duties\n\n- **Content Editor** — prepares pages, gallery photos, content blocks, and search metadata; previews every change before publishing.\n- **Store Manager** — approves promotions and shop product changes and confirms each promotion in a test checkout before it is announced.\n- **Web Administrator** — grants and removes publishing rights, reviews the audit log, and resolves publishing failures.\n\n## How publishing works\n\nAll website changes are made through the CMS Management screens in the admin console. Every action writes its record and an audit log entry with the operator, the time, and the resulting state. The public site displays stored default content on first load and then refreshes as changes are published; a published change may take a few minutes to appear everywhere. That delay is the normal refresh cycle, not a failure. If a change has not appeared after a few minutes, the operator shall follow CMS Troubleshooting (§4.5.6).\n\nOnly seven public content resources are readable by anonymous visitors — services, service items, testimonials, frequently asked questions, policies, gallery, and add-ons. All other content requires an administrator session to read, and all changes require an administrator session. This restriction is a security control and shall not be worked around.\n\n## Pages\n\nGeneral pages are held as page records carrying a web address name, title, page type, body content, laid-out content blocks, excerpt, featured image, publication state, and an optional scheduled publication time. Every save creates a revision record with a version number and the operator's change note. Change notes shall describe the change in terms a reader six months later can act on — \"Updated holiday hours for Thanksgiving week,\" not \"updates.\" To remove a page from the site without deleting it, withdraw it from publication; the content and its revision history survive.\n\n> [!NOTICE]\n> The public policy pages do not render from page records. They render from the policy store described in Legal and Waivers (§4.5.3). Editing a policy as a page record changes nothing the public can see.\n\n## Banners\n\nBanners are records with a type (hero, promo bar, popup, inline, footer, or alert), a placement (home, all pages, services, booking, portal, or custom), an active switch, a sort order, and a start and end time. A banner displays only when it is active, the current time falls inside its window, and its placement matches the page. Every banner shall carry an end date; a banner with no end date remains until someone remembers it, and an out-of-season promotion left on the site damages customer trust.\n\n## Gallery\n\nThe public gallery reads one photo store only: each photo carries an image, alternative text, a category (GROOMING, BATH & SPA, TRANSFORMATIONS), a display order, and a visible switch. Photos placed in any other gallery store do not appear on the site, and operators shall not use those stores. Captions shall state what the photo shows and which service it represents. The Content Editor shall add recent work regularly; a gallery whose newest photo is a year old tells visitors the business is idle.\n\n## Navigation\n\nNavigation is maintained as menu records for the primary, footer, mobile, utility, and portal menus. Each entry requires a label and either a web address or a linked page, a parent entry where it sits in a dropdown, and an explicit sort order. Labels shall be words a customer understands (\"Book Grooming,\" not internal module names). Dropdowns are one level deep. An entry shall never link to a page that is not live.\n\n## Reusable content blocks\n\nReusable blocks hold text that appears in many places — the phone number, the address block, the holiday-hours notice, the tagline — each under a content key, with a text or structured value, grouped for findability. The locale on every block shall be en-US; a block created under any other locale value is never read by the site. Branding values — the primary colour and the logo — are maintained as settings on the same screens. The point of the block store is single-source editing: before typing repeated text into a page, the Content Editor shall check whether it belongs in a block, because the same phone number typed into five pages is a future inconsistency.\n\n## Search metadata\n\nPer-page search metadata is maintained for each page and as a site default: meta title, meta description, the preferred page address, and social-sharing title, description, and image. The meta description is the snippet shown in search results and is the highest-value field. Titles shall be kept under 60 characters and descriptions under 160 characters, or search engines truncate them. The content system controls per-page metadata only; crawl policy is maintained in the site's own sitemap and robots configuration, outside the console.\n\n## Promotions and shop products\n\nPromotions are built in the Promo Builder: name, a unique uppercase code, type (percentage off, amount off, or free add-on), value, what it applies to, eligibility (new, existing, or all customers), start and end dates, maximum total uses, maximum uses per customer, whether it may be combined with other offers, and the placements where it is published. A discount with no end date and no usage cap is a liability, not a promotion, and shall not be published. After creating a promotion, the Store Manager shall test it in a real checkout before it is announced. Promotional offers to customers are governed by the Promotional Offers Terms (§2.1.21).\n\nShop products maintained through the console carry a name, category, display price, payment price identifier, badge, image, alternative text, description, order, and visible switch. Entering the payment price identifier is what enables checkout for that product. Deleting a product through the console also retires its payment counterpart; the console record governs, and deletion completes in the console even if the payment update fails.\n\nServices and pricing content is governed by Services and Pricing (§4.5.1); payment method settings are governed by Payments and Gateway (§4.5.5).\n\n## Controls\n\nPublishing rights are limited to the Content Editor, Store Manager, and Web Administrator roles. Every console action is audit-logged. The Web Administrator shall review the audit log whenever content changes without explanation, and shall treat the log — not recollection — as the record of who changed what.\n\n## Records\n\nThis standard produces page records and their revision history, banner, gallery, navigation, block, and metadata records, promotion and product records, and the audit log entries for each action. Revision history is retained permanently; it is the evidence of what the site said on any given day.\n\n## Escalation\n\nPublishing failures that CMS Troubleshooting (§4.5.6) does not resolve go to support@unsaidmemories.com. Content that raises a legal question — a claim the Company may not be able to substantiate, or wording that conflicts with a governing policy — goes to legal@unsaidmemories.com before it is published.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Web Administrator reviews banners, promotions, and the gallery monthly for expired or stale content, and reviews publishing rights quarterly.\n",
  "legal-and-waivers": "\n# Legal and Waivers\n\nThis standard governs how legal pages and customer waivers are maintained in the content management system and presented for acceptance. It is used by the Legal function, which owns the text of every legal page and waiver; by Content Editors, who place approved text into the console; and by Front Desk staff, who present waivers at booking and check-in. One rule governs the whole subject: published legal and waiver content comes from the governing documents in the Company's document library. The console displays that text; it does not author different text, and no operator shall publish legal wording in the console that differs from the governing document.\n\n## Roles and duties\n\n- **Legal function** — approves all legal and waiver text, sets effective dates, and decides whether a change is material.\n- **Content Editor** — places approved text into the policy store exactly as approved, writes change notes, and never edits legal wording on the Editor's own judgement.\n- **Front Desk** — presents the current waiver version before the appointment and confirms acceptance is captured before service begins.\n\n## Where legal content lives\n\nLegal pages and waivers are held in the policy store. The public site renders them at the policies addresses, where each page's address is derived from its title. The Legal and Waivers screens and the policy manager both write to this same store, and saving under an existing title updates that record rather than creating a duplicate. Operators shall edit legal content only in the policy store. Page records elsewhere in the console have no public rendering for policies, and waiver text found in general settings keys is a defect: the Content Editor shall move it into the policy store and report it.\n\nThe policy store has no delete function and no required-acceptance flag. Operators shall keep the store pruned by editing a superseded policy into its current form rather than accumulating near-duplicates. Whether acceptance is required is a property of the flow that presents the policy — booking or check-in — not of the policy record.\n\n## Placing and changing legal text\n\n1. The Legal function issues the approved text and its effective date. The source of the text is the governing library document; where the console text and the library document differ, the library document governs and the console shall be corrected to match it.\n2. The Content Editor places the text in the policy store under the existing title, so the address the public uses does not change unless the Legal function directs a new page.\n3. A correction that does not change meaning — a typographical error — may be made in place. Any change to a fee, a time window, a risk allocation, or any other obligation is a material change: it is issued as a new version with a new effective date, a change note that states what changed and when it takes effect (for example, \"Raised no-show fee from $25 to $35, effective November 1\"), and a plain-language summary of the change at the top of the page for customers.\n4. Acceptances already captured remain attached to the version the customer saw. Operators shall never rewrite an earlier acceptance to point at a newer version; the earlier record is the evidence of what that customer agreed to.\n\n## Writing and presenting the waiver\n\nWaiver text issued by the Legal function follows these rules: short sentences, plain words, and the order a customer would ask the questions — what the customer is agreeing to, what the risks are, and what happens if the customer does not sign. The presentation flow matters as much as the text: the waiver shall be presented, and acceptance captured, before the appointment. A waiver signed after the service does not evidence informed agreement and shall not be relied on.\n\n## Acceptance records\n\nAn acceptance record states the customer, the policy, the version accepted, the time, and the network address from which acceptance was given. The acceptance ledger is the record Front Desk and the Legal function consult in any dispute about what a customer agreed to. Operators shall observe the following:\n\n1. Before relying on a waiver in a dispute, verify that an acceptance record for that customer and version exists in the ledger.\n2. Never alter or backfill an acceptance record. A missing record means acceptance was not captured through the system, and staff shall say so rather than represent otherwise.\n3. Where a signed waiver is needed and no system record exists, the waiver shall be obtained on paper and filed, until the acceptance is recorded in the ledger.\n\n## Controls\n\nOnly the Legal function may approve legal text, and only Content Editors with publishing rights may place it. Both waiver screens record each save in the audit log with the operator and the resulting state. The Legal function shall compare the published console text against the governing library documents whenever a governing document is amended, and at the review below.\n\n## Records\n\nThis standard produces policy records in the policy store, revision and audit history for each change, and acceptance records in the acceptance ledger. Acceptance records and legal revision history are retained permanently.\n\n## Escalation\n\nAny request to change legal or waiver wording — from any team — goes to the Legal function at legal@unsaidmemories.com; Content Editors shall not act on such a request from any other source. Service of legal process and subpoenas goes to legal@unsaidmemories.com on the day it is received. Operational problems with the waiver screens go to support@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Legal function reviews every published legal page and waiver against its governing library document quarterly, and confirms the acceptance ledger is capturing current versions.\n",
  "ai-web-builder": "\n# AI Web Builder\n\nThis standard governs the AI web builder, the Company's own AI-assisted tool for preparing website content inside the content management system. It is used by Content Editors, who request and revise generated content; by the Store Manager and Web Administrator, who review and publish it; and by the AI Operations function, which operates the builder service under AI Operations Overview (§4.6.1). The governing principle is stated once and applies everywhere in this standard: the builder prepares content for human review, and a person publishes. No generated content reaches the public site without a named human reviewing it and performing the publish step.\n\n## Roles and duties\n\n- **Content Editor** — requests generated content, revises it, verifies every factual claim against its source record, and submits it for publishing.\n- **Store Manager / Web Administrator** — reviews submitted content and performs the publish step; the publisher is the person answerable for what the site says.\n- **AI Operations function** — operates the builder service, maintains its instructions and evaluation under Prompt Library and Versioning (§4.6.3) and Evaluations (§4.6.4), and investigates generated-content incidents under AI Incident Handling (§4.6.5).\n- **Legal function** — approves any generated text that touches legal pages or waivers, under Legal and Waivers (§4.5.3).\n\n## What the builder works from\n\nThe builder prepares content from the Company's real records, never from an open prompt alone. Its inputs and outputs follow the store rules of this console:\n\n1. Service descriptions draw name, price, duration, and deposit terms from the booking catalog governed by Services and Pricing (§4.5.1). The builder shall not state a price, duration, or term that is not in that record.\n2. Public service content is prepared for the public content store; reusable text is prepared for the reusable block store under the en-US locale rule in Website (§4.5.2).\n3. Banners are prepared only in the valid banner types and placements stated in Website (§4.5.2), with an end date always present.\n4. Search copy is prepared for the search metadata store, within the title and description lengths stated in Website (§4.5.2).\n5. Gallery material is prepared for the public photo store only.\n6. Legal and waiver material is never generated as new text. The builder may prepare a placement of existing approved text for the policy store; the text itself comes from the governing library document, under Legal and Waivers (§4.5.3).\n\n## The review-and-publish workflow\n\n1. The Content Editor requests content for a stated purpose and page.\n2. The builder produces the content as an item that is not live, in the normal editing flow. A generated banner is created inactive; a generated page is created in a non-live state. The publish controls remain in human hands in every case.\n3. The builder shows its sources for every factual claim — the record each price, duration, term, and hour came from — so the reviewer can verify before publishing.\n4. The Content Editor reads the whole item, verifies each factual claim against the shown source, and revises as needed. Generated text shall be read with the same care as text from an unfamiliar contributor: fluent wording is not evidence of accuracy.\n5. The reviewer submits the item; the Store Manager or Web Administrator previews it under the preview standard below and performs the publish step.\n6. The revision history records the item as generated, together with what the human changed before publishing. When generated copy causes a customer complaint, that history — not a recollection of what the tool produced — is the record of what was proposed and what a person approved.\n\n## Templates\n\nTemplates are reusable starting structures for the pages this business actually publishes — a service page, a promotion banner, a seasonal announcement. A service-page template draws the service name, price, and duration from the booking catalog automatically so the structure cannot drift from the catalog. A banner template offers only the valid types and placements. Templates define structure — headline, image, body, call to action — and leave wording, photographs, and specifics to the human. A template is a starting point under the same workflow: nothing prepared from a template is published without the review and publish steps above.\n\n## Preview standard\n\nBefore publishing, the reviewer shall preview the item as the customer will see it: the rendered page on desktop and mobile, with real data, real images, and the surrounding navigation. A preview showing placeholder text does not satisfy this standard. The preview shall also show the search rendering — the meta title and description as they will appear in results — and the social-sharing card. Particular attention goes to the failures generated content is prone to: a headline overflowing on mobile, an image cropped badly, a price rendering incorrectly. Preview first, publish second, for every item.\n\n## Controls\n\nThe builder has no publish permission and no path to the public site except through a human publish action in the normal console flow. Every generated item is marked as generated in the revision history, and every console action is audit-logged. Legal and waiver content is excluded from generation under step 6 above. The Web Administrator shall disable the builder's access to any store it is found writing to incorrectly, and report the event under AI Incident Handling (§4.6.5).\n\n## Records\n\nThis standard produces generated items in the editing flow, their source citations, revision history marking them as generated with the human changes recorded, and the audit log entries for each console action. Generated-content records are retained with the page revision history, permanently.\n\n## Escalation\n\nGenerated content that misstates a price, a term, or a policy is treated as an AI incident under AI Incident Handling (§4.6.5) and reported to support@unsaidmemories.com. Any question whether generated text may lawfully be published goes to legal@unsaidmemories.com before publication.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Web Administrator and the AI Operations function jointly review a sample of published generated content monthly, checking factual accuracy against source records, and review this standard quarterly.\n",
  "payments-and-gateway": "\n# Payments and Gateway\n\nThis standard governs payment method settings in the content management system and the handling of payment credentials. It is used by the Web Administrator, who maintains payment method settings; by the Finance function, which owns payment credentials and their rotation; and by Front Desk staff, who recognise and report payment failures. Payment configuration is split in two, deliberately: credentials are held where only the Company's infrastructure can reach them, and business settings are held where an authorised manager can reach them. The two shall never be mixed. Customer payment obligations are governed by the Payment Terms (§2.1.16); this standard states how the console is operated, and does not restate them.\n\n## Roles and duties\n\n- **Finance function** — owns payment credentials, performs rotations, and confirms a live test transaction after every credential change.\n- **Web Administrator** — maintains which payment methods are active and their configuration, through the gateway settings action only.\n- **Front Desk** — reports payment failures with the booking, the time, and what the customer saw, and never attempts to reconfigure settings for a single declined card.\n\n## The two halves of the configuration\n\nCredentials — secret keys, publishable keys, and webhook signing secrets for the payment processor — are held as protected infrastructure secrets, managed outside the console. They are never stored in the database, never appear in the console, and shall never be pasted into chat, documents, email, or tickets. A credential that appears anywhere outside its protected store shall be treated as compromised and rotated immediately.\n\nBusiness settings live in the payment methods store, maintained through the gateway settings action in the console. Each method is identified by a code (card payment is the current method) and carries an active switch and a configuration record. Activating a method offers it at checkout; deactivating it removes it from checkout. That is the entire settings surface: which methods are offered, and their configuration. Status displays elsewhere in the admin console — connection state, terminal reader status, fee and payout schedules — are readouts for information, not settings to edit.\n\n## What the settings control\n\nThe Web Administrator shall attend to three matters in the configuration:\n\n1. **Active methods.** Only methods the business intends to accept shall be active.\n2. **Deposit behaviour for bookings.** Whether the deposit is captured at booking time or authorised at booking and captured at the appointment. Capture is simpler to operate; authorisation is friendlier to the customer. The choice is the Finance function's, recorded in the configuration.\n3. **Notification health.** Payment notifications from the processor are how the system learns a payment succeeded. If notifications stop arriving, payments sit in pending. After any gateway change, the Finance function shall confirm that money is actually flowing — a completed test transaction and its notification — not merely that the method shows as active.\n\n## Credential rotation\n\nRotation follows this order, and the order is the control:\n\n1. Generate the new credential in the processor's console.\n2. Store the new credential in the protected secret store alongside the old one.\n3. Redeploy the payment functions so they use the new credential.\n4. Run a live test transaction and confirm it succeeds, including its notification.\n5. Only then, revoke the old credential.\n\nRotate first, revoke last. Revoking first breaks every checkout until the new credential is in place. Test and live credentials shall be kept visibly distinct wherever they are handled; the most expensive gateway error is test credentials in production on a busy day.\n\n## Failure modes and first responses\n\nOperators work failures in this order — customer, configuration, processor:\n\n1. **A single customer fails while others succeed.** That is the customer's card, not the gateway. Do not reconfigure anything; assist the customer with another payment method.\n2. **Payments fail across the board after a change.** Check the active switch on the payment method record first; the audit log states who changed it and when. If the method is active but charges fail, the cause is in the credential half — a rotated credential not stored, or functions not redeployed after the store changed.\n3. **Payments stuck in pending.** Notifications are not arriving. Check the notification endpoint configuration and the processor's delivery record before changing anything else.\n4. **Before assuming an internal fault**, check the payment processor's published service status (Stripe, under the Stripe Services Terms (§2.1.41)).\n\n## Controls\n\nGateway settings changes are made only by the Web Administrator role and are audit-logged with the operator and the resulting state. Credential changes are made only by the Finance function, are never recorded with their values in tickets, documents, or the audit log description, and are always verified by a live test transaction before the old credential is revoked.\n\n## Records\n\nThis standard produces payment method records and their configuration, audit log entries for each settings change, and the Finance function's rotation record — date, credential type, and verification result, never the credential value. Rotation records are retained permanently.\n\n## Escalation\n\nA payment outage, a suspected compromised credential, or notifications failing for more than one hour goes to support@unsaidmemories.com immediately and to the Finance function the same day. A charge dispute or legal process concerning payments goes to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Finance function reviews active payment methods and credential age quarterly, and rotates credentials at least annually and immediately upon any suspected exposure.\n",
  "cms-troubleshooting": "\n# CMS Troubleshooting\n\nThis standard gives the diagnostic order for content management problems. It is used by Content Editors and the Web Administrator when a change does not appear or a public page behaves unexpectedly, and by Front Desk staff reporting such problems. Most console problems are one of three things: the wrong store was edited, the store the site reads is empty, or the public site has not yet refreshed. Operators shall work this standard in that order before requesting engineering help.\n\n## Roles and duties\n\n- **Content Editor** — performs the checks below and corrects content in the correct store.\n- **Web Administrator** — checks the audit log and record state, and escalates what this standard does not resolve.\n- **Front Desk** — reports the page, the expected content, and the time the change was saved; does not re-edit content to force a result.\n\n## First check: the wrong-store traps\n\nThree duplicate stores account for most \"I updated it but nothing changed\" reports. Check these first.\n\n1. **Gallery.** The public gallery reads the public photo store. A second gallery store exists but has no public reader; photos placed there never appear. If photos are missing, confirm which store received them and move the work to the public photo store, under Website (§4.5.2).\n2. **Policies.** Public policy pages render from the policy store. Page records have no public rendering for policies. If a policy edit did not appear, the edit was made in the wrong place; repeat it under Legal and Waivers (§4.5.3).\n3. **Reusable block locale.** Reusable blocks are read only under the en-US locale. A block created under any other locale value is invisible. Check the locale before anything else, under Website (§4.5.2).\n\n## Second check: empty stores\n\n1. **Banners.** If no banner displays, confirm a banner record exists at all. Once one exists, check in order: the active switch; the schedule window (a future start means scheduled, a past end means expired — both are the system working as set); placement matching the page being viewed; and a valid banner type, under Website (§4.5.2).\n2. **Navigation.** Navigation entries take effect through the public navigation reader once records exist, filtered by menu and active switch, in sort order. Every entry requires a web address or a linked page, and shall never link to a page that is not live.\n\n## Third check: the refresh delay\n\nThe public site displays stored default content on first load and refreshes as changes publish. After saving, allow a few minutes before treating an absent change as a failure. If the change still has not appeared, verify in order: the record actually saved (check the store, not recollection of clicking save); the correct store was edited (first check, above); and whether the refresh mechanism failed. Console actions return explicit errors — \"page not found,\" \"image is required,\" \"either address or page is required.\" Read the error instead of retrying without changes.\n\n## Content-specific failures\n\n**Page not publishing.** Check the publication state (a page that is not live is invisible by definition); then any scheduled publication time (a future time means waiting, not failure — clear it to publish now); then the page address (two pages sharing one address means the site may serve the other); then the revision history, which shows whether the save landed. For policy pages, see the wrong-store traps.\n\n**Pricing rule not applying.** Check the surcharge record: inactive switch (an inactive rule does not apply); a restriction to a service the booking does not include; the automatic-application switch off, meaning the surcharge waits for staff to add it manually — the state of both current weekend surcharges; and whether the amount is a flat fee or a percentage. Batch pricing edits are audit-logged; check the log to see whether the batch ran and what it changed. Duplicate active weekend surcharges shall be consolidated under Services and Pricing (§4.5.1).\n\n**Service invisible.** For the booking catalog, check in order: the online-booking switch, the active switch, and the location record for the customer's location. For public services content, check the visible switch and the display order. The two stores do not update each other; see Services and Pricing (§4.5.1).\n\n**Booking flow broken.** Isolate the failing step. Service selection: the catalog checks above. Date and time: blocked times on the calendar, or a service duration that does not fit the open gap — a 60-minute service cannot book into a 45-minute opening. Deposit payment: a gateway problem, worked under Payments and Gateway (§4.5.5). Confirmation: confirm whether the appointment record exists before treating the flow as failed; a booking saved without a confirmation message is a messaging fault, not a booking fault.\n\n**Gateway errors.** Work in the order customer, configuration, processor, under Payments and Gateway (§4.5.5). Record every gateway error with what happened, when, and for which booking; a kept log turns a recurring mystery into a visible pattern.\n\n## The audit trail\n\nEvery console action — page edits, banners, gallery, navigation, reusable blocks, search metadata, waivers, gateway settings, promotions, and pricing and service changes — is recorded in the audit log with the action, the record, the operator, the network address, and the resulting state. When something changed and no one reports changing it, when a batch edit appears not to have run, and when a price moved overnight, the audit log is the first diagnostic, not the last resort.\n\n## Controls\n\nTroubleshooting shall not be performed by deleting and recreating records to force a result; deletion destroys the history the audit trail and revision records exist to preserve. Corrections are made in the correct store, and the incorrect record is deactivated or corrected, with a change note stating the reason.\n\n## Records\n\nThis standard produces corrected records with change notes, audit log entries for each corrective action, and gateway error logs kept by Front Desk. A fault that recurs three times shall be written up as a short incident note — symptom, cause found, correction made — and kept with the console records.\n\n## Escalation\n\nA fault this standard does not resolve within one business day, or any fault affecting checkout or booking, goes to support@unsaidmemories.com with the incident note. A fault that has published incorrect legal or pricing content to customers goes also to legal@unsaidmemories.com the same day.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Web Administrator reviews recurring-fault notes monthly and amends this standard where a new failure pattern has been established.\n",
  "ai-operations-overview": "\n# AI Operations Overview\n\nThis standard describes what the AI Operations function operates and the duties that attach to operating it. It is used by the AI Operations Lead, who owns the Company's AI services day to day; by AI Operators, who monitor and maintain them; by the Clinical Reviewer, a licensed veterinarian who reviews triage quality and safety; and by Support staff, who receive customer reports about AI behaviour. The Company's AI services are the Company's own systems: models the Company operates on its own data, reached through the Company's own gateway. No part of these services is provided by an outside AI vendor, and no customer request is sent to an external AI service.\n\n## Roles and duties\n\n- **AI Operations Lead** — owns the model, prompt, evaluation, incident, and cost standards in this section; approves every model or prompt activation.\n- **AI Operator** — monitors invocation records, error rates, and latency; performs the checks in AI Troubleshooting (§4.6.8); files incidents.\n- **Clinical Reviewer** — reviews triage outputs for urgency accuracy and the no-diagnosis boundary; approves any change that affects triage behaviour.\n- **Support** — routes customer reports about AI answers into AI Incident Handling (§4.6.5) with the session identified.\n\n## The services operated\n\nThe Company operates two customer-facing AI services, both served through the Company's gateway and both recorded in the same invocation log:\n\n1. **Kinnect AI**, the pet-care triage and shopping assistant on the storefront. A customer describes symptoms or asks about products; the service classifies urgency, answers general care questions, recommends products from the Company's own catalog, and directs emergencies to a veterinarian. Kinnect AI is the only customer-facing AI with clinical-safety consequences, and it is operated under the Tele-Triage Terms (§2.1.24) and the Teletriage Disclosure Policy (§2.5.24). Kinnect AI provides general information and urgency guidance only; it does not diagnose and does not treat.\n2. **The classroom tutor**, inside the Company's learning portal. It answers course questions from the Company's course materials. Its errors confuse a learner; they do not endanger an animal, and its review cadence reflects that difference.\n\n## How a Kinnect AI request is handled\n\nOperators shall understand the handling order, because each stage has its own failure behaviour:\n\n1. **Abuse control.** Requests are limited to 20 per 60 seconds per network address. The limit is abuse control, not a quota, and exceeding it returns a plain-language message asking the customer to slow down.\n2. **Input limits.** A message is capped at 1,500 characters; conversation history is the last 8 turns at 1,200 characters each. Pet context is attached from the customer's own records where the customer is signed in; a general mode suppresses pet context for a shopper buying for someone else.\n3. **Triage.** The request passes to the Company's triage service, which returns an urgency classification (INFO, ROUTINE, URGENT, or EMERGENCY), any red flags, whether the customer must be directed to a veterinarian, and a triage record identifier. Triage is a hard dependency for safety classification. If the triage service does not answer, the conversation may continue but no urgency classification has occurred, and operators shall treat that state as a safety-relevant failure under AI Troubleshooting (§4.6.8).\n4. **Reply.** The reply is prepared under the governing prompt in Prompt Library and Versioning (§4.6.3): brief, practical, never a diagnosis, with the fixed list of emergency signs — trouble breathing, collapse, seizures, poisoning, bloat, repeated vomiting, blood, inability to urinate — always answered with direction to contact an emergency veterinarian immediately.\n5. **Product selection.** Where the shopper would benefit from products, a selection step produces a short search query of 2 to 5 words, which is run against the Company's own product search index. Empty or invalid results are discarded. Emergencies skip this stage entirely: no product cards are shown on an EMERGENCY.\n6. **Grounding.** Before it is sent, the reply is checked against the products actually returned, and may name only those products. This is the control that keeps the assistant from presenting a product the Company does not sell as if it were Company fact.\n7. **Recording.** Signed-in customers' turns are stored as chat sessions and messages carrying urgency, red flags, products shown, and the triage identifier. Guest conversations are not stored. Every gateway call — including failures — is written to the invocation log with its feature, usage counts where the service reports them, latency, and a result of success, error, or timeout.\n\nThe classroom tutor applies the same pattern at lower stakes: messages capped at 4,000 characters, the last 16 messages of history, and answers drawn from the Company's course materials, recorded in the same invocation log.\n\n## Human oversight duties\n\nNo AI output reaches a customer outside the oversight this section specifies:\n\n1. Models and prompts reach customers only after evaluation and human approval, under Model Inventory and Lifecycle (§4.6.2), Prompt Library and Versioning (§4.6.3), and Evaluations (§4.6.4).\n2. The Clinical Reviewer samples live triage sessions weekly and reviews every session classified EMERGENCY or URGENT, checking urgency accuracy, red-flag handling, and the no-diagnosis boundary.\n3. Any output reported as wrong, unsafe, or misleading is investigated under AI Incident Handling (§4.6.5), and the service may be restricted or suspended by the AI Operations Lead at any time.\n\n## Data duties\n\nCustomer data is used by these services only as the Privacy Policy (§2.1.2) permits. Pet and conversation data attached to a request is limited to what the answer requires. Customer data shall not be used to improve a model except as that Policy permits, and evaluation datasets drawn from real sessions are reviewed and corrected by a human before use, under Evaluations (§4.6.4).\n\n## Controls\n\nThe invocation log is the shared operational record for both services, and it is kept complete: a logging failure shall never be allowed to interrupt a customer answer, which means a missing log record does not prove a call did not happen. Operators shall reason about absence accordingly in every investigation. Access to operate these services is limited to the roles named above. Disclosure duties for these services are governed by AI Disclosures and Agent Registration (§4.6.7) and the AI Disclosure Policy (§2.1.38).\n\n## Records\n\nThis standard relies on and produces: invocation log records, triage session records, chat session and message records, the model registry, the prompt library, evaluation records, incident records, and cost records, each kept under its own standard in this section.\n\n## Escalation\n\nOperational failures go to support@unsaidmemories.com. A suspected clinical-safety failure — a missed emergency or diagnostic language served to a customer — goes to the AI Operations Lead and the Clinical Reviewer immediately, and is filed the same day under AI Incident Handling (§4.6.5). Legal process or a regulator's enquiry concerning these services goes to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe AI Operations Lead reviews this overview, the service inventory, and oversight sampling results monthly, and the Clinical Reviewer reviews triage safety sampling weekly.\n",
  "model-inventory-and-lifecycle": "\n# Model Inventory and Lifecycle\n\nThis standard governs the register of AI models the Company operates and the lifecycle each model follows from registration to retirement. It is used by the AI Operations Lead, who owns the register and approves activations; by AI Operators, who keep the register accurate against what is actually serving; and by the Clinical Reviewer, whose approval is required for any change to a model that classifies triage urgency. Every model that serves a customer is a model the Company operates on its own infrastructure and its own data, reached through the Company's own gateway. A model that is not in the register shall not serve traffic.\n\n## Roles and duties\n\n- **AI Operations Lead** — owns the register; approves registration, activation, and retirement.\n- **AI Operator** — verifies the register against serving behaviour, including the model identifier recorded on triage sessions; reports any mismatch the same day.\n- **Clinical Reviewer** — approves or refuses any model change affecting Kinnect AI triage, on the evaluation evidence.\n\n## The register\n\nThe model register is the authoritative list. Each entry records the model's human name, its exact model reference (the identifier the gateway uses for it, and the key by which invocation records are joined to the register), its version, its status, and its serving configuration — temperature, output limits, timeout values, and any fallback order. Invocation records resolve to a register entry by that reference; an invocation that cannot be resolved is a register defect and shall be corrected.\n\nStatus follows one path: registered, then in evaluation, then active, then retired. Exactly one entry per serving position shall be active at a time. A model entry that ever served production shall never be deleted; retirement is its final state, because evaluation and incident records join to it, and deleting it destroys the ability to answer, months later, which model produced a given answer.\n\n## Serving positions\n\nThe register covers, at minimum, one entry for each serving position: triage classification for Kinnect AI; the Kinnect AI conversational reply, product selection, and grounding steps, including any fallback order among Company models for those steps; and the classroom tutor. Where a fallback order exists, it is recorded in the configuration: the primary Company model serves first, and a further Company model serves only if the primary errors or returns empty content. A fallback that returns incorrect content with a successful result is not caught by the fallback order; it is caught by Evaluations (§4.6.4), which is why evaluation gates activation.\n\n## Changing a model\n\nA model change for Kinnect AI is treated with the gravity of a clinical-safety change. The procedure is:\n\n1. **Register.** Enter the candidate in the register with status registered, and record why the change is proposed — quality, capacity, or retirement of the current model.\n2. **Evaluate.** Run the candidate, with the production prompts, against the evaluation suite under Evaluations (§4.6.4). For triage, the comparison that decides is urgency accuracy on the reviewed session dataset and the safety checks. A less costly model that misses one more emergency is not less costly.\n3. **Assess cost.** Compare the candidate's expected usage profile per feature against the incumbent's, using the invocation records under Cost Tracking and Budgets (§4.6.6), before activation.\n4. **Activate.** On the AI Operations Lead's approval — and the Clinical Reviewer's, for triage — set the candidate active and the incumbent retired in the same action, then set the serving configuration to match. The register and the serving configuration shall never disagree overnight.\n5. **Watch.** For the first hours after activation, an AI Operator monitors invocation records for error rate by feature and latency, and the Clinical Reviewer reads a sample of triage sessions for urgency drift.\n\n## Credentials\n\nThe gateway credential that reaches these models is a Company credential, held in the protected secret store, never in the register, never in a document, and never in chat or tickets. Rotation follows the order in Payments and Gateway (§4.5.5): store the new credential, redeploy, verify with a live request, then revoke the old. A sudden wave of authentication failures with no configuration change is the signature of a rotation performed out of order, and is worked under AI Troubleshooting (§4.6.8).\n\n## Controls\n\nNo model serves traffic unless its register entry is active. The AI Operator shall check weekly that the model identifier recorded on new triage sessions matches an active register entry; a value that matches no entry means the serving configuration changed without the register changing, and is filed as an incident under AI Incident Handling (§4.6.5). Register changes are recorded with the operator, the time, and the reason.\n\n## Records\n\nThis standard produces register entries and their status history, change proposals with their reasons, evaluation references for each activation, and post-activation watch notes. Register history is retained permanently.\n\n## Escalation\n\nA serving model that cannot be identified in the register, or a triage model change proposed without evaluation evidence, goes to the AI Operations Lead immediately and to support@unsaidmemories.com the same day. Questions about the lawful use of training or evaluation data go to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe AI Operations Lead reviews the register quarterly against what is actually serving, and reviews each active model's evaluation standing at the same time.\n",
  "prompt-library-and-versioning": "\n# Prompt Library and Versioning\n\nThis standard governs the library of instructions — prompts — that direct the Company's AI services, and how a prompt version is created, tested, activated, and withdrawn. It is used by the AI Operations Lead, who approves activation; by AI Operators, who prepare and test new versions; and by the Clinical Reviewer, who approves any version affecting Kinnect AI's triage or safety wording. A prompt is behaviour: a one-line change can alter what thousands of customers are told. The library exists so that the exact words serving at any moment are known, tested before they serve, and reversible in seconds.\n\n## Roles and duties\n\n- **AI Operations Lead** — approves activation and withdrawal of every prompt version.\n- **AI Operator** — prepares candidate versions, runs evaluations, and records results.\n- **Clinical Reviewer** — reviews the safety wording of every Kinnect AI prompt version and the hard cases described below, before activation.\n\n## The prompts in service\n\nThe library holds one active version per feature. The Kinnect AI features and the rules each active prompt carries are:\n\n1. **Conversational reply.** The assistant is Kinnect AI, the shopping and pet-care helper for UnSaid Memories in Memphis, giving brief, practical answers. It is not a veterinarian. It shall never diagnose. On any of the fixed emergency signs — trouble breathing, collapse, seizures, poisoning, bloat, repeated vomiting, blood, inability to urinate — it shall direct the customer to contact an emergency veterinarian immediately. It shall never invent, assume, or embellish pet names, breeds, weights, or details; it may refer only to pets explicitly on file, and where none are on file it names no specific pet. It shall never mention suppliers, distributors, or internal sourcing. It shall never claim it cannot do something the site does.\n2. **Product selection.** The step answers in a fixed structured form only: whether the shopper would benefit from a product, a search query of 2 to 5 plain words a store search would match (including dog or cat where known), and a short reason. A malformed answer produces no products for that turn rather than an error shown to the customer. This step is the least costly call in the flow and the one most able to be wrong quietly — an inaccurate query returns irrelevant products, not a failure signal — which is why prompt changes to it still require evaluation.\n3. **Grounding rewrite.** The preliminary reply is rewritten to under 110 words against the products actually returned: every safety point is kept; no product, brand, or product line may be invented; only products in the returned list may be named, and only where relevant; where none were returned, the product type is described and nothing is named; pet details shall not be invented; the no-diagnosis boundary is kept. This step is the second line of defence — the answering step is constrained a second time by the real product list.\n4. **Triage.** The triage prompt directs urgency classification and returns the urgency level, red flags, whether the customer must be directed to a veterinarian, and the reply text. Because it carries the highest stakes on the platform, it is held to the same library, evaluation, and approval rules as every other prompt, and its current active version shall always be retrievable from the library in full.\n\nOperators reading a prompt shall read its prohibitions first. Every prohibition in the active Kinnect AI prompts records a past failure or near-miss that was corrected with a sentence; removing a prohibition because its reason is not obvious is prohibited.\n\nThe classroom tutor's prompts are held in the same library under the same versioning rules, at the classroom's lower risk level.\n\n## Library rules\n\n1. One record per version. The version number increments; a superseded version is never edited. The text of a version that served traffic is fixed in practice: a change is made by creating the next version, never by altering the serving one. Evaluation and incident records refer to a version, and they must always resolve to the exact words that served.\n2. Each record states its feature — conversational reply, product selection, grounding rewrite, triage, or classroom tutor — which is the key joining the version to invocation records.\n3. Status follows: prepared, then in evaluation, then active, then withdrawn. One active version per feature. Activation and withdrawal of the predecessor happen in the same action, so there is never doubt about what is serving.\n\n## Testing a prompt change\n\nBoth halves are required before activation:\n\n1. **Evaluation.** The candidate runs against the standard dataset under Evaluations (§4.6.4) and shall meet or beat the active version on every safety measure: no-diagnosis compliance, urgency accuracy on the reviewed triage sessions, escalation correctness, and grounding fidelity (every named product exists in the returned list). Better prose with any safety regression is refused, without exception.\n2. **Adversarial reading by a human.** The Clinical Reviewer and an AI Operator put the hard cases to the candidate and read the answers: a bloated dog described casually; a male cat straining to urinate mentioned as an aside; a shopper asking whether a product is safe for a puppy where the product is age-restricted. The evaluation measures what it was built to measure; the human reading exists to catch what no one thought to measure.\n\n## Activation and rollback\n\nA new version is prepared, moves to evaluation while testing runs, and becomes active only on the AI Operations Lead's approval after evaluation passes and human reading is complete. Rollback is a status change, not a deployment: withdraw the new version and reactivate the previous one. That speed is the safety argument for the library — a prompt regression in clinical classification is reversed before the next customer finishes typing. Customer data used in testing is limited to what the Privacy Policy (§2.1.2) permits, and test cases drawn from real sessions are used only in reviewed, corrected dataset form under Evaluations (§4.6.4).\n\n## Controls\n\nNo prompt is edited outside the library process. The active version for each feature shall be identifiable at any moment from the library alone. Any discrepancy between the library's active version and the words actually serving is an incident under AI Incident Handling (§4.6.5).\n\n## Records\n\nThis standard produces prompt version records, evaluation results for each candidate, adversarial-reading notes, and activation and withdrawal records with approver and time. Version records are retained permanently.\n\n## Escalation\n\nA prompt change proposed for urgent safety reasons may be fast-tracked by the AI Operations Lead with the Clinical Reviewer, with evaluation completed retrospectively within one business day; the fast-track and its reason are recorded. Other questions go to support@unsaidmemories.com; questions about the clinical boundary in prompt wording go to the Clinical Reviewer, and legal questions about disclosure wording to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe AI Operations Lead reviews the active versions and library completeness monthly, and the Clinical Reviewer re-reads the active Kinnect AI prompts quarterly.\n",
  "evaluations": "\n# Evaluations\n\nThis standard governs how the Company's AI services are measured against known cases before a model or prompt version is allowed to serve customers, and how those measurements are recorded. It is used by AI Operators, who run evaluations; by the AI Operations Lead, who applies the release gate; and by the Clinical Reviewer, who owns the triage dataset's labels and grades the safety checks. An evaluation is a measured check against known cases, recorded so the result can be reproduced and compared over time. No model or prompt version is activated without one.\n\n## Roles and duties\n\n- **AI Operator** — assembles each run, executes it against the frozen dataset, and records the result with its per-case detail.\n- **Clinical Reviewer** — labels and corrects the triage dataset, grades emergency and diagnosis checks, and dispositions every safety failure.\n- **AI Operations Lead** — applies the gate: no activation where a required run is missing, stale, or failing on a safety check.\n\n## The unit under test and the record\n\nThe unit under test is the pair of model and prompt version together, because the same model with a different prompt is operationally a different system. Each run is recorded with the model, the prompt version, the evaluation name, the dataset reference, a numeric score, a result, and the per-case detail: which cases failed and what the grader observed. The score alone is never sufficient — a high score concealing two missed emergencies is worse than a lower score failing only on phrasing — so decisions are made from the per-case detail, not the headline number.\n\n## The triage dataset\n\nThe starting dataset is the Company's own record of real triage sessions: the customer's own words, the urgency assigned, the recommendation given, the knowledge documents consulted, and the red flags recorded. Operators shall observe the following:\n\n1. The urgency labels in the session record are the service's own calls, not verified truth. Before the dataset may be used as a measure, the Clinical Reviewer reviews every case and marks each label correct or corrects it. The review starts with the EMERGENCY and URGENT cases — the minority classes are the ones that matter.\n2. After review, the dataset is frozen. If cases are added or labels change, the dataset version increments, and every run records which dataset version it used. Scores from different dataset versions are not comparable.\n3. New production sessions are reviewed and appended on the review cadence below; the increment is recorded in the same way.\n4. Session material is used only as the Privacy Policy (§2.1.2) permits, and cases are handled as clinical records for access purposes.\n\n## The checks that matter\n\nFor Kinnect AI triage, in order of harm:\n\n1. **No-diagnosis compliance.** The output shall never state or imply a diagnosis. Any occurrence of diagnosis language is a failure. This check carries the Company's clinical position: Kinnect AI gives general information and urgency guidance under the Tele-Triage Terms (§2.1.24); it does not diagnose or treat.\n2. **Emergency escalation accuracy.** Every true EMERGENCY case shall be returned as EMERGENCY or with direction to a veterinarian. A missed emergency is the worst output the service can produce and outweighs every other measure.\n3. **Urgency consistency.** The four levels — INFO, ROUTINE, URGENT, EMERGENCY — shall be applied consistently. Over-triage is a lesser failure than under-triage but is still a failure, because it trains customers to disregard the service.\n4. **Red-flag surfacing.** Cases with known red flags shall surface them rather than soften them in friendly wording.\n5. **Response form.** The triage response shall arrive in the expected structured form. A malformed response forces the conversation onto an unclassified path, which is visible in the invocation records.\n6. **Grounding fidelity.** Every product named in a final reply shall appear in the product list returned for that turn.\n7. **Selection relevance.** For the product selection step, the query produced shall retrieve products a human judge rates relevant. Relevance is graded by humans; it is graded anyway, because an inaccurate query is a silent quality failure with no error signal.\n\nFor the classroom tutor, the checks are answer correctness against the Company's course materials, no invented citations, and correct refusal where a question falls outside the course. The stakes are lower and the cadence may be lower.\n\n## Running an evaluation\n\nThe Operator runs the model-and-prompt pair against every case in the frozen dataset, grades the outputs, and records one evaluation record per check set. Grading methods, in order of preference for safety-critical checks: human rubric review for emergencies and diagnosis language; a stronger Company model as judge, with human spot-checks of samples, for phrasing and relevance; and fixed assertions for mechanical checks (structured form parses; urgency is one of the four levels; named products are within the returned list). Runs are executed against the Company's own serving configuration — same gateway, same output limits, same knowledge access — because a run against a different configuration proves nothing about production.\n\n## Reading results and gating releases\n\nThe per-case detail is read, not the score. Every failure receives a disposition before the version may serve: corrected by the change, accepted with a written reason recorded in the run, or escalated to a change in human workflow. A safety regression — any missed emergency, any diagnosis language — blocks activation regardless of improvement elsewhere. The gate is applied by the AI Operations Lead, who confirms the required runs exist, are newer than the candidate's creation, and pass, before any status changes under Model Inventory and Lifecycle (§4.6.2) or Prompt Library and Versioning (§4.6.3).\n\nA failing evaluation shall never be corrected by editing the test until it passes. The evaluation is the standard. Changing a check, a threshold, or a dataset label is a policy decision, made by the AI Operations Lead with the Clinical Reviewer for safety checks, and recorded in writing with its reason.\n\n## Controls\n\nDataset labels are changed only by the Clinical Reviewer. Evaluation records are not edited after a run; a corrected run is a new record referring to the earlier one. A version activated without a passing run is an incident under AI Incident Handling (§4.6.5).\n\n## Records\n\nThis standard produces the reviewed and frozen datasets with their version history, evaluation run records with per-case detail, failure dispositions, and gate decisions. Evaluation records are retained permanently; they are the evidence that a version was fit to serve.\n\n## Escalation\n\nPressure to activate a version over a failing safety check goes to the AI Operations Lead, whose refusal is final at the operational level, and is recorded. Disagreement about the clinical boundary goes to the Clinical Reviewer; a dispute with legal consequences goes to legal@unsaidmemories.com. Operational questions go to support@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Clinical Reviewer reviews new triage sessions into the dataset monthly, and the AI Operations Lead reviews check thresholds and dataset coverage quarterly.\n",
  "ai-incident-handling": "\n# AI Incident Handling\n\nThis standard governs how failures of the Company's AI services are recorded, investigated, corrected, and reported. It is used by every AI Operator and Support staff member who notices a failure, by the AI Operations Lead, who owns each incident to resolution, and by the Clinical Reviewer, who owns any incident touching animal care. The threshold for filing is deliberately low: anything an AI service did wrong, or nearly did wrong, that is worth recording is an incident. The cost of an unnecessary record is nothing; the cost of a pattern nobody recorded is a systemic failure discovered by a customer.\n\n## Roles and duties\n\n- **Any operator or Support staff member** — files the incident immediately, while the evidence is fresh.\n- **AI Operations Lead** — assigns the owner, sets severity, and confirms the verified correction before closure.\n- **Clinical Reviewer** — investigates every incident touching urgency, diagnosis language, or animal outcome, and decides the clinical remediation.\n- **Incident owner** — investigates, records the failure classification, and verifies the correction against the original case.\n\n## Severity\n\nSeverity measures harm, not embarrassment:\n\n- **Critical** — a missed emergency; diagnosis language served to a customer; any case where an animal's outcome may have been affected.\n- **High** — an urgency misclassification short of critical; a grounding failure on a care-related turn; a sustained failure of the triage path.\n- **Medium** — irrelevant product selection; phrasing problems; transient serving errors.\n- **Low** — cosmetic faults; a single transient failure with a clean fallback.\n\nWhere two levels are arguable, the higher is taken. Severity may be lowered after investigation; a critical incident filed as medium does not receive the immediate response it needed, and that error is not recoverable.\n\n## Where the evidence lives\n\nThe investigator works from the session outward:\n\n1. **Triage session records** — the fullest record: what the customer wrote, the urgency concluded, the recommendation given, the model that served, the knowledge documents consulted, and the red flags recorded. Reconstruct the incident here first: what was said, what was concluded, and where the two diverged.\n2. **Chat message records** — the turns as the customer saw them, with urgency, red flags, the products shown, and the link to the triage record. This is where the investigator checks whether the grounding step constrained the final reply and whether the products shown matched the selection step.\n3. **Invocation records** — the serving record: feature, model, usage counts, latency, and result. This is where \"the model answered badly\" is separated from \"the model never answered.\" An error result with no usage counts and very low latency is a gateway failure — nothing was computed; a successful result with a bad answer is a quality failure. The two have entirely different corrections, and this record is the only place that separates them cleanly.\n\nThe investigator then classifies the failure, because the correction depends on it:\n\n- **Reasoning failure** — the knowledge consulted was sound and the conclusion was wrong. Correction lies in the prompt or the model.\n- **Knowledge failure** — the documents consulted were wrong or incomplete, or the wrong documents were reached. Correction lies in the Company's knowledge base or the way it is searched.\n- **Serving failure** — no answer, or the fallback answered. Correction lies in the gateway, the triage service, or timeouts, under AI Troubleshooting (§4.6.8).\n- **Selection or grounding failure** — the answer was sound and the products were wrong. Correction lies in the selection prompt or the product search path.\n\nThe classification is recorded in the incident. The next investigator shall not have to repeat the work.\n\n## Filing and working the incident\n\n1. File the incident record immediately: model (the model reference from the triage or invocation record where the register join is unavailable), severity, status opened, a title written for a future reader, and a description carrying the session, message, and invocation identifiers.\n2. Where the incident touches care — wrong urgency, missed emergency, any animal-outcome question — file the corresponding telemedicine incident record as well, with the reporter type, the session, and an honest statement of whether the record contains protected health information, because that statement governs who may handle the record from that point on. Clinical incident duties to customers and regulators are governed by the Tele-Triage Terms (§2.1.24) and the Teletriage Disclosure Policy (§2.5.24).\n3. Work the record as a sequence — opened, investigating, remediating, resolved — with a named owner at every stage. Corrections follow the classification. A prompt failure is corrected by a new prompt version under Prompt Library and Versioning (§4.6.3), with the incident referenced in the change record; a model failure is tested under Evaluations (§4.6.4) and, where the model is at fault, changed under Model Inventory and Lifecycle (§4.6.2); a knowledge failure is corrected in the knowledge base and the affected evaluations re-run; a serving failure is worked under AI Troubleshooting (§4.6.8), with a note on whether the fallback behaved as intended.\n4. The incident stays open until the correction is verified against the original failing case — the actual case, not a paraphrase — and the verification is recorded. An incident closed without a verified correction is an incident waiting to recur.\n\n## The reporting loop\n\nCritical incidents are reviewed immediately. All other incidents are reviewed in a regular session where patterns are the point: three medium urgency failures in a month is a systemic finding even where each looked minor alone, so incidents are aggregated by failure classification as well as by severity. External reporting follows the Company's regulatory obligations and its veterinary partners' requirements; where reporting is arguable, the Company reports. The record exists so the Company can answer, at any time: what went wrong, what was done, and what changed so it does not happen again.\n\nOne asymmetry governs every investigation: the invocation logger is built never to interrupt an answer, so a logging failure is silent. A missing invocation record for a session that plainly involved a gateway call means the logging failed; it does not mean the call did not happen. A present record with an error result is positive proof of the failure and its timing. Trust presence; treat absence as ambiguous.\n\n## Controls\n\nNo incident record is deleted or rewritten after filing; corrections are appended. Access to incident records that contain protected health information is limited to the roles named in this standard. The AI Operations Lead may restrict or suspend an AI feature at any point during an investigation, and shall do so for any critical incident whose cause is not yet understood.\n\n## Records\n\nThis standard produces incident records with severity, classification, owner, and verification, telemedicine incident records where care is touched, and periodic pattern reviews. Incident records are retained permanently.\n\n## Escalation\n\nA critical incident goes to the AI Operations Lead and the Clinical Reviewer immediately, and to support@unsaidmemories.com the same day. An incident that may require notification to a regulator, a partner, or an affected customer goes to legal@unsaidmemories.com the same day it is identified.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nIncidents are reviewed weekly by the AI Operations Lead and the Clinical Reviewer, and pattern reviews are conducted monthly.\n",
  "cost-tracking-and-budgets": "\n# Cost Tracking and Budgets\n\nThis standard governs how the operating cost of the Company's AI services is measured, reviewed, and kept within budget. It is used by AI Operators, who run the cost checks; by the AI Operations Lead, who owns the budget and the response to a spike; and by the Finance function, which reconciles the measured cost monthly. Because the Company operates its own models through its own gateway, the invocation log is the complete meter: every call the services make is recorded there, with its usage, and cost control is a matter of reading that meter correctly and acting on it in the right order.\n\n## Roles and duties\n\n- **AI Operator** — runs the scheduled checks below and reports breaches of the alert thresholds the same day.\n- **AI Operations Lead** — investigates every threshold breach, decides the response, and records what was found.\n- **Finance function** — reconciles invocation-based cost against the operating cost of the AI infrastructure monthly.\n\n## What the meter records\n\nEach invocation record carries the feature, the model, the prompt version, input and output usage counts, the computed cost where cost has been calculated, latency, the result, and supporting detail. Two reading rules govern everything in this standard:\n\n1. **Failed calls are not usage.** An error record with no usage counts and very low latency records an attempt in which nothing was computed. Cost shall be computed only on successful records with recorded usage. Summing cost across all records without that distinction produces a wrong number.\n2. **Latency and usage are independent signals.** A slow call with small usage is a slow service, not a long prompt; a fast error is a fast failure. A latency spike shall not send an investigator into the prompts when the usage counts say the request was small.\n\n## The usage profile of each call\n\nOperators shall know the normal profile of each feature, because drift from it is the earliest cost signal. On the Kinnect AI path, per customer turn: triage is the largest input (the instruction, the customer's message, knowledge material, and history); the product selection step is the cheapest call and the highest-volume one (observed at about 170 input and 20 output units per call, against its 120-unit output cap); the grounding rewrite costs more per call (observed at about 265 input and 103 output units, against its 260-unit output cap); and the conversational reply (400-unit output cap) runs only where triage has not already produced the reply. The classroom tutor is metered in the same log on the same basis, so one set of checks covers both services.\n\n## The standing checks\n\nUntil these checks are presented in a dashboard, they are run as queries against the invocation log on the cadence stated:\n\n1. **Usage by feature, per day.** Successful records grouped by feature, with output usage as the standing proxy for cost until per-call cost is computed for every record — output usage is the larger price component in the Company's cost model.\n2. **Error rate by feature.** The share of records that are not successful, over a rolling 24 hours. A sustained share above a few percent on any feature is investigated the same day.\n3. **Latency distribution by feature.** The middle and 95th-percentile latency of successful records. The observed spread on the selection step is wide relative to its small output; that spread is service variance, and it is what the customer-experience budget must absorb.\n4. **Usage drift.** Average input and output usage per feature per day. A prompt change that lengthens outputs appears here before it appears in total cost. Output caps bound the worst case per call; average drift is what moves the monthly figure.\n5. **Seller attribution.** Where sellers use AI features, usage grouped by seller. A seller at ten times the median is either receiving exceptional value or running an integration out of control; the record distinguishes the two before the cost does.\n\n## Budgets and alert thresholds\n\nThe following thresholds are procedure, applied by the AI Operator on a daily check:\n\n1. **Daily AI operating cost above three times its 7-day trailing average** — investigate before noon. Causes, in the order they are checked: a prompt change lengthening outputs (usage drift); knowledge material returning more context (input usage); a traffic increase (record counts — the per-address abuse limit caps anonymous misuse, not genuine growth); a seller integration out of control (seller outliers).\n2. **Any feature's error share above 5% over one hour** — check the gateway first, then the credential: a wave of authentication failures is the signature of a rotation performed out of order, under Model Inventory and Lifecycle (§4.6.2).\n3. **The first response to a spike is measurement, not restriction.** A feature is restricted only once the cause is known and recorded. The abuse limit is an abuse control, not a cost tool, and tightening it punishes legitimate customers first.\n\nWhen alerting is automated, each alert shall record its threshold, window, scope (feature, seller, or platform), who is notified, and the action — notify, restrict, or record only — and the thresholds above are the definitions it implements. Thresholds actually used shall be written down as they are adopted, so later automation inherits recorded judgement rather than inventing its own.\n\n## Controls\n\nCost figures quoted internally shall state their basis — computed cost, or output usage as proxy — and the period. No operator shall adjust usage or cost records to meet a budget figure. Budget changes are made by the AI Operations Lead with the Finance function and recorded with their reason.\n\n## Records\n\nThis standard produces the standing check results, threshold-breach investigations with their findings, monthly reconciliations, and the threshold register. Reconciliations and investigation records are retained for seven years with the Finance function's records.\n\n## Escalation\n\nA threshold breach that remains unexplained after one business day, or a single-day cost above five times the trailing average, goes to the AI Operations Lead and to support@unsaidmemories.com the same day. Suspected misuse of a seller integration is handled under AI Disclosures and Agent Registration (§4.6.7) and, where legal process is involved, legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe standing checks run daily; usage drift and seller attribution are reviewed weekly; budget and thresholds are reviewed monthly with the Finance function.\n",
  "ai-disclosures-and-agent-registration": "\n# AI Disclosures and Agent Registration\n\nThis standard governs how the Company discloses its use of AI to the people who rely on it, and how automated agents — the Company's and sellers' — are registered before they act on the Platform. It is used by the AI Operations Lead, who maintains the disclosure list; by Product and Support staff, who present disclosures in the services customers and sellers use; and by the Seller Operations function, which registers seller agents and records seller acknowledgements. The governing duties are stated in the Automated Access and Agent Disclosure (§2.7.11) and the AI Disclosure Policy (§2.1.38); this standard states how they are carried out, and does not restate them differently.\n\n## Roles and duties\n\n- **AI Operations Lead** — maintains the authoritative disclosure list and confirms every AI feature appears on it before the feature serves customers or sellers.\n- **Seller Operations** — registers seller agents, verifies enumerated scopes, records acknowledgements, and operates revocation.\n- **Support** — answers \"where do you use AI and what do you tell people\" enquiries from the disclosure list only, and routes gaps it finds to the AI Operations Lead.\n\n## The disclosure list\n\nThe disclosure list is the single authoritative answer to where the Company uses AI and what it tells people. Each entry states the feature, what it discloses, where the person sees it, and when the wording last changed. The AI Operations Lead shall maintain the list under the same change control as policy, because it is policy made visible. No AI feature serves customers or sellers until its entry exists. The Kinnect AI features — conversational reply, product selection, grounding rewrite, and triage — and the classroom tutor are the standing entries, and the disclosures they carry in the product are:\n\n1. **Identity.** The assistant presents as Kinnect AI, the pet-care helper. It does not present as a veterinarian or as a human staff member, and its governing instructions state its identity and its limits in the same breath.\n2. **Limits.** Every triage-shaped conversation carries the no-diagnosis boundary and the emergency direction: the fixed list of emergency signs — trouble breathing, collapse, seizures, poisoning, bloat, repeated vomiting, blood, inability to urinate — is answered with direction to contact an emergency veterinarian immediately. The customer is told what the service cannot do, not only what it can, in accordance with the Teletriage Disclosure Policy (§2.5.24).\n3. **Accuracy of product statements.** The grounding step exists so the assistant never names a product the Company does not sell. Presenting generated output as Company fact where it is not is a disclosure failure, and is filed under AI Incident Handling (§4.6.5).\n\n## Seller AI agents: registration before operation\n\nAn unregistered agent acting for a seller is indistinguishable from fraud tooling, and the standard is applied accordingly, under the Automated Access and Agent Disclosure (§2.7.11):\n\n1. **Registration before operation.** No AI agent acts on the Platform — listing tools, pricing agents, buyer-messaging tools — until it is registered and approved. Registration states the operator, the agent's capabilities, its scope (which resources, and which sellers or buyers it acts for), the data it touches, and its level of decision autonomy.\n2. **Enumerated scopes, least privilege.** Anything not enumerated at registration is denied. An agent approved for listing content does not receive pricing. An agent acting for a seller shall not exceed what that seller authorised.\n3. **Revocable within 60 seconds.** Every registration carries a revocation mechanism that the Company, the operator, or the affected seller or buyer can trigger. Stale or unused registrations are revoked on review. Revocation is a protective act, not a penalty; repeated revocations are reviewed under the enforcement provisions of the governing policy.\n4. **Identification as automated.** A buyer-facing agent identifies itself as automated at the start of every interaction. Impersonation of a human is prohibited without exception.\n\nRegistration is a written record held by Seller Operations where it can be produced in an incident. The absence of a registration system entry is not a defence: a seller deploying an agent notifies the Company in writing what the agent is, what it touches, and who operates it, before it acts, and Seller Operations keeps that record.\n\n## Seller acknowledgements\n\nThe acknowledgement record — seller, feature, time acknowledged — is the audit trail of who was told what, and when. Before any seller-facing AI feature is enabled for a seller:\n\n1. Present the disclosure text at the point where the seller first uses the AI feature, not buried in onboarding.\n2. Require explicit acknowledgement before the feature activates, and record it: seller, feature, timestamp.\n3. Acknowledgement is per seller and per feature. Acknowledgement for listing assistance is not acknowledgement for pricing automation.\n4. Check the record before enabling the feature. No record for that seller and feature means the feature stays off. This is a gate, not a reminder.\n5. Where disclosure wording changes materially, present it again and record a new acknowledgement. The history of what each seller acknowledged, and when, is the evidence of informed agreement.\n\n## Automated connections to the Platform\n\nEvery connection by which an automated system outside the Company's own services invokes Platform functions or reads Platform data is registered with its scope, its credentials, and its owner — or it does not operate. The Company's own AI services are reached only through the Company's own gateway under Model Inventory and Lifecycle (§4.6.2); a credential that cannot be rotated on demand is a connection the Company does not control, and is treated as a defect until corrected.\n\n## Controls\n\nThe AI Operations Lead shall confirm, before any AI feature launches or changes its disclosure wording, that the disclosure list entry, the in-product disclosure, and the governing policy agree. Seller Operations shall verify quarterly that every operating seller agent has a current registration matching its actual scope, and revoke registrations that do not.\n\n## Records\n\nThis standard produces the disclosure list with its change history, agent registration records, revocation records, and seller acknowledgement records. Acknowledgement and registration records are retained permanently.\n\n## Escalation\n\nAn unregistered agent found operating on the Platform is revoked immediately and reported to support@unsaidmemories.com the same day. A disclosure failure served to customers is filed under AI Incident Handling (§4.6.5). Questions about the Company's disclosure duties to regulators or partners go to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe disclosure list is reviewed monthly by the AI Operations Lead, registrations quarterly by Seller Operations, and both are reviewed whenever a new AI feature is proposed.\n",
  "ai-troubleshooting": "\n# AI Troubleshooting\n\nThis standard gives the diagnostic order for failures of the Company's AI services. It is used by AI Operators investigating a fault, by Support staff handling customer reports about the assistant, and by the AI Operations Lead deciding whether a fault is also an incident under AI Incident Handling (§4.6.5). Every failure mode below is taken from the services' actual handling paths as described in AI Operations Overview (§4.6.1). Operators shall work the diagnostic order at the end of this standard, in sequence, rather than beginning with the component they suspect.\n\n## Roles and duties\n\n- **AI Operator** — performs the diagnosis, applies the corrections within operator authority, and records the finding.\n- **Support** — reports the customer's exact experience, the time, and whether products, urgency guidance, or the whole reply were affected.\n- **AI Operations Lead** — owns faults this standard does not resolve and decides on restriction or suspension of a feature.\n\n## The time budget\n\nA Kinnect AI turn runs inside a total budget of 45 seconds. Inside that budget sit the triage call (up to 30 seconds) and the subsequent gateway calls for the reply, product selection, and grounding steps. Operators shall understand the arithmetic: a slow triage followed by slow gateway calls can exceed the total budget, and when the budget is reached the turn ends wherever it stands and the customer sees a general failure. Slowness anywhere in the chain therefore converts to total failure at the budget line, not to graceful delay. The diagnostic signature is a partial record: a selection record with no grounding record after it, or a triage attempt with nothing after it, in a turn the customer reports as \"the chat died.\" The correction is to bring the inner timeouts down so the budget adds up — the 30-second triage allowance is the loosest figure in the chain and the first candidate — not to raise the total budget without that analysis.\n\n## Gateway failures\n\nThe Company's gateway is the single path to the Company's models. Its failure handling, in order:\n\n1. **Authentication failure.** The gateway credential is wrong or revoked. A wave of these failures with no configuration change is the rotation signature: the old credential was revoked before the change carrying the new one took effect. Correction follows the rotation order — store the new credential, redeploy, verify, then revoke the old — under Model Inventory and Lifecycle (§4.6.2).\n2. **Throttling by the gateway.** Distinct from the Platform's own customer-facing limit of 20 requests per 60 seconds per network address: gateway throttling appears as error records in the invocation log, while the Platform's own limit never reaches the gateway and leaves no invocation record. Operators shall establish which limit produced a \"slow down\" report before acting; tightening the Platform limit does nothing for gateway throttling.\n3. **Successful result with empty content.** Treated as a failure, and the next Company model in the recorded fallback order serves. A burst of these on the primary model, with clean successes, means the primary is degrading quietly and the fallback is silently carrying — and costing — the traffic. The model reference on the successful records shows which model actually served.\n4. **Timeouts.** Classified by their error signature in the invocation records. A timeout pattern on one model while another is healthy is a model-side fault; on all models, a gateway or network fault.\n\nEvery gateway failure degrades the turn step by step, not all at once: no selection step means no products; no grounding means the ungrounded reply requires incident review where the turn was care-related; no reply step means the customer is told the assistant could not be reached. A single turn may succeed at one step and fail at the next, and the stored message does not show which; the invocation records do.\n\n## Triage service failures\n\nWhere the triage service does not answer, answers too slowly, or returns an error, the conversation continues on the conversational path, with these consequences, which the operator shall treat as the dangerous degradation in this system: no urgency classification has occurred; the stored message carries no triage identifier; and the customer receives an answer that looks normal while the safety step is absent. Operators shall check for this state whenever chat traffic continues while new triage session records stop accumulating. Answers that change character with no prompt or model change recorded are checked first against the triage service's model setting, observable in the model identifier on new triage sessions, and against the register under Model Inventory and Lifecycle (§4.6.2).\n\n## Product path failures\n\nProducts are drawn from the Company's own product search index. Where the index is unavailable or slow, the turn shows no products and the conversation otherwise works: the shopping half of the service disappears silently. Where \"the assistant stopped recommending products\" is the report, the operator shall check the search index before the model — the selection step may be producing sound queries into a failing index. The assistant drawer and the recommendations surface share this dependency, so simultaneous failure across product surfaces is one index fault, not several model faults.\n\n## The abuse limit's limits\n\nThe 20-per-60-seconds-per-address limit is held in process memory. Operators shall know its three consequences: restarts clear all counters, so a burst timed to a restart passes through; multiple running instances each enforce their own limit, so the effective limit multiplies; and the limit keeps no record — a customer reporting \"it told me to slow down\" leaves no trace to query, and the message on the customer's screen is the only record. The limit is a tripwire, not a quota system. If misuse becomes sustained rather than theoretical, the AI Operations Lead shall move the limit to shared storage with recording, as a change under AI Operations Overview (§4.6.1).\n\n## The logger's silence\n\nThe invocation logger never interrupts an answer, by design, and logging failures are therefore silent. A missing invocation record is ambiguous: the call happened and logging failed, or the call never happened; the table cannot distinguish the two. What can be trusted: a present record with an error result is positive proof of the failure and its time; a record with no model join means the register has no matching entry, which is a register defect under Model Inventory and Lifecycle (§4.6.2); no usage counts on an error record means nothing was computed and no cost arose; no usage counts shall be read as zero usage where the record is incomplete.\n\n## Classroom tutor failures\n\nThe tutor's serving model is selected by configuration. Where the tutor changes behaviour or stops answering, the operator shall check the configuration first: a changed or missing configuration value can switch which Company model serves, and the two possibilities behave differently. Tutor errors reach the learner as a general failure message; the underlying status is in the invocation records for the tutor features.\n\n## The diagnostic order\n\n1. **Invocation records, last hour, by feature and result.** An error concentration on one feature points to that step. Errors on all features point to the gateway or the credential. No records at all during reported failures means the request never reached the gateway — the abuse limit, input limits, or the time budget above.\n2. **The feature's handling path.** Read the actual handling for that step: its timeout, its fallback, and what occurs on empty content, under AI Operations Overview (§4.6.1).\n3. **The gateway and credential.** Service state, credential validity, fallback-order behaviour.\n4. **The data.** Triage session records for classification quality; the product search index for relevance; the knowledge base documents — where a care protocol is wrong, correct the document, not the prompt.\n5. **The registers.** Model and prompt records identify exactly what served the failing turn.\n\nAnd the closing rule, carried from AI Incident Handling (§4.6.5): a correction is verified against the original failing case, not a paraphrase. The fault is closed when the correction is proven, not when it is shipped.\n\n## Controls\n\nDiagnosis shall not be performed by altering prompts or models speculatively; one change at a time, recorded, so the record shows what corrected the fault. Every fault affecting triage classification is also filed as an incident, whatever its cause.\n\n## Records\n\nThis standard produces diagnostic notes attached to the fault or incident record: the invocation pattern found, the step at fault, the correction applied, and the verification against the original case.\n\n## Escalation\n\nA fault unresolved within one business day, any fault affecting triage classification, and any suspected credential compromise go to the AI Operations Lead and to support@unsaidmemories.com the same day. Legal process arising from an AI failure goes to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe AI Operations Lead reviews fault patterns monthly and amends this standard where a new failure pattern has been established.\n"
};
